      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>GWVOIR MMO UGA t, I AIS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOU R THE EXAMINER, FRI DAY, AUGUST 31 1900</head>
        <p>GWVOIR MMO UGA t, I AIS PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOU R THE EXAMINER, FRI DAY, AUGUST 31 1900</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The Duchess of York when staying with Lord and , Lady Barnard, at Raby Castle, paid a visit to the great colliery near Bishop Auckland. To those who have never seen a coal mine the working of a great collicry is of great interest, and, alter watching the proceedings above ground, Her Royal Highness expressed a wish to descend the shaft. It is, c,t course, underground, that one sees the real wonders of a coal mine, but this was evidently not included in the day's programme and the directors wt-ze unable to take the responsibility cf allow- ing the Duchess to go down the pit. There are however, several members of the Royal Family, including the Prince and Princess of Wales, who have teen down a coal mine, and no one who has been down in the cage can ever forget the shock, the feeling of having one's heart in one's mouth as the walls of the pit fly upwards. Neither do they forget the joyful sense of'relief with which they regain the light and sun- snine of the day. The Duke and Duchess of Sutherland are, as usual, at Dunrobin, for the autumn, this being perhaps the most famous of Scotland's historic castles. The Duchess is not only known as a writer, and for her practical help to Pottery workers, but also for the great interest she takes in the Highland cottage industries, and when at Dunrobin, she frequently visits the aheilings, eiving the inmates the benefit of her advice, guidance, and encouragement. It is largely due to this assistance that the Harris and Sutherland tweeds, and other home-spun goods, have become noted for the excellence of their cclourings and designs, as well as for their unsurpassed durability. This work which has been systematically encouraged by the Duchess of Sutherland has now assumed con- siderable proportions, and affords employment to some six or eight thousand people, principally on the Duke's estate, but also scattered as far West as Lewis, and as far North as the Shetlands.</p>
        <p>Many .mothers have no doubt been advised by doctors not to let their children paddle when out of sorts, but according to a medical writer in the " Queen "it is possible to carry this caution too far. There is nothing inherently risky about paddling, and the terrible results that are supposed to proceed from it are described as phantoms. "The truth is," says this medical practi- tioner. " that a child whose digestive habits are veil looked after will naturally revel in the many delights of paddling, and will never come to any harm:' If anything would convince one that there is no pre- ventive for sea-sickness, it would be the list of twenty- eight alleged remedies recently published in a French newsp&amp;per. "Lie down," says one direction. " Walk about," says another. "Be gay" suggests a third,which seems only another way of saying " don't be sick." " Do nothing at all." is almost as useful a suggestion as the one " don't go on the sea." "Carry a lemon in each band" says one experienced traveller. " Have a bag of sea-salt about you" recOmmends another. All these suggestions are seriously meant, thought they say very little for the intelligence of their authors. There is unfortunately no cure for sea-sickness, that is to be recommended. There are drugs which will stop it, but they have a very harmful effect, and should be avoided at all costs ! There is after all a good deal of truth in the sai!ors slying, that a sea trip never does you so much good unless you are sea-sick, though it is of course, difficult to appreciate this at the time. The Wild Birds Protection Act has always lo.ten very much of a dead letter, and it is rendered more difficult of enforcement by different regulations in varicus parts of the country. That beautiful songster, the lark is protected all the year round in the county of London, but the birds are frequently taken and sold as coming from unprotected counties. In a prosecution instituted the other day by the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, the defendant who had been selling larks in the market, brought a WitQeE9 to prove that tho birds were caught in Cambridgeshire, where tiv.:y are only protected up to the first of August. It ia therefor!, quite easy for people to evade the law, if they are so minded, and what seems to be needed is more uniform action all over the country, if we aro to protect the fast diminishing number of our native songsters. So far, few birds of any kind appear in our autumn headgear. Brightly coloured ribton:ehaux, and a quill or two seems tn be the favoured trimming of the moment preference being shown for felts in soft shades of tan and fawn. Strings have departed, to be no more seen till the summer of 1901 is 'with us.</p>
        <p>Coats and skirts in friers serge, and tweed, are well to the fore just now. The latter are built to clear the ground, and with a box pleat or gathers at the back, the coats, usually of tie smart " pilot" variety—being adorned with the regulation silver or enamelled buttons. The bigh "storm" collar is at present attached only to travelling cloaßs of the stoutest order,and there is some reason for hope that commonsense bas at lenth pre- vailed, and that we have abandoned this very foolish fashion.</p>
        <p>For some years past perfumes of all kinds have been tabootd by vntlewomen, and, despite all that is said to the contrary, this state of affairs still obtains. Say.het powders however are in great request, being scattered in great profusion in trunks, wardrobes, and linen cheats. Sachets of orris root and lavender, impart t, ones clothes a faint sweet, indefinable odour, and many society women nowadays pia their faith to this particu- lar mixture.</p>
        <p>Orris root, which comes almost exclusively from the neighbourhoods of Florence, and Verona, in Italy, has recently been raised to enormous prices by the opera- tions of a monopolist syndicate. There will soon be nothing left in which the trade will not be manipulated by some monopolist ring or combination, but unlike coal, and other necessaries, people can at least do with- out the orris root, if it is made excessively dear. There are many peculiar features in the production of orris root, and the fact that the plant will only flourish in a few districts in Italy, makes it easy for the capital- ists to secure a monopoly of its cultivation. The plant produces exquisite flowers with a delicate fragrince.but it is reared for the root alone, and for this purpose it is left to grow for five years, when it dies. It is then taken up, and the bark peeled. but the strange thing is that it has no cdour. It is put aside for two yeers, during which time some chemical fermentation gces on, which transforms the stems into those delicately scented fragments which we know as orris root. The Kitehoi Committee of the House of Commons de- scribe all the beverages sold at that establishment as " wines." This is no doubt done for the purpose of abbreviating matters,in the rendering of accounts, but it reminds one of a system of classifying humanity which is sometimes adopted by the house agents. Some time ago a lady was inquiring about a house, in London, and asked the ai,ent what fort of neighbourhood it was in. This, he replied, was thoroughly desirable as the people were, without exception, "soup and fish" families. BUTTERED EGGS —Beat well three eggs, and place them in a saucepan with one and half ounces of butter. Stir all the time until quite hot, but not boiling. Kav,. ready six slices of toast, phce a portion of the egg on each,and serve. A little bloater or anchovy paste spread on the toast gives a nice flavour.</p>
        <p>" MADGE*"</p>
        <p>Brass Band Contest at Bootle.—Success of Warrington and Widnes Bands _ _ _</p>
        <p>A brass band contest was held on Saturday after- noon in the North Recreation Ground, Stanley road, Bootle, with the worthy object of assisting the Bootle War and Indian Famine Funds. Although the weather was not very fine, there was a large attendance of the public. A strong committee made all the necessary arrangements in connection with the contest, the secretarial duties being ably discharged by Mr W Pickles, who was assisted by Mr J Fidler. Money prizes were offered to the ex"- tent of £3O. No less than nineteen baoda entered for the contest, but three of them did not play. The test piece selected was "Songs of England" (Wright, and Round) and Mr J 0 Shepherd, the musical direc- tor at the Royal Court Theatre, Liverpool, was the adjudicator. At the close of the contest, which lasted till after nine o'clock, Mr. Shepherd gave his decision as follows :—ist prize (Xl0), Haigh ; 2nd (£7), Widnes Subscription ; 3rd (£5), Warrington Borough; 4th (£4),AshtonTemperance; sth (.£2 108). Lower Ince Temperance. There was also a grand march contest,the firi,t prize (11.), being awarded to Ashton Temperance Band, and the second (I0s), to the Coppull zlubscription Band. The other bands which took pait in the competition were Lither- land Silver, Tranmere Gleam,Bootle Borough, Long- ridge St. Lawrence's, Ilindley Public, Platt Bridge, Whit-lane Primitive Methocist, Garswood Hall, St, Luke's, Crosby, and Christ Church, Waterloo, The adjudicator, after awarding the prizes, said that there had been a great improvement in the playing since the last baud contest at Eastharn. Some of the bands gave excellent performances, but none of them were perfect.</p>
