        <p>PHILOSOPHERS arc simply people who don't want what they can't get. WANTED TO KNOW.—Whether it is probable that the "funny•bone" is supplied by the vein of humour."</p>
        <p>ExTßacr from a diary kept by a little bor. "December 1.--My birthday. Had a December 2.—Had a sturnmer-cake."</p>
        <p>" Ea—l want some sort of a present for a young lady." " Sweetheart or sister ?'' "Er—why— she hasn't said which she will be yet."</p>
        <p>WHY is a selfish man like the letter "P"? Be- cause he is the first tk) pity and the last to help:_</p>
        <p>"'Do you suppose scorching is the cause of all these bicycle faces we see?" " Shucks, no! In nine cases out of ten it's the instalment plan. WHAT is the difference between a husbandman and a seamstress ?—The one gathers what he sows, the other sews ..vhat she gathers. "DON'T you think every man ought to have some poetry in his soul !" "Yes," replied the weary editor ; " that's the place for most poetry." A BOY in a Sunday-school, when asked the ques- tion, "What is the chief end of man ?" "The chief end is the end with the head on !"</p>
        <p>NEIGHBOLTP, : WhAt'S the matter. Where yer going?" Jinks : "Burglars Going fcr a police- man." Neighbour: "Did yer leave your wife alone?" Jinks : "Se, she's holding the burglar." UNPLEASANTLY REM INDF.D. Jones : "Chilly day, isn't it?" Brown : "Yes, I forgot my over- coat this morning, e.nd r have been trying to forget it ever since."</p>
        <p>A BOOKSELLER'S assistant was greatly startled recently by a customer's inquiring if he could be supplied with an "epidemic [academic) dic- tionary. . _</p>
        <p>AN'up-to-dato grammarian says that, although a girl may be intelligent enough to know that " kiss " is a noun, she is frequently unable to de- cline it.</p>
        <p>" Doc-ron, why is it that people are generally so much more pleased with 13°T-babies than with girls?" "Nothing simpler, madam. A boy-baby never comes amiss."</p>
        <p>JUSTICE : " You are charged with stealing Colonel Julep's chickens. Have you any wit- nesses?" [lacle Moses : " I heb not. I don't steal chickens befo' witnesses."</p>
        <p>Miss TRAvis : "Johnny, I have caught you with a fishing rod over your shoulder. I'll tell your father. Where is ho?" Johnny: "Down at the end of the garden finding some worms." NOT PERFUMERI7.. 3 ridge t (surreptitiously smel- ling a bottle of lavendar salts) : " OA, quick ! Cut MI me nose before it efficts th' rist av me body."</p>
        <p>CLIMAX REVERSED, ETYMOLOGICALLY.-01)00- lan : "Fitzgerald says he's descinded from some of the greatest houses in Ireland." Flannigan : "Sure so he did many's the toime—on a laddher." HUSBAND: "Really I didn't notice the dresses Mrs. Brown, though, wore her gown en traine." Wife: "It's a wonder you noticed that much." Husband: "Couldn't help it—l stepped on it." GAVE HIMSELF AWAY. Mrs. Bloobumper : "'How did the detectives happen to suspect him, disguised as he was in women's clothes ?" Bloo- bumper : "He passed a milliner's store window without looking in." MISTRESS: "Bridget, you've broken as much china this month as your wages amount to. Now how can we prevent this occurring again?" Britet "01 don't know, mum, unless yez raises me wages." IN a Parisian omnibus an ill-bred male passenger made a grimace when a very stout old lady got in. "Hippopotamus !" he ezclaimed under his breath, but so loudly ;that the old lady heard him. "Sir," she said, "you know an omnibus is like Noah's Ark; it takes in all the animals—even donkeys." GEORGE, DEAR: "I drew all my money out of the savings bank to-day and went shopping with it. I spent every penny." "But, my dear," protested George, "the understanding was that our savings were put by for a rainy day." " Precisely ; and my dear boy will re- member that to-day was one of the rainiest days we have had for several months."</p>
        <p>Someone within earshot of an Irishman happened toy that Shakespeare died on the day of his birth. "By the Mother of Ireland," said he, "Shakespeare was the man for a good day's work, thin ! A janius that could turn out Hamlet and all the rest of it, complayte, in an ephemeral twenty-four hours deserved to live afterward at his also for iver and iver !"</p>
        <p>AN EVEN THING. —" Yes," said the minister; "stormy weather keeps almost as many people away from church as fine weather." "What do you mean?" "Why, in fine weather they go out on their bicycles,''</p>
        <p>A COLONIAL SECRETARY once remarked to a member of the House of Commons, who was notoriously lazy and vacillating, "My dear boy, observe the postage-stamp; its usefulness and value depend upon its ability to stick to one thing till it gets there." MISTRESS: "Bridget, some of the bed-clothing s missing. Was any of it left out-of-doors over- night?" Bessie, aged five': "Bridget doesn't know anything about it, mamma. I know where it is. Papa's got it." "What do you mean, child?" "I heard same of the folk next door sayin' this morning they saw papa the other day with three sheets in the wind."</p>
        <p>Sin: "Dear, please tell me how to spell costume. I'm writing to mother about my lovely gown." He: "Well, are you ready?" She: " Yes." He: " C-o-s-t, cost"— She : "Yes." He : "T -u, to"— She: " 'Well?" He : me—ninety-six shillings, as yet unpaid." She: "You're a wretch."</p>
        <p>HORRIBLE SYMPTOMS. —Mr. Ruskin Morris: "Why, Penelope, what is the matter ?" Mrs. Rus- kin Morris: " Oh, Horace, I am afraid Emerson has not that elevated mind we have a right to ex- pect in a child of ours. He just said 'dada" like that common washerwoman's baby I heard in the park !" THE HAUNTED MAN.—Again that ringing in his ears! It was the warning he had dreaded. He knew his time had come. Yet, although he had started at thc sound, he seemed half-dazed and wholly careless of the consequences. But still the ringing in his cars! " Drat it !" he finally said; and, springing from his bed, the careworn suburbanite shut off the alarm clock and proceeded to dress for the 8.10 train. LITTLE NELLIE, while at the seaside with her mother, was very fond of making calls upon some ladies in a neighbouring cottage, and the frequency with which she would bring home little presents of cards, pictures and other things, led her mother to fear that she might be in the,habit of asking for them. She there:nre gave the child strict orders never to ask for anything. The very next day Nellie returned from her visit laden with pretty odds and ends of various sorts' and, when her mother called her and questioned her severly as to whether she had asked for them, with conspicuous virtue she replied, "No mother. You told me I must never ask for things, E 0 I just took them !" EXTREME COURTESY. —I Suppose you have often heard et pre-occupied people apologising to cloth- ing-store dummies, after havin,ei accidentally brushed against them," remarked Urbanby, with a chuckle. " I've done it myself, darn'ern !" re- plied Harkins, with a scowl at the recollection. " Well, my wife is'nt satisfied to let it go at that," continued Urbanisy ; "she pushes her politeness over into the ridiculous." "How so ?" asked Harkins, with intense curiosity. "Why, this morning she was bent on getting me a necktie that I would wear, and stopped to look at some in Hastitsteins and Dazzlevitch's window," explained Urbanby, in a glow of glee. "As she spotted one she was sure would suit, and started to rush into the store to secure it before some one else bought it, she banged square into one of the dummies. Of course, without thinking what she was doing, she -apologised. Then, however, she realised that it was nothing but a dummy • and, in her confusion over her mistake, she tried to square things by saying fervently, Oh, I beg your pardon; I thought you were a man !' " THE MOST NUTRITiOUS.</p>
      </div>
