      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>C C</head>
        <p>C C</p>
        <p>BREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>• LADIES' LETTER:</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, Summer millinery becomes shabby with strange rapidity during August, as if aware that the moment of its departure is shortly due. Then, arrive delightful early editions of what stormy autumn will make a necessity. They begin and end with the toque in various forms, for the period of the picture hat is not yet. It rests until the calmer days of winter approach. A round turban, called by its old name, the pork-pie, is one of the newest hats, and made of tartan panne possesses a remarkable air of smartness. It is trimmed with enormous pompons. To clean sailor hats first brush off all dust. Then dissolve a piece of gum arabic about the size of a •,,ery small nut in three tablespoonfuls of cold water. The best plan is to put this soaking overnight. Brush the hat well over with this solution, being careful th•tt the brush penetrates to every part. Seeing that the hats can be bought new at such a low figure it is questionable whether they are worth all this trouble, for even at its best a cleaned bat nev loos as well as a new one.</p>
        <p>I have just been shown an elegant gown of satin. faced foulard in a bright cherry colour with white spots. It has a double skirt, mounted on a foundation of gros-grain silk, and edged with creamy guipure lace insertions, which are run with rows of narrow black ribbon velvet. The skirt is slightly gathered over the hips and at the back, and the bodice is slightly draped over to the left side, and shows a pointed vest of lace and square revers, run with black velvet to match the skirt. The sleeves, which are slightly draped, reach only to the elbows, and the waistband is of cream silk, with jewelled ornaments at the sides. The toque worn with this is made of draped red silk, with a large rosette of narrow black ribbon velvet in front. This gown might be made of crepe de Chine, voile, or any other soft clinging material with good effect.</p>
        <p>Those who are travelling can do very well with the gown they travel in, a better one for any special occasion which may turn up, and a couple of elegant blouses with a suitable skirt (not any old makeshift of a skirt, which stamps "tourist" all over its wearer) for table d'hôte; a bright summer silk, or voile if still fresh, may be utilised again for this last purpose. It is necessary to add that low dress is never worn by people of good taste when dining in a restaurant of a hotel abroad. A foulard frock is admirable to take on ,one's travels, for it submits to a good deal of packing and crushing without resenting such treatment, and emerges at the other end fresh as ever. Such a dress would be pretty and serviceable in a dark blue and white figured foulard, the skirt and bodice inlet with irregular designs in ecru lace, mounted over a very pale mauve silk foundation, the sleeves turned back below the elbow to show under pagoda sleeves of the lace, to match the transparent lace collar. The American custom of carrying a satchel round the waist is one largely adopted now by most women, and proves a great comfort for the bestowal of all sorts of trifles. The guardsman's cloak—a long coat to the heels, with military collar, and a band at the waist—is one of the smartest novelties of the season.</p>
        <p>Muslin and chiffon bridal dresses do not yet out- number the orthodox white satins, though they are, as a rule, very much more graceful and becoming. The lighter materials are gradually winning their way to the front, and perhaps the brides of Eng- land will one day wonder at the choice of their predecessors who could prefer so stiff and ungainly a fabric to the softer and more poetic muslins and crepe-de-Chines. White satin is ideal when it is at its best; but that means a very costly dress. Cheap white satin has several odious faults. The prettiest bridal dress seen for some time was made of very fine India muslin, tucked and gauged in every direction, and trimmed on bodice and sleeves with fine old Mechlin lace insertion. Fichus are more in fashion than ever. Most of their effect depends upon the manner in which they are arranged, and it is not every one who possesses the gift of putting them on well. A very °heap one, gracefully disposed, far sur- passes an expensive one hum, round the wearer anyhow. A little study of the art is well worth while. All doubt ends now as to the future of the sleeve. The accepted pattern is not a tightly fitting one. Misguided makers may mould it to the arm of its wearer with torturing fidelity, but that way smartness lies no longer. A graceful looseness is to be its dominant feature. The prettiest sleeve for little mid-season evening frocks and tea-gowns • is called the cloud, a soft semi-transparent swathery of gauze or crepe de Chine set into the arm-hole with fine gaugings, like those used for the bonne femme skirt. Every kind of fancy is permissible When sleeves are concerned.</p>
        <p>Your loving, ' JENNIE.</p>
        <p>THE</p>
