      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>YELLOW TERROR. [By Mr. H. Whitham ]</head>
        <p>THE YELLOW TERROR. [By Mr. H. Whitham ]</p>
        <p>It is quite impossible for me to send readers of the "Examiner" any news, not that there is any scarcity of the article. Although thousands of miles stand between writer and reader yet the same swift current conveys to us all alike the same appal- ling information of the horrible doings of the unspeakable Chinese. I think it one of the greatest misfortunes of the end of the 19th century that the Governments of the two English speaking nations cannot, or do not, see a little nearer eye to eye than they do at present. I cannot, at toe moment, think of any particular time, or of any special incident in Vie history of the Boer War whereby the British Government could have made it plain to the American Government thatinot only their material interests were identical, but, that the moral phase of the question called for a united effort in preventing the war. I have never been among those who hive thought that war with the Boers could be entered into with a light heart, or being entered into cOuld be brought to a speedy finish,My position has been the reverse of this. Yet I would say with Polonius, "Beware of entrance to a quarrel; but, being in, bear't that the opposed may beware of thee." So it happens that I have look'd not favourably upon the "stop the war," party. Had the English speaking people been united upon the South African question,and, also, had the American politician been a politician in the true sense of the word, and not been led astray by the rant of the Irish demagogue, and, again had there been a_thorough historical comprehension of the meaning of the two words Republic and Monarchy, I am confident the happenings in China we are now -reading of would have been spared to us. We, in San Francisco know the Chinese pretty well. When it is known that right in the centre of the city we have a China town with a population of near upon 20,900, and, that these celestials do all our pork trade, and nearly all our vegetable and laundry trade, it will be seen at once that we rub shoulders with the pig-tail pretty frequently. To-night as I rode home in the train things were rather lively. The evening papers were being passed around. Men's feelings were wound up to the highest pitch. What man could sit quietly and read of the horrors being enacted in China? A British general ordering his own men to be shot to save-ithem from barbaric torture. Of white men gathering their dear ones together to meet death</p>
        <p>from their own hands rather than meet horrors worse than death at the hands of these yellow barbarians? It is quite possible ere these lines are in print matters may have assumed a more pacific aspect, _ _ and, such</p>
        <p>the -tilled__ forces may</p>
        <p>have- used</p>
        <p>judicious yet striking arguments as may lead to peace, though I scarcely think it likely. The whole question has a wider significance than I have as yet seen given to it in the papers. Even should it come to an end,the _end will only be an apparent one. The</p>
        <p>real issue will be, and the-struggleli now right on, between the Slav and the Anglo. Saxon, which of these two races is to be the dominating power of . .</p>
        <p>the twentieth century. The Russian is bound to make headway in China. Some few years ago Eng- land and English influence stood supreme in China; to-day, Russia is almost in a position to defy Great Britain. There is something more than mere trade interests at stake in the struggle now pending. lam not sure if it would not be to England's interest to take a lesson from the attitude America is taking in the struggle. America demands an open door, and no • nation to have any special privileges. She demands also twit the Chinese Empire shall not be divided among the nations. Will England say the same,and be prepared to stand by America in her demands? If not, what may the results be? Simply this, that the dividing up of China will liberate and flood the western world with such an amount of cheap labour as will satisfy all who defend what we know as " irresponsible competition." There seems to be a desire to allow japan a free hand in China. If this is done the result will be that the peculiarities of the two nations will draw them together to resist what the people of the two nations will look upon as the invasion of a c)1131310/1 enemy, and this will be the opportunity of Russia, and will not be neglected, and in spite of all ethnological divisions a Slav race, With its strain of Mongolian blood, will stand Side by side with the little brown man of Japan and the yellow terror of China: all Mongolians. This is what the western world is n -m=facing. Does Itussia ask for an open door in china'? Not so. Aussie, the dominant power in China would mean the closing of her markets to the western world. It is a consummation to be devoutly wished for. The day is fest approaching when, in any case, the markets of the Orient will be supplied by the mills of the Orient. Don't for a moment suppose that the immense quantity of machinery, that has left the shores of Europe and America is lying idle in the Oriental towns and cities, or that the people of these towns are not apt as imitators. Those who have dwelt among them know better. What has England to fight for in China? I should say simply nothing. I care nothing for her export figures. The simple fact is •.iust this, that • England may fight in China and may be success- ful, and when all is over, after blood and money • have towed like water, she may stand on one side and say to all he world," We close no door, go in and enjoy the land." Neither France, Germany, Russia. or any other country adopt this magnanim- ous policy, nor can England afford to do it any longer. England's policy in China has been a scandalous one. No one can read the history of the opium question in China, and England's share fin it, with- out feeling that a day of retribution must come. I urn quite satisfied that an invasion by the Jap and Chinamen of Europe's agricultural districts is immin- ent. It will be called in to supply the demand for Cheaper labour, and to fill the places of those who are, all over Europe, flocking into the towns. Don't for a moment think this is wild talk. Let us see what has happened in California since I came here twelve years ago. The firet fourth of July parade I saw was made Op largely of members of trades unionsa,nd friendly societies. A splendid show could he made up of these two features alone. Had any Chinese Put in an appearance their welcome would have been too warm for the pigtail's com- fort. As for the Jap he had scarcely arrived. Now the Jap swarms here like the yellow fellow and the last fourth of July parade had neither trade unionists, nor friendly societies to swell its ranks, nothing but Chinese, soldiers and policemen. There is scarcely a town or village throughout, the States that has not its China town firmly planted ill its midst. Of course axclusion laws without end are on the statute books, and blood has been shed Pretty freely,but all to no use; the Chinaman comes all the same. Within the past two or three years the feeling has grown steadily that "John "is not the only curse that is surely crowding the white Man to the wall. Since the war between China and Japan the latter nation has come to think itself one of the great powers. The bay of San Francisco is seldom clear of its war-shipesand rarely a week goes by,without one having the opportunity of judging, from groups of her sailor boys, as to their brawn UM muscle. They are short in etature,but seem cut out for work. Of the two, the American workman Prefers the Chinaman to the Jap. There seems to be an opinion abroad that because the Chinese do not seem to organize in their struggle to keep Ceina for the Chinese therefore, they know nothing of or- . Ranization.lf the allied governments and their armies Ro on this assumption they will make a terrible Mistake. There is not, on this earth, a people who</p>
