      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>better understand what organiz Ition anfl boycotting means,and how to carry the wtoie business through with scientific accuracy than do the Clunese. If they decide on a strike, woe b 3 to the Chinese that dares oppose that decision, the knife or t,e bullet will soon give an object lesson to others who may wiTh to do likewise. It is in the use of boycott they shine most. A sign on the door or shop is sufficient, until that is rumoved there is no further trouble with "John." It is one of the most difficult things imaginable to describe the Chinese. It is im- possible to name any feature in their lives,methods, or institutions that do not carry out the most laugh- able and ludicrous contradictions. One may read of their cowardice,and ut,ter lack of a knowledge f truth, and yet have a certain feeling that they are brave, and are utterly indifferent about life, while anyone who has dealings in Chinatown will vouch for the honoar and truth of the Chinese tradesmen.</p>
        <p>The Chinese are gamblers to the very core of them. Their gambling dens attract numbers of white men. I have never yet heard of a dispute on money matters. It is rare that any trouble occurs between the two races of white, and yellow. When any trouble occurs in Chinatown it can generally be traced to their unions. They understand as well as any white man the desirability of netting as much money as possible for their work, and to gain their end they are, all the time, all things to all men. You can always convert a Chinese when he is made to see it will pay him, and if he is sure of profit one Chinaman is willing to carry all the contra- dictions that all the Scots and denominations in Europe and America can pile on him. Yet with all his agreeableness and desire to be accommodating he prefers the Roman Catholic religion. It meets all the curious twists in his build, and never hampers his actions from a business point of view. The Cainaman as a rule:remains a Chinaman,and he is quite justified in trying to keep the " white devils "out of China. If he would only be con- sistent and stay in his own fhwery land I for one should be willing to say close the door and let them stew in their own juice. The Jap is a different fellow altogether, and I sup- pose this accounts for his sudden jump to the front. While not so apt at imitation as his yellow brother the little brown man seems to put the little he has to more practical use, or it may be he is less cun- ning. The Jap is a puzzle. He will work, every time, for less than the Chinese. If it is possible he is more menial, and yet it is strange how soon he gets into the white man's cloth es,dons the cuffs,and tall collar, and sports the tan shoes. The Jap is certain to prove the greatest scourge Europe and America has yet seen. He knows no limitations in working hours, and no sum is too small a pay- ment. With all this he is a great student, and the old book sellers say they are among their best cus- tomers for educational work.</p>
        <p>These two races, the brown and yellow, are,unless lam much deceived, going to play an important, maybe a tragic part on the twentieth century stage. I thought to say more mbout the Russian. An editorial in the "Examiner " recentlN made a slight reference to the history of the Russian sect of Doukhoborsti, or spirit-wrestlers. The history of this sect dates back at least two centuries, and is one of the Most heartrending character. Some eight thousand of them have been driven from -Russia and are now settled in Canada. I merely refer to this to draw attention to the kind of civi- lizers who are now joined with pious William in forcing Western civilization on China.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Purchase of Surrounding Property.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Warrington, on Friday after- noon. a meeting of the governors of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary was held. The object of the meeting was two fold. In the first place, the Board of Manegement asked for authority to Sell at their discretion,securities of the Infirmary, in order to provide the purchase money and expenses con- necz,ed with the transfer of property in beghest., Kendrick-street, and Beamont-street, recently bought on behalf of the Infirmary. Sacoadly, the Board of Management asked the consent of the governors to an alteration of the rule regulating the visits of friends of in patients. Mr. George Thompson 974 S voted to the chair, and there were also present, the Rev E C E Carleton, Rev Tether Wnittle, Rev Theodore Bishop, Rev J F Metthews, Messrs Georee Dakin, John Fairclough, J.P., T J.P., F Monks, J.P., Charles White, J.P, C J Holmes, 'Joseph Taylor, E Pickavance, EL Thornton, 5 L Tunstali (hon. sec), Dr Battle (senior house surgeon). and Mr Charles Leigh (assistant hon secretary). The CHAIRMAN in referring to the business before the meeting said the property in question came into the market quite recently. The Board of Management had not time to call a meeting of governors prior to the sale by auction but they thought they were doing quite right in buying the property in the immediate- ate vicinity •of the Infirmary. (Hear, hear.) He thought the governors would agree with him that it- was a wise procedure to acquire the property. He- would thank someone for the proposal. Mr. John FAIRCLOUCE moved the following resolution: .....esThat the Board of Management be and they are, hereby authorised to sell, at their discretion, securities to be selected by thern,in order to provide the purchase money and expenses of transfer of property in Legh- street,Kendrick-street, and Beamont-street, Warrington, recently bought on behalf of the Infirmary by order of the Board of Management, and that the Trustees in whose names the re- spective securities stand, be, and are hereby requested to execute the necessary transfers thereof:" He observed that he did not think the resolution needed any remarks from him in order to commend it for their acceptance. He believed it ve,s a very Wise thing for the Infirmary Committee to buy the property. As e.n investment it would bring in more money than that which was already invested and that meant getting a bigger income for the Infirmary. Then again if at any future time they were called upon to extend the I Infirmary they would be in possession of land which would enable them to do so and they would be in a I Position to meet the demands upon them for many years, to come. Dr. WHITE seconded. He remarked that Mr. Fairclough had considered the financial part of the question but he rather looked at it from a sanitary point of view, and the probable need of even - sloes in the near future. It was absolutely necessary to acquire the property in order to prevent the building' of anything which would contaminate the good air now surrounding the Infirrnery.Judging be the rate at which Warrington was erowine they could calculate that they would want an extra bed added to the accommodation of the Infirmary every year. They would therefore require accontmerlation for ten more beds irrtm years and twenty in a like number of years. They must look upon the extension of the Infirmary as an absolute necessity and he thought it was quite providential that the property came into the market at the present time. At a moderate price they had been able to obtain it, and it would enable them to extend the Infirmary when necessary, as well as empower them to keep the surroundings as they are at preeent. The Rev. Tiaeononz BISR 1P asked what amount was ' desired to purchase the nroperty ? Mr. TIINSTAL : £7,025. The Rev Father WHITTLE inquired what was the total amount of the Infirmary investments ? Mr. TUNSTALL replied that nominally they stood at £20,661, but they were werth considerably more. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. Dr. WHITE then moved the following resolutions "That the folinwine, be substituted for rule 90 or the rules and reeuletione of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary, viz The friends of in-patients shall be permitted to visit them at such times as shall tr prescribed by the Board of Management, but at no other times unless by permission from the Senior House Surgeon, or. in his absenca, from the Junior House S irgeon." He said that as a rule the friends of in- - patiente Were admitted on Fridays. The intended alteration of the rule was in order that they might be admitted on Stturday, which was more convenient for the people generally. Mr. THOMAS BIRCHALL in secendine, said he under- eon(' that Friday was also the visiting day at the Workhonee Infirmary arid the Fever hospital, but the more important reason for the alteration was that work- ingmen did no like leaving their work on Fridav,which probably meant the lesine of half a day, in order to see Mende who were inpatients of the Infirmary. As most of them finished work at noon on Saturday it would be more convenient for them if they were allowed to make their visit on thet day. fl • thought that a very good reason for making the eite-ation. Father WHITTLE Stlnpnrt,d.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN aka r.rnarkNi that he knew from experience at hi' own work thtt men did net like losing tin t 4 yis;t the InArmary The 1,-,whitinh wa; Kn3niniuty curie&amp; - -</p>
        <p>_ The CIItIRNIAN at the cln-e of the meeting rafPrred to the cycle par Afir. which ia to he held to-day. He slid they were all looking forward with very great confidence to the event, and he hoped it wou'id result in a very Fubstanti )1 amount being handed over to the Infirmary, Be trusted they would all do their best to make it as successful as possible. An a-khowle lgment of the Chairman's services con- cluded the proceedings. At or CU INGHAM HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS—Ad</p>
        <p>UNDER THE _CLOCK FOR GOOD_BOOTS.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MP. EXAMINEE, FIUDAY, AUGUST 31, 1900.</head>
        <p>MP. EXAMINEE, FIUDAY, AUGUST 31, 1900.</p>
        <p>which was reali9el. As a splendid wind-up to the pro a ceelino.s, a ,unkitig concert was afterwards'held in Parr Han. The ulatform was filled with thc,se who had taken part in the procession and presented an animated and picturesque spectacle. Seldom if over has such a living wealth of colour been seen on the platform and the ensemble was a most pleas- ing one. . .</p>
        <p>The f Wowing were the pr:zl winnerg : &amp;qv:3 neatest turnitic (eye,ies) : 1, A. Bri..-ms, 41, Scotc-street, Henry V. ; 2, (1 P.)Limor. W.l.rriogcoa C.C., RTheipierre ; hc, T J H pkios, 36, Wilth—street, th 3 VicAr of Wakenold•</p>
        <p>neateit turnout :-1, Miss Holbrook, 22, E Igeworth-stteet. Summer ; 2, Mrs Stott, 15, waiter- street, Padgati-lane, ivy ; hc, Mrs Webiter, Glenthorre, West uidsbury, Manchester. Parra' violets ; Miss A B md, 7, Froghall-lane, Imperialism. Most humorous get-up : I,Stanley Warbarton, Callan's Farm, Hallam, Church and Stage; 2, R Scariabri;.k, 46, alliesron-street, tramp en wheels. _</p>
        <p>Most original get-up (single) : 1, E G Ryder,7,Froghall- lane, vow oil 'ighness; he, Miss Ada NorOury, 24, St. Jam,-;os Street, Southport. royal mail. Mobt original get-up (tandem) : 1, Ernest Morris, 154., Wilderspool-road, Miss Simpson, Norton Arms Hotel, Pierot and Pierette.</p>
        <p>Best turnout boy (uo let 15) : 1, W Welsty, 53, G4,11,11-s!reet, St-.ckton Heath, Lord Costley. Best turnout (girl) : 1, Miss Annie Moore, 6, Howley lane, ivy queen. Harriers (including all on foot, neatest turnout) : 1, Miss Cissio Hill, 36, Golborne-street, Italian ; h c, Miss Crowley, 16, Ellesmere-aveine, Liverpool, American heiress ; Miss Eva Davies, 49, Museum-street, Mercia ; MiQs Lily Hughes, 50, Knutsford-road, f,irg,et.-mo-nots. M•)st comical turnout : 1, T K Gibbs. 329, Koutsford- rnad, school by ; h c, H Li Dell, 30, Sankey-street, clown. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Msst original turnout : 1, E Battersby, 29, Mande- ville-street, Vt'altoa,dark tr)rse ; h c, J J Pritchard, 165. WellfiAd-street, after the match. Best horseback turnout : 1, J Woods, 61e, Allen— street, Indian Prince ;hc, J Whitehouse, Bride-sLre.t Robinson Crusoe. B !st decoratei vehicle: I,Lieutenant Hulse, Ambulance. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>The judges suggeFted that the following were deserving of prizes :—W Holt, 8, Rutland-street, Runcorn, Special Scotch (Band) ; Furness and Company, Krager ;Bliian and Picka.vance, Naval Officer and Chinaman (mot,)r earl ; D DiVie.S. clown (donkey car) ; R Thuetle and Mr B Miller, home-made motor car. _ _</p>
        <p>Muster prize (cyclists) : 1, Warrington Royal, C.C., 59. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Minter prin (barriers), to be decided lat-ir. The judges were Messrs. W Fl Stoilier (fluyon), Bur n- inghain White, J Crizchley, and J E Warren. A watch competition is also to be held. i 3 gent's I.Bct gold centre-wconds lever watch, v due £l3, will, on the 251 s day of October, 1900, be wound up, snit started, and immediately sealed up and deposited at Parr'd Bank. The sealed pack:;.t containing the watch will be opened on the 31st,day of October, 190 d, and the wirch will be awarded to the person whose name appears on the line opposite the hour, minute, and sec )rlil at which the watch is found to have stopped. In ease no name appears opposite the second at which the watch stopped, the one nearest thereto becomes the winner. The watch will be supplied by M ‘ssrs Euatance and C.mpany, in whose window it will be on view until the 25;11 day of October, 1900. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>FOR THE INFIRMARY. Liberal Response. Saturday's Street Pageant.</head>
        <p>FOR THE INFIRMARY. Liberal Response. Saturday's Street Pageant.</p>
        <p>No recent event in Warrington has excited so much interest or been rewarded with such liberal support as marked the fancy dress parade and carni- val of Saturday evening last,which was the means of raising about £3OO towar is the reduction of the debt on the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary. It was in fact a brilliant pageant on wheel, suet as has never been seen previously in the town. The cyclists and members of athletic clubs rose to the occasion with commendable alacrity and neither expense nor trouble was spared to make the showy spectacle a success. For several weeks previously preparations had been in progress, and they INT..re as complete as possible in good time. All that was required on the day of the festival was fine weather The changeable weather and lowering clouds of the morning gave rise to some misgivings, and at 530 p.m.— the time appointed for the commencement of the parade—the weather was far from satisfactory, a biting east wind and forebodings of rain being only too much in evidence. Fortunately the rain kept off and the public had an excellent oppor- tunity of witnessing the parade. All taking part in the procession assembled round the bandstand and in front of the Town Hall and for some time the mars tals were busily engaged in. alloting them to their several seetions. In all there were nine sections, the positions in each having been previously balloted for. Mr. Harry Ashton was the chief marshal and he was assisted by the following :—Messrs. J Elliott,Jones, Critchley, G Rigby, H H Evans, G Turley, G W Richardson, J Poitnore. jun., and G H Smith.</p>
        <p>Promptly to time the glrgeous procession moved on its way. It was heralded by a motor-car in charge of Messrs. Bellian and Pickavance, the former dre-sed as a naval officer and the latter as a Chinaman. The brief announcement on the car "The procession is coming; get your money ready was certainly to the point, and the band of collectors following in its train did their level best to garner in the harvest of coppers. As the parade passed the Town Hall steps, where a number of ladies and gentlemen had assembled,it :was not quite complete and can hardly be said to have been seen at its best at this point. Judged as a whole the procession was a brilliant one, full of colour and animation, with characters grave and gay,and for the most part,well represented. The war in South Africa su4e,sted numerous characters and Boer and Briton were vary much to the fore. As a first attempt at such a spectacular display it was singularly successful and won the encomiums of all who saw it. The route taken was as follows :—Sankey street, Butter- market-street, Irlam-street, Church-street, turn at lamp near Parish Church and back down Church- street, Mersey-street, Bridge street, Horsemarket- street, Win wick-street, Tanners'-lane, Froghall-lane, Priestley-street, Powyi-street, turn to tne left into Liverpool-road for Bank Quay Bridge,Parker-street, Wilson Patten-street, Bold-street, Palinyra-,quare, and finish at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The place di honour in the procession was taken by a number of mounted characters, representing soldiers and attendants as well as the inevitable Ally Sloper, who bore a legend to the effect that tie was off to join B. P. after De Wet (gin). The first sec- tion was headed by the St. Paul's band and the other bands leading up the other sec`ions were The Volunteers;Messrs Monks Hall and Co ;Buckley- street ; Walton; Penketh Tannery; Messrs Fletcher, Russell and C 0.,; League of the Cross; Messrs CrosfielTs, and the Blue Coat Schoal. The Infirmary was represented by the matron and four nurses accommodated in a flower-bedecked landau in charge of Mr Charles Leigh, the treasurer of the parade. The members of the Warrington Cycling Club, the Royal CC, Paigate CC,, Runcorn C.C., Latchford, St. Helens, Earlestown, and Widnes Cycling Clubs all contributed to the attractiveness of the parade,while the Volunteer Cycling Company looked very smart and soldierly. The Rugby and Association Football Clubs were represented by members riding in waggonettes, those of the Asso- ciation code, being very prominent in the collecting line. A number of representatives of Friendly Societies wearing regalia of their Orders added to the general effect of the parade. Messrs. Fletcher, , Rassell and Co., had an excellent show of their stoves and asbestos fires on a couple of, lurrie a and Messrs. Richmond and Co., gave a representation of their well-known advertisement of Kruger bringing his goose to Mr. Chamberlain to be cooked. " Joe " was characteristically pourtrayed, but the represen- tation of Kruger was not so good. The Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Association was well represented, and the particularly attractive feature was provided by Privates Hollowdy and Newns. who,sun-stained by their recent experiences in South Africa, gave an illustration of their work on an ambulance !arm The Naval Brigade with a 4.7 gun, christened " Buller's Pet " was a prominent feature, The Brigade was composed of the mem- bers of the Warrington Royal Cycling Club, who looked exceptionally well in their sailor dress. Then there was an amusing representation of a Highland Band composed of a jovial troupe from Runcorn, who produced a unique discord of sound on a variety of toy instruments. The members of the fire brigade, with the engines Captain and ' Major prettily decorated, provided one of the smartest turnouts in the parade. The veteran super- ', intendant looked particularly smart on a charger. lAn enormous effigy of Kruger, made and lent for I the occasion by Mr C.Sandall,excited plenty of good- humoured banter. The figure carried a bulky tome with the quaint title" Kruger's Progress."Repree iota- Lions of the principal generals at the front were re- ceived with hearty plaudits, and numerous other soldier characters elicited approval. There was 'a good representati on of a couple of mounted Boers. A coster and his wife in their" 13ttle donkey shay' , attracted a good deal of attention. Messrs Crosfleld'a " Erasmic "coach drawa by four grays was a pro- minent feature.</p>
        <p>Of the characters represented by cyclists, some were very neat and 'tasteful. The representations of "Henry V." I Robespierre" and "The Vicar cof Wakefield" were good examples of this amongst the men, but they were outclassed in this depart- ment by the ladies. Miss Holbrook nad an extremely pretty turnout as summer whilst Mrs. Stott as“ivy" was very attractive.As."Partna violets" Mrs Webiter, Didsbury was equally successful, whilst " Im- perialism" was typiEed by Niiss Bond. The first prize for the most humorous turnout fell to Stanley Warhuron whose representation of Church and stage as "The Prodigal Parson" was very happy. Ntore laughable still was the get up of 'Air Scarisbrick as "The tramp on wheels." Originality was not a very strong point with the majority of the characters represented, but the re:presentation of 'your oil "ighness by E G Ryder was distinctly so. This oily. potentate was crowned with an oil can and from head to foot he showed his belief in oil cans. There were very few tandems in the procession, but the best characters were"Pierot and Pierette" and "The Alabama Coons." Amongst the children's displays, the representations of "vy leen," "The Italian Girl," "['he Aaaerican Heiress (with her dress of gold)"klercia" in Wilson Barrett's "Sign of the Cross" were the b st amongst the girls. The boy s were prominent as clowns, crickters, soldiers and sailors.</p>
        <p>Thousands of spectators gathered along the line of the route of the procession land considering the crowded state of the streets, it. is a matter for congratulation that the event passed off without the slighte.s; mishap. A named of praise is due to the officials and the police for the excellent arrange- ments made. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts), who was at, Llancltzlne, sent, the following telegram: "T 3 Pickavane,e, Cycle Parade, Warrington. Mayor and Aayoress hope committee's unselfish efforts will be:crowned with success. Very sorry not to be with you." As the picturesque scene passed slowly before the eyes of the spectators, the collect)rs did good business in col- lecting co7ltributions, which were freely given. This may bs iudged from the satisfactory sum</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>TFIE OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>TFIE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The ofcials, &amp;c., were the following :—President, his Worship the Mayor (Alderman Henry Roberts, J.P.) ; chairman at committee, Councillor John Roberts ; vice- chairman of committ?e, D Fanshaw,Esq.; hon treasurer, Mr Charles Leigh ; hon secretaries, Mr E Piekava,nee, Jun., Mr A Booth. Committees: Finance, Messrs H H Evans, T P Bradshaw, Charles Leigh, and C H Wood (hon secretary) ; prize, Messrs J Davenport, Cooper, Friar, Pritchard, J Wilkinson, W H Bellian, and W Habber:ey (loon secretary) ; printing and advertising, Messrs F Hogan, J Podmore, jun., D Davies, and J Newton (hon secretars ) Infirmary workingmen's. Joseph Taylor (hoe secretary) ; collectors, the Working men's Committee and one representative from eacn of the bodies taking part in the parade ; entertainment, Messrs W Brown, J Davenport, D Fanshaw. B Wads- worth, A Booth, E Pickavan,e, jun.. and E LI Clare (hon secretary) ; watch competition, Messrs W 'Jabber- ley, W Litton, W Brown, J Sampson, jun., E Whitham, G H Smith, J Wilkinson, T H Spinks, and T P Bradshaw (hon secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>The following are details of the proceeds :—Padgate Cycling Club (on road from Padgate) £6 16; 1:11 ; sundries collected on sheets on lurries, taoxes, and organ £l4 1s 61; handbags, £2l 14s 51; standing boxes, £8 us 011 ; pole bags, £2B 5; 51; hand boxes, 6s lAA ; proceeds of concert, £2B 18 ;3d tstal, £2OO 3s Ed. The money was made up of £1 103 in gold, £3B 153 31 in silver, and £l3l 0.1 21 in copper. Tnere will be a small deduction for minor exp-nses. The collecting books are still out, and the amount raised by the wa;c'i cc,M- pl,ition will also have to be included. It is estimated approximately that £3OO will be realizd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>-DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. A. HOUGH, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>-DEATH AND FUNERAL OF MR. A. HOUGH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>W? regret to announce tae death, in his 37th year, of Mr. Artnur Hough, proprietor and licensee of the R.fle- man Inn, Church-street, Rancorn. The sad event took place on Friday evening, at his residence, after a brief illness. Deceased was a.victim of that insidious disease diabetes, and was unconscious fee some days prior to his death. He was a jovial and popular landlord, and was much esteemed by his colleagues in the traae, as evidenced by his election to the office of president of the Runcorn Licensed Victuallers' Association. He took an active and untiring interest in the Runcorn Football Club, and had figured among its list of vice-presidents for many years. Any movement for the welfare of the organization alwao had his un- qualified support, and, when the club was not very flush in money a year Or two ago, he took a prominent part with others in the promotion of sports with a view to improving the state of the exchequer. At the election of Commissioners in 1892 he came forward as a candi- date, at the request of a body of ratepayers and, as a native of the town, he expressed himself strongly in favour of schemes—this being pretty much discussed— for the erection of a vehicular bridge over the Mersey and a bridge across the Bridgewater Canal in Halton- road. He was, however, unsuccessful at the pelt alter a stiff contest. His death in the prime of life is greatly deplored by his large circle of friends, and on all sides the utmest sympathy is felt for tne widow and family in their .painful bereavement. The mortal remains of the deceased gentleman were in- terred in Runcorn Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon. Walking in the procession were representatives of the Licensed Victuallers' Association of which the deceased gentleman was president, members of the Duke of Devonshire Lodge of the Independent Order of odd- fellowsZtrachsmen and townspeople. Following were the doctor's brougham, the hearse alai four coaches. The mournful cortege as it slowly wended its way to the place of burial was witnessed by a large concourse of people. The representatives of the Licensed Victuallers present were :—Messrs Tnwniis Jones, vice president, T C Price, treasurer, H Grundy, secretary, T White S Johnson. W Baker. G Horsefield, T Anderton, R Jones, M Conway, J Lloyd. W Faulkner, T Pendlebury, Baardman, R Riley. J Winfield, W Rodeik (Weston I Point), W Hulme, W It Wass, T Sanders (Evershods I Brewery) and J Leyland. The Oddfellows were Bros. J 31ade ;P.P-G.Me H B Toone P P.G.M. A Riley, G Bnrrows, C S W Lewis, S Lane lIJ Pendlebury. The tradesmen and others were Messrs. S Coventry, W Hankinson, T Rathbone, J Peacock, T Stringer, W Nick- son, Wesley Cooke, William Cook -,J Pewter, W Hughes, E Shaw, J Richardson, W Mtioc, W Eliison, and J Green. The bearers were Messrs. 'I Richardson, J Richardson, W Powell ane Rayle Garnet. In the doc- tors coach were Dr. Carruth-rs, Mr W Handley, J.P., and Rev C Garnett. The mourners were : Ist coach, Messrs. Arthur, Harry, and Leonard Eouge, son; William Houeh. uncle; Mr Traces' and James Armstrong : 2ad, Messrs Garnet Hough, E Sherratt, J Yates, C Dutton and Cropper ' • 3rd, Messrs Ashworth, C Rawle, G Turley, W Miles ; 4th, Messrs T Whinyates, W Craddock, Arden, H Briscoe, and J Erown. rhe service in the church and at the eraveiide was impressively conducted by the Rev. W Scholes. Bro. Slade, also delivered an appropriate Oddfellows' address. Beautiful wreaths were laid n the grave from the widow and family, Uncle VD ill, Uncle and Aunt (James and Maggie Armstrong), Timmas Jones' Mr and Mrs Arden, Mr and Mrs 'l' Richardson, Mr aed Mrs T C Price. Mr and Mrs J Capper, Mr rod Mrs Powell (Frod- sham), Mr ,and Mrs Miles, Mrs Coward, Mr 4nd Mrs P Riley, Mr C Ramie. (Licensed Vic•uallers Avociati Mr and Mrs Ashworth (Bury), Mrs Richards el, P A Morris, Mr and Mn Craddock, Peter Walker and Son, Warrington ; Mr Joseph Burton. Mr arid Ms 'r Mr and Mrs Gregory, Mr and Mrs C Ball, Mr and Mrs Rode Garnett, and Messrs T Whinyates, J Saeriatt. J Ellison, E Sherratt, T Snerratt, and F Lewis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Warrington Markets.</head>
        <p>Warrington Markets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday : wheat fetched 3s 101 to 4; (70th to the bushel) ; new oats, 2s 31 to 2; 4-A- I; old oats, 2s lid; flour (best seconas) per 2401 n., 21s; bran, 100 per 201 b. At the Provision :Market prices were as follows : Fresh butter, is 34 and 1s 41 per lb; cheese, 61, 7d and Bcl per lb; fresh eggs, 9 and 10 for is; fowls, 4s to 5s 6d per COUPiC; English beef, 6il to 104 ; mutton 7,1 to 101 ; lamb, tolld ; veal, 74 to 10d; pork, 6..1 to Bid. . .</p>
        <p>A First class Life Company, offering special advan- tages in the way cf investments and insurances, is pre- pared to receive applications for the post of Resident Agent at Warringtrin. A fixed stip-nd and commission would be offered to a sultaLle applicant who would be prepared to devote a lair portion of his time to the introduction of a high-class business. Address : " Assuee.NcE," 28 and 49. St. Swithln's-lane, London, E:C.—Advt•</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BRNEFIT UNDHR THE CLOCK FOR Bssr BOOTS IN LANCASHIttli.—Advt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>;OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>;OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Catholic Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>As wilt be seen elsewhere, special sermons in aid of the above Mission will be preached on Sunday next (morning', and evening) by the Rev Father Christopher, OP., St. Anne's Retreat, Sutton. A special feature of the musical services will be the production of a "Messe des Anges" by the late Mr. Samuel Moorat, and as this is the first performance in Warrington ajew remarks I respecting the work may not be out of place. The Mass is a fully developed and very beautiful one, and throughout may be discerned traces of the influence of the old Masters Mozart, Haydn and Beethoven. Never for a moment, however, does the composer lose his own identity in that of the great writers upon whose methods ' "Mews des Anges" is to a certain extent modelled. The work is, by reason of its intrinsic merit, worthy to rank amongst the best examples of church music. Filled almost to over-fl iwing, with ltaunti:2.g melodies, com- bined with brilliant harmonic and contrapuntal treat- ment, there is not a dull page in the whole work. What is of btill greater import, there is not a single trivial thought throughout the composition, the keynote etruck being an ex&amp;lted conception of the true religious mean- ing of the words set to music. How well the composer ' has succeeded in his task can only b 2 judged after a ravercnt hearing of the Mass. More need not be said, except that the interpretation of the work is in the hands of a small, but comp-tent choir (assisted by a few friends) under the conductorship of Mr. P.:_ter Bate- An efEe.ient orchestra led by Mr. R H Pearson, assisted by Mr. J E2kersley at the organ will do full justice to the accompaniments,which have been specially arranged by Mr R W Manning, and we feel safe in promising a good all round performance. In the evening when the orchestra will again he in attendance, a miscellaneotas selection from the works of Mr. Moorat will he given, and will well merit a hearing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Wycliffe P. 8.4. Society, Open Meeting.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P. 8.4. Society, Open Meeting.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above named society, what is called an open meeting was held on Sunday last, when the church was well filled with a mixed congregation of the members and their friends. Mr. Bindloss, of War- rington, presided and gave an interesting address on the value ot the Christian Bible. In the course of his address he said that from personal observation in India, he doubted whether the Christians in England haa that same reverenc 3 for their Bible whieh the Mohammedans had for the Koran. It was a common thing to see a Mohammedon pick up pieces of paper in the street and examine them, for fear it should be a portion of the Koran that was being destroyed. He advised those present to set a very high value on their Bibles. Mr. Whalley, of Manchester, was the speaker and in an excellent and at times humorous address, gave some good and sound advice as to how homes can be made happy. Am ingst the things necessary he mentioned courtesy, cleanliness, good temper, forbearance and godliness. Mr. E. L'ewellyn Pearce :ang two solos which were highly appreciated. The Rev. John Yonge appealed to the members to canvas the town during the week, so that they mighticonamence the new half year on Sunday next with an increased membership. Mr. Edward Wallington read the Scripture lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Gospel Hall.</head>
        <p>Gospel Hall.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, August 26th, Mr. J. Green preached from John speaking about people who are always trying to get to Heaven their own way. But Christ said "I am the Way." There are some persons who try to put their own works to Christ's finished work. When Christ was down hero, in the midst of the doctors he said " Wist 313 not, I must be about My Father's business." and on Calvary's Cross when expiring He said " It is finished," He drained our bitter cup to the very dregs, and yet some people laugh and scoff. If people only knew the awful responsibility that rests upon their souls in not trusting Christ, they would not remain in their sins a minute longer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SHORT STORIES AND LONG.</head>
        <p>SHORT STORIES AND LONG.</p>
        <p>With many complaints it is a short story—short and harp. In a few days or a few hours it is over, and the patient is either convalescent or dead. Pardon this scyle of talk ;it is not pleasing, but it is true. There is no time for much treatment or much sympathy. Toe kind of thing, however, that msk=mi me heave deep sighs of pity,is a tale like this of Mrs. Woodward's. It drags on.emd drags on—such a case as this does— lik3 a Scotch winter,or,goodness help u!—like the opera of" The Huguenots."</p>
        <p>" Nearly all my life," the lady says—fancy an illness of these dimensions—" nearly all my life I have suffered from indigestiou and weakness. I was always "—weigh the word !—" always tired and languid." "No matter what I ate I had pain around the chest and sides. Now and then cold, clammy sweats broke out upon me; and I bad an all -gone, fainting feeling, that was like sinking into oblivion. I got little sleep,and the morning light and the birds' songs had no cheer in them for me.</p>
        <p>I barely crawled about tho hous?, and for weeks together was unable to do even that. This was my way rom year to year.</p>
        <p>Dcctors and medicines I had in tiresome plenty, but nothing did me any good until I read about Mother Seigel's Syrup, and—buying a bottle from Mr. Ellis, the Chemist—l began taking it. That bottle helped me,and by keeping on for awhile with the syrup, I steadily improved, and at last, wondering how it could be, I was a well woman ; practically for the first time in my life. It was almost too good to be true, yet true it is ; and grateful I am for the medicine that brought it about. You may publish what I have said, and refer anyone to me."—Sarah Woodward, 3, Sherbrooke Terrace, Sher- brooke Road, Carrington, Nottingham, September 12th, 1899.</p>
        <p>" For many years," so runs another letter, "I was low, languid and weak. My skin was sallow, and I had a cnnstant feeling of sickness. All food pained me, or made m.! uncomfortable. No treatment or remedies did nia any go3l until I began using Mother Seigel's Syrup, wider the recommendation of my friend, Mrs. Webster, of Mansfield.</p>
        <p>"After a few 41tys of this there was a marked im- provement in my condition and in a little time I became healthy and strong. My h usband also bas been cured of indigestion by Mother Seizel's Svrup. You may be sure we commend it to all our friends."—Mrs. Ann Waring, Skegby Forest, Skegby, near Mansfield, Notts, Sept 13th, 1899.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Hodgson: We regret to state that Mrs. Hodgson,of 20, Salis- bury-street, died suddenly on Tuesday, at Elmsted Vicarage, Kent, when on a visit to the Rev Frank and Mrs. Timins. The tidings of Mrs Hodgson's death will come as a painful shook to a large circle of friends, both at Bolton, where she formerly lived, and at Warrington, where she had resided with her family since 1890, when her son, the late Rev Piston Stanley Hodgson, took up work as one of the clergy of the Parish Church. For the last ten years her life bad been much bound up with the progress of the work at Bank-street—now St Clement's—Mission,of which her son was in charge until his death in 1891. Her universal kindness, her devotion to good works, her untiring zeal for the welfare of those who cams under her influence, en- deared her to many in all classes of life, and her loss will be keenly felt. SUMMER SALE.---W. T. WILLIAMS is now proceed ing : The whole of the Season's stack to be cleared ;it a " Sweeping Reduction." SkNKEY•STREET AND- LEGH -STREET. Warringtoa, —Advt.</p>
        <p>Good Templary. The weekly meeting of the "Young Mission juvenile temple was held on Wednesday evening in the Brick-street schoolroom. Sister S L Sheath,C.T., opened the temple and there were eignt now mem- bers initiated. After the usual business,the follow- ing members gave recitations and songs ; —Sisters, N Walton, Wright, J Walton, Shaw, Dunbabin, Storey, and Howarth, Brothers Hardm tn and Royl- ance. A trio was given by Sisters r: heath, Dean and Plucknett. The Temple was closed by Sister Sheath. The meeting of the "Temperance Mission " lodge was afterwards held in the same room. Bro. R )berts, presided, visitors being present from the "Onward" and" Star of the Eist " bodge, Warrington and the Hope of all Souls "Lodge, Liverpool.—Bro White Liverpool, gave a report of the Di,trict Lodge Ses- sion held at Liverp.,ol on Saturday. Mimr the usual business had been transacted, the following pro- gramme was rendered :—Sister Eva Nurse, recita- tion, " Don't sen I your little cr;ildren for beer," Bro Di:lon, song, "Queen of the &amp;Joh," Bro J .s.heath, humorous reading," The Royal Bumper Deg reel; Sister Mrs Nurse, song, "00l v an Armours bearer," ; Bro White, song, " Mona," ; Sister S L Sheath, recitation, " Pat's letter,"; Bro Litherland song, "The wreck of H.B. London,"; Bro Roberts address ; Sister Mrs !=lbeath, song," Rock of Ages." The lodge was closed in due form by Boa Roberts, C.T., after an enjoyable evening had been spent.</p>
      </div>
