      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>AUGUST 31sT 1900.</p>
        <p>THE Taff Vale trouble is The Railway Unrest. only one of several indica-</p>
        <p>_ _ _ tions that railway men are I extremely dissatisfied with their conditions of remuneration and employment. They have a natural desire to better themselves, and they form trades unions and set up the machinery for shaping and pressing their demands. But it is not demon- strated that these unions are themselves the cause of unrest, or that salaried officials egg on the men to prefer unreasonable requests or encourage them to go out on strike. The Taff Vale case shows that the officials are more ready to adjust matters and display a more conciliatory spirit than the men are. We are glad of this, because the unions are so readily assailed by unthinking persons, and there is so much loose talk about their tyranny and their driving of trade out of the country. It is surely better for the railway officials to deal with respon- sible representatives than to have to negotiate with men who are less skilled in affairs and less capable of taking a cool survey of a wide situation. We fear; however, that some rail- way directors still cherish feelings of antagonism towards the unions, and believe they can score better by attributing all the unrest to their agency and refusing to have anything to do with their spokesmen, though they are themselves the elected and paid agents of the shareholders. There can be no good feeling whilst these views prevail, and whilst it is assumed that the railway men have no business to negotiate, and should instantly and uncomplainingly submit to what the directors may lay down or declare. Courtesy and fairness on each side are the best guarantee for excellent rela- tions and the absence of quarrels. In one or two cases we cheerfully recognise that the directors always act in this way, and they have found the plan to answer admirably. It answers so well in other branches of industry that it can hardly fail to be successful in railway affairs. Wherever it is possible direct conferences between the directors and the men should be promoted, and details should be dealt with at the time in a businesslike manner. Were this the case we cannot believe that all-round increases of wages would seem so plausible to the persons who demand them,and that the power of the unions as well as of directors would be seen to have limits beyond which it is imprudent to advance. Probably in some cases conciliation beards could be established and dispute avoided. In the larger railway concerns this would not be possible, because conditions vary so much in differ- ent parts of the same railway. The general:atti- tude of the public and the press in these railway disputes is one of sympathy and of reserve. Dis- cussions upon details cannot be undertaken with advantage, or with the requisite knowledge. The utmost that can be done is to recommend confer- ences and an attitude of conciliation and give- and-take. It is not, we cannot too firmly lay down, a wicked thing to ask for better conditions of labour even from a railway board,and, as a general rule, outsiders have nothing to do with the matter until their interests and their convenience begin to be imperilled. We still hope that the present un- rest will not develop into a serious struggle, but if such should unhappily be the case the Board of Trade will have to be more vigorous and bold than it has hitherto been disposed to be. We have long been friendly to compulsory arbitration, and if the Board of Trade is unable, with its present powers, to avert a great railway war we shall con- tend that compulsion is the only remedy in such extreme cases. However, much may be done by tact, courtesy, and perfect frankness.</p>
        <p>The actions of the Germa n Doubts and Jealousies in China. Government are being</p>
        <p>freely and rather sarcasti- cally commented upon on the Continent. The appointment of COUNT YON WALDERSEE excites the French people, who are saying that if it be true that the appointment was made under the inspira- tion of Russia, it was not friendly for an ally to act in this way. The Germans are detecting a difference of policy between the KAISER and COUNT VON &amp;MOW, and gravely objecting to what they fear is a new movement,to obtain for Germany , some fresh authority and position in China. The increase in the German Fleet in Chinese waters excites apprehensions and jealousies in this coun- try, where the hesitation and feebleness of Lord SALISBURY have already made a bad impression. The disbandment of the Belgium Legion is now being attributed to pressure from Berlin. The conduct of Russia is very significant. It is her aim to be first in everything, or to be conspicuous in the field. Ever since military operations com- menced the Russian accounts have represented her troops as achieving wonders, acting independently, and taking over the lion's share of work. In the event of the Chinese Government abandoning Pekin as a capital and the Pekin provinces as inde- fensible, the Russians will, some persons suggest, find means to assert a superiority in concert with Germany, leaving the other Powers to do most of the hard work and then retire. Obviously, the situation is one of doubt land embarrassment. There must be a good deal going on beneath the surface, and Lord SALISBURY must either be an assenting party, or he must feel himself unable to resist what he is compelled to dislike. We cannot help thinking that there is a secret understanding between Germany and Russia, likely to produce some strange proposals in the immediate future. Upon the prospects of peace there is still much room for speculation, and the results must depend upon the understanding between Russia and</p>
        <p>Germany, as well as upon th fate of the Mauch. dynasty. If it be found impo,lsible to restore the dynasty, the alternative policy l'ill assuredly dis- close the real views and aims of the two Powers we have mentioned. In the event ol he EMPEROR or the DOWAGER EMPRESS being' reinstated some pecuniary compensation must lx,,ienforce3, and then the difficulty will be as to —whether: the money shall be raised by loan or by taxation. The guarantor of the loan willbciure to command a strong position at Pekin. If taxation be relied upon, the finances of China ought ta be in the hands of some European director. In that case an Englishman might stand a good chance of being appointed. But we fear that the aim of three of the Powers is to undermine the positior of Great Britain in China. The Powers of whom we cannot help being jealous are Russia,Germany and France. The Americans are not disposed to be too active—at any rate, until the Presidential election is over—and Japan has reasons of her own for being very cautious and discreet. These Powers are friendly to this country, but whether they are agreed in pursuing a policy which will preserve our interests in China is another matter. Lord SALISBURY is like the lady of whom Dr. JOHNSON' said: "She was wiggle-waggle--I could never persuade her to be categorical." He is never very pronounced, firm, or courageous. It is much to be feared that he will be out-manoeuvred to the end, and that when the Chinese crisis is over we shall have to lament the feeble part he has been playing.</p>
        <p>We cannot very well complain of the liberation of Sipido, the youth who fired at the Prince of Wales, when we remember our own treatment of Simon Bernard, the man who was implicated in the attempt upon the life of Napoleon 111 in 1858. In the "Fortnightly Review," for September, Mr. John F. Taylor, draws a parallel between the cases of Sipido and Bernard. There is little question that the Orsini plot against the French Emperor, which came to a head in the terrible explosion of bombs at the Paris Opera House on January 14th, 1858, by which several people were wounded,. though the Emperor and Empress escaped, was hatched tin England. Simon Bernard was arrested here and charged with procuring the explosives— indeed, explosives were actually found upon him— and it was given in evidence that he was one of the chief plotters. But an eloquent appeal was made to the patriotism of the jury by Mr. Edwin James, the prisoner's counsel, to guard well the right of asylum for political refugees in England —the home of the oppressed. "Tell the French Emperor," said he, "that he cannot intimidate an English jury. Tell him the jury system is the sanctuary of English liberty," and so on. Public opinion.was hostile to the French Emperor at the time, and Bernard was acquitted. The crowd cheered him on his liberation, just as they cheered Sipido in Brussels. Lord Palmerston introduced into the House of Commons a bill making the crime of conspiracy to murder a felony, and in- tended to deal with such cases but the bill wig thrown out by a majority of 234 against 215. "If we oblige France to-day," said Mr. Horsman in the discussion, "we may have to oblige Austria to- morrow, or even the King of Naples." It is ira" possible to say that the country was not right then. But, remembering the way we dealt with Simon Bernard, we can hardly make a grievance of the escape of Sipido from Belgium.</p>
        <p>The motor-car has been a long time in "catching on " in the metropolis, despite that memorable parade to Brighton through the November nin.,d and mist nearly four years ago. But "automohu" ism," the latest name given to the movement! 3t seems at last to be making progress in the Orea ;City. The electric cabs were withdrawn after more or less unsuccessful trial,but motor-Cars," and cycles have now become common objects or the London streets, and even the peculiar soundg and the still more peculiar smells by which solne ot them announce their approach have ceased tcc'l !- attract attention. The Automobile Club iS $ S 1 to have had a large accession of members lately, no fewer than two hundred having joined durl.ng„ the present year, and the club is conteraplat,ll:l removal from Whitehall-place to more commodious premises, where the motors of its members MO find accommodation as well as the members theln: selves. The coming of the motor-car was predictea more than ten years ago, but the prophecy has taken longer to fulfil in London than in, perhaPs, any other great capital, and even than in some go- ahead provincial towns. Even now there does nu,t,„ seem to be any fear of that "slump in horse-flesh which was also predicted as a result of the moto.r. It is a mistake to suppose that we very ,readilY adopt new inventions. In this respect, although, our country has been in the past the " workshop 01 the world," we are an exceedingly conservative'. nation. The earliest steam motor-cars appeared In the streets between sixty and seventy years age. • Bat they were killed by Act of Parliament, and It- is only in the last decade of the century, the first quarter of which witnessed their invention, that auto-cars have had a resurrection, and appear to have come to stay.</p>
        <p>Saturday s cycle parade and carnival was one °E the best spectacular functions ever held in War" rington, and the measure of success it achieved viles remarkable. Granted that the Infirmary is 3 deserving object of charity, the claims on the pub- lic purse this year have been so great that a soul of modest dimensions would not have surprised many people had it been the outcome of the parade. But the most sanguine promoters ef the affair have had their anticipations realised, and more than £3OO will be handed over to the Institu' tion when all the sources of income have bees properly tapped. This is magnificent, and reflects the very highest credit on the ladies and gentlemerl who have worked so hard and so assiduousli to make the parade a success. The street collection alone was admirably carried out,and a rich harvest of over 45,000 coins was gleaned from the public, realising £l7O. A feature of the parade has bee 3., the excellent management and attention to detail which characterised it and which drew praise fro); the judges as well as from the man in the street' The Infirmary will benefit handsomely, and as th borders of the Institution will have to be extende,' ere long to cope with its ever-increasing useful' ness, this is very gratifying.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. St. Helens. This match took place in the Corporajoci BathsiLeg,bci street, Warrington on Tuesday night, before a VI, number of spectators. The home team were Pocir" represented, the players being as follows :—Robertslog goal ; Ashton, Crenshaw, Warren, Sutton, Prince a° Lowe. The Saints defended the deep end in the half. From the throw in, Mavity got p ,ssession for Helens and passed back to Preston who threw well tig the bath but Warrington soon had it b,ck again. I. foul against Warrihgton in front of the home ges,,r ended in nothing tangible being scored, prince f'r. Warrington got possession and swam right up to the% Helens goal but vn-zs robbed when in the act of shootiobi; ' Soon afterwards, Lowe tried a good shot for Warringt°c the ball striking the cross bar. Preston for the Saints gocid possession and tried a shot which Robertson sav'st grandly. The same player swam up the bath but lots the ball when shooting. Lowe and Sutton tried 5110 r for Warrington. but Aspinall saved easily. The lattet threw up the baths, and Mavity getting possession Seljd an in a shot which Robertson had no chance of saving' i.ts thus the Saints registered thair first goal. The Sai-e. were now playing grandly. Just before halHirfl„, Lawrenson shot in tut Robertson saved. Half-tltt,o Saints. 1 ; Warrington, o.—After the restart, Warringr,e showed improved form and a rush was made on Saints' goal, but Aspinall was equal to all ernergencilfr Lowe, Sutton, and Prince for Warrington sent in SW' A did shots which Aspinall succeeded in keeping out. rush by the Saints' forwards ended in Kearsley additlEo't second goal from shcrt range. Warrington had now' no ,hd their bolt and St. Helens began to press, Robertson the score would have been mite. At the c' St. Helens were victorious by 2 goals to nil. been To MOTHERS.-3lrs. Winslow's Soothing SyruP " their used for over fifty years by millions of mothers zhildren while teething, with perfect success. It will re.116'..,4 the poorsufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ria produces natural ouiet sleep, oy relieving the child frglii and the ittie cherub awakes " as b,ight as a button.' 11 lid Per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>r</p>
      </div>
