      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>– .14</head>
        <p>- .14</p>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Three bishops are at the present time officially Laking part in the services at Exeter Cathedral. There is probably no other Cathedral Church in Bziglana that can boast such an experience. They are the Lord Bishop of the diocese ; the Bishop of 44arlborough, who has this week been installed as the new Dean; and the Bishop of Credition, one of the canons of the Cathedral. There is also a fourth tiphop doing duty in the same diocese, namely, Bishop Kestell-Cornish. England does not suffer so tnuch from Jack of bishops, as some would have us believe.</p>
        <p>At the recent manceuvres near Shorscliffe there Was a curious and surprisingly large number of instances of ball cartridges being found among the blank. There is only one possible explanation for this kind of thing. It is the prevalence of a mild form of kleptomania. A man engaged at volley tiring at the butts finds that somehow his rifle has not gone off. He is, therefore, " one cartridge to the good." Ile puts that round into his pocket, and .Rets nervous owing to the presence there of his rnatches, though it is quite a needless fear. Then he Puts it into the bottom of a pouch. Volunteer "Ifficers ny that scarcely a field day passes:on which there is not at least one bullet heard whistling through the air.</p>
        <p>Last week it was Bournemouth Regatta that was Postponed on account of bad weather,and this week the same thing happened at Torquay, although the second day's events passed off at the latter sea- side resort, on Tuesday, satisfactorily. From most '&lt;)f the health ana pleasure resorts come!complaints °t unfavourable weather, which appears to have quite upset the calculations of hotel and lodging. house conductors.</p>
        <p>The high and still increasing price of coal has already been commented upon in this letter and, from present prospects, is likely to be mentioned. ?Zany times in the next few weeks. Notwithstand- nag the present tightness, purchases in large quantities continue to.be made by other nations. it has just transpired that the Admiralty have had to pay '2Bs to 303 per ton for a hundred thousand tons of Welsh steam coal,to bs delivered in October. Had not th contract been signed prior to this Miserable Taff Vale strike, in which both sides are greatly to blame, the price would no doubt have been higher. It is an enormous figure 7vhen the quantity required by the Admiralty considered, and, of course, involves a increase of expenditure on the Navy. But more serious still is the handicap which the high prices must occasion to our mercantile marine, which on this account is bound to be hampered in competition with that of Other countries. Of so much importance is the Whole question that if the House of Commons were Sitting it would soon have to engage the attention pi its members. All this time colliers' wages are Increasing to such an extent as, with the additional ten per cent. now conceded, to make the wages zSte sixty per cent. above the standard.</p>
        <p>, A strikingly innocent but effective way of evad- Ing the Seats for Shop Assistants' Act is reported from "down West." The seats are provided, as the Act require% but are not allowed in certain estab- lishments to be used. The proprietors affect ignor- %ace of the purpose for which the seats are re- quired; but as no action has yet been taken in the Courts, they have yet to learn what the penalty Upon conviction may prove to be. For the benefit .of such, it is well, nerhapg, to state that on sum- mary conviction these employers are liable for a larst offence to a fine not exceeding .£3, and for a second or subsequent offence to a fine of not less than £1 and not exceeding £5. As seats are made and provided to be sat upon, it need scarcely be added that the attempted evasion is of a kind erninently calculated to provoke the indignation of 'magistrates and result in the imposition of the kaximum penalty. A Chinaman's affected inno- •tence could be no more" childlike and bland," or more offensive to an Englishman's common sense.</p>
        <p>This countt y is admittedly behind some of those ell the Continent in utilising the latest scientific discoveries for purposes of locomotion, or for adding the amenities and conveniences of civilised life. ,the defects of our telephone system, as well as its leadequacy, have for long been a byeword. While :11 towns of importance, even in so unenterprising a c?ttntry as Italy, are provided with admirable electric tramways, the majority of our great towns Teraain in this respect at least a generation oehind the Continent. The tram in this country iS merely an omnibus running on rails dragged by the sorriest of jades, and worked on a system long since obsolete even in the least ener- getic cities of the South American Republic. But, having said this,it has also to be said that when we do Lestir ourselves,we are apt to make great strides forward. This ig what has now been done in the case of the Central Landon Electric Railway. Lon- don has not even in this respect beaten the pro- vinces, as Bristol can testify; but the provinces ziust look to their laurels.</p>
        <p>Brewster Sessions continue to produce as many and as varied licensing questions and points of Reneral interest as ever, while the decisions given afford greater satisfaction as a rule, judged by the Comments in provincial newspapers, than was the CRB6 a few years ago. One reason for this con- Susion probably is that magistrates have been led by the agitation in favour of prohibition, or" lcnal option," to regard the subject as of increasing social ii.nd political importance, and have therefore been a little more careful and painstaking than formerly. Liverpool has this week proved an exception among ,the towns and cities in which Brewster Sessions nave been held and the course taken by the licensing authorities to reduce the number Of public-houses in thickly populated districts nas met with approval. The decision •of te Licensed Victuallers' and Brewers' Associa- tion to organise a guarantee fund in opposition to What they call "magisterial action,' can scarcely bp considered prudent,having regard to the general trend of popular opinion on the question involved. It is not a case of Sir Wilfred Lawson or General kung, so • far as the public are concerned, since the latter are very determined at present to have lleither of these, and appear quite content with the evidence of greater diligence on the part of licensing justices in dealing with the applications brought before them, and in having the courage to refuse the renewal of licences where good cause for refusal has been shown. Liverpool may do worse than follow the example set by Birmingham and one or two other large and important centres.</p>
        <p>The complaint made at Fareham licensing ses- sions against three publicans was one in which very Many of the public are concerned who for consider- ations of health feel bound to take their walks abroad. It was not alleged that the licensed houses Concerned had been improperly conducted, but it Was complained that the publicans had declined to Serve with tea two lady cyclists who had applied for it. There are probably few either cyclists or Pedestrians who have not met with the same diffi- Culty in country districts, where the inconvenience 18 intensified by the fact that, as a rule, the inn- keeper has the only house in the village at which auch refreshment can be hoped for. The tealess one generally grumbles and moves on, the annoyance being too transient to lead to any complaint to the , Dowers that be." In this, however, the two ladies were persistent, and took the proper legal steps to ventilate their grievance ; with the result that an emphatic warning was administered rom the bench that publicans must supply tea if it IS asked for, and the ladies were thanked for under- ftking an unpleasant duty in the public interest. It !,8 only fair to say that a considerable improvement alte taken place during the last decade in the pro- vision at licensed houses of refreshments other than rinks. Many now give good accommodation of this kind. True, a minority are unreasonable in their charges, and a still smaller number will not take the trouble to fulfil their legal obligations. To the last mentioned class the cap in question should Drove a useful lesson, and it is trusted they may heed no other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TREAT TO THE MISSION Scapors.—The Sunday school enholars of the new mission, at Frodsham Bridge, were, • Friday last, entertained to a capital tea by the Misses 4Yes, of Frodsham. The Rev Mr Davis, Mr W Shaw Itzlissioner)Rancorn' and the teaching staff of the mission *ere also present. After tea the children were further entertained with the inimitable " Punch and Judy " 1/11°97, the efficient entertainer being Mr W F W Jones, f uncorn. Subsequently sports were held and very use- ,lll Prizts distributed to the winners. B9fore the corn- • broke up, Miss Hayes loaded each child with toys, and other good things. The children showed their an -Preciation by giving hearty cheers for their kind entertainers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
