      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WiDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WiDNES.</p>
        <p>PECULIAR ACCIDENT TO A WIDNES BOY</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a youth named Albert Edward Daintith, residing at 90, Mersey-road, met with an accident under somewhat peculiar circum- stances. He was playing in the Park with some companions when he slipped down and injured him- self. Assistance was promptly to hand, and the un- fortunate youth was removed to his home. Dr. Edwards was summoned, and on examination he found that Daintith had dislocated his right hip. On the following morning he was conveyed to the Accident Hospital. He is now doing well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>OCCAkONAL COURT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>OCCAkONAL COURT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Messrs A R Norman and G L Davies presided, on Wednesday, over an Occasional Court at Runcorn —Ellen Revs, was fined 53 and 63 cost or seven days for indecent behaviour in Waterloo-road the prev- ious night. —Frank Sefton, and Charles Whittle,tor sleeping out, and being withourvisible means of subsistence, were sentenced respectively to seven and 14 days imprisonment Sef ton was given the option of an us fine.—Harry Farrington, for being drunk and disorderly in High-street, on the 2Sth August was also fined its. MINISTERIAL CHANGES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Wesleyan circuit said farewell to its ministers on Sunday last. The Rev C W L Christien (superintendent), who left on Monday for Farnworth, preached his last sermon to a crowded congregation at St. Paul's Church on Sunday even- ing last; and contemporaneous with his last utter- ances the Rev J A B Malvern from the pulpit of "Camden "took an affectionate farewell, of a con- gregation, who have become greatly attached to him for his many good qualities. Mr Malvern left Runcorn for Hanley on the Thursday.—The new ministers are the Rev Alfred Wells (superintendent) and the Rev J Barnett Foster, and they enter upon their duties at St Paul's on Sunday next. VICTORIA. ROA.D BOARD SCHOOL BROKEN INTO At Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday before Mr. G L Davies, Alfred Lewis and Robert Roberts, described as tailors from Wolverhampton and Sal- ford respectively, were charged on suspicion of breaking into Victoria Road Board School. Supor- intendant Pearson said during Monday night some person or persons broke into Victoria Road School and smashed open the drawers of the main room, but failed to secure any booty. Two persons were seen loitering about the premises on Monday night and also on Tuesday morning. Detective Gregory was put upon the scent of these two men and on his bicycle he traced the men to Chester where he apprehended them and brought them to Runcorn. The police had however made full inquiries and the men were able to give a satisfactory account of where they were on the night when the robbery took place. lie therefore, asked for the prisoners , to be discharged. The prisoners were accordingly set at liberty. DASTARDLY OUTRAGE AT WESTON WORKS.</p>
        <p>A gross case of wilful and malicious damage at the Weston Works of the United Alkali Company, was detected on Friday morning. One of the work- men discovered that sulphuric acid was flowing from one of the vitriol chambers in a steady stream, and further inspection showed that a piece of lead had been cut out of the side of the chamber. The manager, Mr. James Tudor, was promptly notified of the fact, and under his supervision the leakage was stopped, but not before 50 tons of vitriol had escaped into the Weaver Canal. In its coarse to the canal the acid had eaten its way through a stock of iron and copper ore, doing great damage, and a large quantity of fish were killel. No doubt is enter- tained that the chamber has been damaged with malicious intent, and Superintendent Pearson and Constable Wylie have been endeavouring to track the culprit, but so far without success. The mone- tary loss to the company is very heavy. PRESENTATION TO THE REV. J. A. B MALVERN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, delivered an address on " False swearing" to a large attendance of members at the meeting of the Wesleyan Men's Bible Class. In the discussion which ensued several of those present took part. After the closing of the school the teachers assembled in the classroom to witness a presen:a- tion;.which was being made to Mr. M.alvera, who is shortly leaving the circuit. The gift consisted of a handsome Gladstone bag bearing the inscription " Presented to the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, by the Men's Bible Class. Weston, as a token of esteem and regard. August 26th, 1900." The present had been subscribed for by the class mentioned and the Mon- day night's Nlutual Improvement:Class.of which Mr. Malvern bad acted as president for the past three years. Mr. 11. Griffiths handed over the gift. He and several other members spoke of the value of the services Mr. Malvern had rendered to the Wes- ton Society during his sojourn amongst them, and they regretted his removal. Mr; James Ball, super- intendent of the Sunday school, referred to the frequent visits made by Mr. Malvern to thatinstitu- tion. The recipient suitably responded.</p>
        <p>THE REV. O. W. L. CHRISTIEIsi CLOSES HIS MINISTRY AT ST. PAUL'S.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev. C. W. L. Christien, in accordance with the Wesleyan Methodist three- years' system, closed his ministry in the Runcorn circuit by preaching a farewell sermon at St • Paul's Church. There was a large congregation, notwith- standing that the holiday season is now on. The preacher said that on such an occasion a minister naturally turned to some passage of Scripture which would sum up all the teachings that he had been endeavouring to give during his term. of service among them. and such a text was found in Col. iii. 11. "Christ is all." In the course of his sermon he</p>
        <p>dwelt upon the three points, that Christ is all in Christian doctrine, in the Christian pulpit, and in Christian experience the life. At the close he re- ferred to his work in the Runcorn circuit, saying that it had been a great joy to preach Christ among them, and that he had often realized that many of them were praying for him. He was deeply thank- ful for Divine help and for blessing on his work. He expressed his earnest dealre for the present and eternal well-being of the congregation of St. Paul's, and trusted that they would ever be a devoted and progressive Church. After the Benediction, the choir led the congregation in singing "God be with you till we meet again." The service was followed by the administration of the Lord's Supper, to which a large number remained. Mr. Christien commences his ministry in Bolton, Farnworth cir- cuit, next Sunday. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN (RUNCORN).</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon, through the kindness of Mr J. G. Rodger ( a member of the Liverpool Committee), a sailors' party was organized from Runcorn and Ellesmere Port. The sailors numbered 39, 22 from Runcorn and 17 from Ellesmere Port. The Ellesmere Port contingent made the journey to Frodsham by train, but the Runcorn company proceeded to Overton Church by waggonettes. A start was made from Runcorn at 2 p.m., arriving at Frodsham at 3.30. On arriving at Mersey View the Mission flag was hoisted to the flag pole. They soon made friends with the Ellesmere Port party. In the absence of Mr. J. Howard the Missionary, he was well represented by his son. John Howard, junior. After all hands had been served out with tobacco, games were then entered into,consisting of football, and cricket. Old Aunt Sally came in for special attention and she lost many pipes through the good marksmanship. Mr Shaw then summoned all hands on deck for tea. All being seated at the tables Mr Shaw mentioned the well-known words of Nelson, " England expects every man, this day to do his duty," a request that was quickly carried out. There was an ample supply of roast beef ,ham. pickles, cakes, &amp;c., &amp;c. Subsequently sports were commenced in earnest the events including flat races jockey races, boot and shoe race, three legged race, and a tug of war match was organized, between 15 Runcorn men, and 15 Ellesmere Port men; they were very well matched,and for over a minute there was not a pin to choose between the teams.At last Elles mere Port proved themselves the strongest. Prizes were then distributed to the successful winners. A vote of thanks was then proposed by Captain Con- way that the best thanks of the meeting be given to Mr. Rodger for his great generosity gift in giving them that most enjoyable treat. This was seconded b Captain W hill. The proposition was received with great applause and with three hearty cheers. The whole party then joined bands in singing " Auld Lang Syne " and " He's a jolly good fellow," with much gusto. Mr Shaw,who had charge of the party, suggested the Natioaal Anthem, This was accord- ingly given. Three cheers were called for the Queen, General Buller, Baden-Pawell and all the generals in South Africa The party then separated. A halt was made at the Misses Hayes, the sailors' friends who had most kindly provided each man with a button hole, which was received with three cheers. The party arrived at the Seamen's Institute at 9 p.m., amid many expressions of the pleasure they had derived from the outing. The day being fine no doubt added to their plessure.</p>
        <p>VOR DISPOSAL. Old Established Milk and Sweet -L: BUSINESS. Fixtures stock and goodwill £45 ; rent Ts 6d clear, genuine bargain.-97, PHOEBE-STBEZT, Regent- road. Salford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>• • ..iiirrot. Ad, _ .</head>
        <p>• • ..iiirrot. Ad, _ .</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, AUGUST 31, 1900.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDSNT.] Tae Licensing Sessions are past ant gone, but leave behind them an object lesson, as to how not to get an off-licence. It is a well known fact that to obtain a licence for any premises in Runcorn, especially of the "off "character. is a very difficult task. The bench always decide upon the merits of a case; but in this instance there was practically no case. Six times has the Balfour-street application been before the Runcorn Licensing Bench, and si times has it been refused. One would, however, have supposed that on each succeeding occasion the array of evidence in support would have been greater; whereas, as a matter of fact, the only evidence that has shown any material growth is that for the opposition. The temperance parties of the town are to be congratulated upon their persis- tence in opposing the application, for no greater truism can be uttered than to say that Runcorn can do without any more licences until there is some radical and efficient reforai of the licensinz laws. Local option etc, in my opinion is played out; and the best control of the drink traffic, will be the taxation of distilleries, breweries, and the retail of the article itself ; with revenue licences for everyone of good character, whether for brewing or retailing, the whole being subject to police supervi- sion.</p>
        <p>Referring casually to the Runcorn Free Library, I would like to impress upon the inhabitants the ex- cellency of the institution. The number of volumes it contains exceeds 4,000 and few towns of a like size can beast of a library so well eqaippad. The range of subject is itself difficult to define, but one can find between either one or other of those 4,000 covers thoughts unique, startling, interesting, in- structive, and even iac3mprehensible. What I woull particularly wish to do just at present, how- ever, is to impress upon our people the treasure the reference department of the Library, when it is fitted up, will unfold to them. The subjects em- braced are comp!ete and comprehensive and will well repay an interest taken in them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FOOTBALL PROSPECTS: RUNCORN'S OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL PROSPECTS: RUNCORN'S OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>In preface one cannot help but refer with plea- surable pride to the glorious achievements of the club last season; and, indeed, talking generally, the last year both from a financial and sporting point of view was an uncommonly goo I one. Success, fol- lowed success, and in the cal "championship" was emblaz )ned for thk3 second tim3 nooa the Football Standard of the "Reitails." Years ago Runcorn established a reputation as Ruby f »tb Were, and last year the world was shown that the "sinew and muscle" which thrive beneath the "Beacon-head" were still capable of the achievements of:that which thrived beneath the same old sandstone long years ago. lEven yet can we in:tly boast that Runcorn has the metal and the ability necessary to continue a giant in the lani of footballers, and waen the town can place its team "united" against the com- mon fee, few there be daring enough to question the result. But in that word " united" hangs the whole issue, and it plays a none too prominent part in the answering of the all important question; " What will Run- corn do in the coming SOASOII ?" Ask other teams' supporters, and they unh4sitatingly say, in that most unmistakable football parlance, "0:1! Rancorn? Well they will go down the nick !" Everyone— ourselves excepted—expects that; an I, assuredly,if we cannot face our rivals with a united front, even the most enthusiastic of us must admit there is some truth in it.</p>
        <p>To commence with,thera has ben a little friction between the players and the Committee. 0, yes. It was about money ! That generally follows after a good season. It was sooa settled, however, and now can as soon be forgotten. But is this the only rift in the lute? From what little I know—and I never an credited with knowing much—there will be quite a number of little differences between the club and the players before the end comes— the season's end I mean.—Thus, it is clear, it will be wise on Runcorn's part not to allow last year's suc- cesses to intoxicate us. The player-3 especially can take this to heart. They were well treated last year,as evidence:the fact that their earnings week in and week out totalled it is said to something like 18s 9d all the season through; and, given a united team,willing to do their best for the Committee the possibilities of the coming seasan;are quite as great.</p>
        <p>Now for the players. The old forms and f5c35 are out again. Houghton is still to figure at back and shows little sign of wearing. J Butterworth. T Warder, Wilkinson, D Jones, C Butterworth, will doubtless take the brunt of the virlrk at the three- quarter line; and will be backed up by several other promising youngsters I c3uld name. Faulkner and Jolley stand next as a matter of course. No Run- corn team ot the present day would be complete without them—and as reserve there is the sturdy Joe Nixon,andfßichardson. Of the pack,the all im- portant part of a team, I can only say they are a daisy lot. " Look at 'am ?" Walker, Darlington, Farmer, Reece, i) Hughes, Tomlinson, F Gayer, Lightfoot, Langley. and a host of otaers. What better material coule be found? None I Let me, however, just for a moment touch a chord in the minor key. Taylor—the ever popular" Alf "—will be missed from the front rank,and though his place will be filled by another player, there isn't a man in the team who would not sooner have him there than that the honour to fill the vacancy should fall upon. him. To continuo, however, we now see the posi- tion of the team is: It is not so far behind in effi- ciency, so far as individuals go. to what it wis last year. Yet again will I ask" What of the unity ?" Ah ! thereby hangs a tale.</p>
        <p>Of other teams in the Lancashire Competition I would now say a word, and without dealing with the units of "the rivals of Runcorn" will deal briefly with them as a whole. Oldham ot course come first. And as a premier team of the North their position is unique. They have, if anything, a better team than last season, and intend to make things fly. Runcorn can only expect, at the most this season, to divide the points with that team, and if that is done I for one will be well satisfied. Broughton Rangers is in my opinion the team from which Run- corn must fear most this season,as I expect them to top the League. Swinton, St. Helens, Warrington, and Widnes, will be the other.; to fear. They will, no doubt, be going strongly all the season through, as all have made some important additions to their list of players. Taking a general summary of things, Runcorn " United" should stand second to none, and again the cup should find a resting place this side the Mersey; but Runcorn as I know and feel she is likely to be this coming season will have a struggle to get any higher on the list than third. The way to succeed then is clearly for the team and committee to be long suffering and give a bit both ways.lf this is done 1. fear nought,and Runcorn will be top. But" I h'ae my doots. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>AN APPRECIATION OF "OLD BRUNSWICK."</head>
        <p>AN APPRECIATION OF "OLD BRUNSWICK."</p>
        <p>The Rev. S Ii Raveuseroft writes from Dundee,South Africa, to Mr. Daniel Bisbrown (his brother-in-law) under date Angust 2od :—'• I have read with avidity the accounts that appeared in the Runeern papers of the centenary celebration of June 15th, 16-,h, and 17th. Every Runcornian of Methodist connection ought to feel a thrill of honest pride as he thinks of " dear old Brunswick." Some of my earliest recollections are of Brunswick Sunday School. I went there as a very tiny lad, and was in Mr. W Rushton's infant class. I graduated, and was taught respectively by men whose hearts were fired with love to Jesus Christ, and hence considered it their duty aud privilege to " Feed his lambs," Messrs. Simpson and Kent were the superintendents when I joined the school. I remember Mr. W. Dutton, too, as superinten- dent, and Mr. William Eaton as teacher of the young men's class, and much else which I need not mention here. In one of the newspaper reports I noticed it was stated at one of the gatherings that nothing had been received in the shape of a letter from the Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft. Your communication respecting the demonstrati'oe came too late for me to send a reply by June 16. h. Please make this known in some wsy to my old friends, the lovers of ' dear old Brunswick.' Never while memory fulfils her office shall I toreet the good impressions made upon my mind and heart in the fl-st Sunday school I attended. My wife was a teacher for many years in Brunswick Sunday School,where she had formerly been a scholar. She keeps a warm corner in her affections for the place that is so sacred to us both. Many a time did Mr Simpson take her by the hand when she was a little girl, and lead her, with other juveniles, in triumph to her school, in which he took swell a deep interest, We are delighted with the photos you have sent, and also with the sonvenir. We wish that we could have been witn you to have shared in the joy and triumph of those days. Our duties, however, kept us here, but we were with you in spirit."</p>
        <p>MAIL CARTS AND BASSINHTTES.—J. S. GinrANo, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, War- rington,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BREWSTER SESSIONS</head>
        <p>BREWSTER SESSIONS</p>
        <p>Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The annual Liceßsing Sessions were held for the Petty Sessional Divisioa of Runcorn, at tho Court Rouse, on Monday last, when the magistrates presmt incluJed : Messrs A Thomas (chsirmin), S Taylor, F J Norm in, A R nrcian and William Handler, TEIE POLICE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Pearson preseated his annual report. There were, he said, CI licensed victualers, 52 ueer- sellers, and six others who Pell off the promises, total 102, giving an average of 248 of the population to each licence. Two licensed victuallers have been proceeded against during the year, one of whom was cenvic' ed, be- lag the same number proaende 1 against and a decreaee of one, convicted in the previous year. Two beerse.llers have been proceeded against and two convicted, being an increase of one proceeded against and one convicted. Two hundred aad one persons have been proceede against for drunkenness during the year, 192 of whom were coavicted,as compared witn 137 persons proceeded against and 133 convicted during the preceding year, being an increase of 61 parsons proceeded against and 59 convicted compared with the precthrag year. The 201. persons are classed as follow viz., 33 sailors, six boatmen, two Irish farm labourees nine tramps, and 151 labourers, &amp; m., of Runcorn, trio latter figures including 29 females. Superintendent Pearson added that he was directed by the Chief Con- stable to draw the attention of the Bench to the back premises of the Fox Tavern, Aleock-street, Runcorn, which are difficult for proper police supervision. Also to the fact that the Albion Inn, Nelson-street, has been closed since last Oetobar and notices have not been served of any intention to transfer such licence. Super- intendent Pearson further mentioned that there was an increase of 14 sailors proceeded against for drunkenness, and a decrease of one female. There was an incre ise of 50 chemical labourers procleded against; fordrunkenness. The latter he attributed to the improvement of trade which had taken place during the year in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RENEWALS.</head>
        <p>THE RENEWALS.</p>
        <p>The Superintendent continued, and said that he had no instructions to oppose any of the renewals with the exception of the Fox Tavern, Ale Ak-street. 0 winZ. however, to the difficulty of providing proper police supervision over the back entrance of this place there would be some opposition. Mr. Raw, the representative of the Bedford Brewery Company, said they were prepared on th-ir parc to erect a gate that would make the yard a self-contained one, and do all that was necessary. The Bench decided that the renewal would too best considered at the adjourned licensing sessions which would be held on the 24th Septemb2r. This was agreed to. THE ALBION INN BEERHOLTSE.</p>
        <p>there was no application foe toe renewal of the licence of the Albion Inn beerhouse, Nols3n-street, whicn it was explained was a '69 licensed house. Ur. Davies (Messrs. Le.vis ani Davies, Liverpx), who appeared for the severa.l temperance partioa in Cal town, sail he wonli give there and then verbal notice of objection to the renewal of the licence. He also sub- mitted that because the house had had n) tenant for same time would not be sufficient grounds for the Bench to refuse a renewal if one was applied for. Bat it could be proved, ht thought t 3 the satisfaction of the Bach. that the house was not of the rateable value necessary to the requirements of thi Act of Parliameat, f)r a '69 house.</p>
        <p>The Magistrates Clerk observal that no notk!.. of obj,!ction had been served and probably the owners of the house were not aware of tie circumstances. The Bench therefore referred the case to the adj )urned sessions. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TRANSFER LISr.</head>
        <p>THE TRANSFER LISr.</p>
        <p>Th 3 following transfers w.3ra grantei Ditch- field, Cleveland Hotel, Runcorn, to E iward Karon ; Thomas Gregory (deceased), Halfway House, Halton, to Martha G:egory ; Alfred Taylor (deceased), Wheatsheaf, Runcorn, to Mary Taylor ; Hanry Moroi, Mtllstone Inn, Runcorn, to John Leylani ; James R)binson. Three Crowns, Runcorn, to Josoph Blackburno ; John Vornin, Elephant and Castle, Halton, to Eiward Inxindra Jones. Temporary authority to sell was gran,d t) Mrs Hough, of the Rifleman Hotel, Couch-street, whose husband had died the previous Friday.</p>
        <p>THE BALFOUR MEET APPLICATION .RF,FU3ED</p>
        <p>FOR THE 6ra TIME.</p>
        <p>This was an application made by Mr. Bremmer, solicitor. Liverpool,on behalf of Mr. Gaorge Partineon grocer. &amp;c., of Balfour-street, Runcorn, for an ofFlooer lice see for the premises occupiel by him at that place.— The application was oppoeei by Mr. Davies, solicitor (Messrs. Davies and Lewis, Liverpool) on behalf of the British Women's Temperance Association and other societies in toe town.—Mr. Bremner, in his address to the Bench, said that the premises were owned by Mrs. Partington (the apolicaat's wife) and wore assessed at a rateable value of £22. The applicant was a most re- spectable person and one fit to hold a licence, should che Bench see fit to grant one ; and had been carrying on business at the place for the last 12 years or so. The applics.tioa was being made for the purpose of supplying the customers with drink for their meals. As the Beach must be far bett.r acquainted with the district than he (the speaker) it would only be necessary to point out that it was a growing one, and many new buildings had been erected and many more were in course of erection. He was not going to deal with any other public house or the distances one stood in ralatiorC to the other ; but he de- sired to show that it was situated in the Heath Ward.and the only otker house in the: yard was what had been named the"Traveller's Rest."He would from its inaccess- ible position (on Runcorn Hill), have rather callei it the "Eagle's Nest." (Laughter.) That house, he was sure, was not sufficient for the needs of the ward, in which there were 410 persons on the register, and counting four or five for each house, would give them a figure far:io excess of the percentage the Superintendent of Police took for each house in the division. Referring to tied houses he did not know the opinion of the Bench re- garding them, and was atta.cking no particular house or brewery company when he said, that the result of tied houses was the granting of a monopoly—quite con- trary to anything English. Mr. Partington was prepared to show that the majority of the people in the neigh- bourhood required the licence, and would swear that he was .tinder no undertaking with any brewery company, or anyone else to sell or do away with the licence. As a matter of fact, bearing a rumour that was abroad to the effect, be had caused a circular to be issued contradict- ing it, and cautioning anyone using the rumour (the circular was produced).—Fiaally Mr. Bremner said he knew Mr. Davies, and that he bad to come te oppose the licence. He was well aware that Mr. Davies opposed everything; and no doubt his friend would tell the Bench it was the sth or 611 time that the application had been before then and it was positively indecent of Mr. Partington to come again and trouble the Bench with the application. All he hoped.: however, was that the magisterial modesty of the Bench would be sufficient to survive the shock and that the application would be granted. ' Mr. George Partingtoo, who bore out his solicitor's statement, in cross-examination, said : He thought it was quite as reasonable for him to COlll3 before the Bench and make the application as it was for Mr. Davies to come and oppose it. Oa three previous occasions the application was backed by an understanding with the Wilderspool Brewery Company, but not on this.—By Mr Bremmer : rho advances were made by the Brewery Company. The Chairman expressed no wish to hear the opposi- tion, and said the Bench were of opinion there was no new evidence,and would decline to grant the application. THE BRUNNER GUILD HILL-</p>
        <p>- 01bha1f of Mr. William Garrett, keeper of the Brunner Guild Hall, St, John-street, an application was made for a licence for music and dancing for the Brunner Guild Hall.—Mr. F. A. Lake appeared, awl said the application was made °wine, to the fact that some dancing classes were held there, and the parties not knowing the exact position regarding the law, and having no desire to de anything outside the law desired a licence.—The Superintendent of Police stated that if the place was licensed it would be subject to police supervision.—He had no obj!ction to the licence.—The apnlication was granted.</p>
        <p>Warrington Petty Sessional Division.</p>
        <p>The annual licensing sessions for Cse Warrington Petty Sessional Divisbn, were held in the Court House, Newton-le-Willows, on Thursday. Mr. T. J. Gillespie presided, and the other magistratc_,s on the bench were: —Colonel Wood, Mr. J. W. Collinczwool, and Mr Brown. THE REPORTS BY THE SUPERINIENDENTS.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Higginbotham, in submitting his annual report and returns for the Warrington Potty Sessional division, said the total number of licence holders was the same as last year : licensed victuallers, 47 ; beer sellers (on premis-s), 14 ; beer sellers (off premises) 3 ; grocers and wine licences, 2 ; total, 66. There was one application for a ne w licence :—William Attfield, of 7. Qaeen-street, Earlestown, for a grocer's and wine licence to sell by retail at a shop, at 7, Queen-street, Esrlestown, spirits and wine to be consumed off the premises. In the comparative return which he gave of licensed persons and persons proceeded against for drunk3nnos9 during the past ten years it would be observed chat there was an increase of eight persons proceeded against for drunkenness compared with last year. A return was also given of the number of the persons proceeded against for drunkenness on week days and Sundays. Generally the licensed persons had conducted their trais3s satisfactorily and there had been few com- plaints. Superint-ndent Cocker submitted his report on the Wigan portion of the Warrington Petty Sessional Division. He stated that there were withtn the town- ships of Golborne and Haydock, 32 licensed persons, viz: , 13 inn-beepers, one spirit grocer, and 18 beersellers (13 on and five off the premise.) being a decrease of one as compared with lasc ye sr. 154 persons had been pro- ceeded against for drunkenness and drunk and disor- derly conduct, being an increase of 13 as compared with last year: There were no applications for consideration. The houses.generally speaking, had been well conducted and he had no objection to make to the renewal of any of the licences.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the magistrates were sorry to se ibat there had been a slight increase in the au:nber of drunken cases. RENEWAL OF THE EXISTING LICENCES.</p>
        <p>No objection was raised to tne renewal of any of the existing licences and the magistrates renewed them.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR A GROCER'S WINE LICENCE</p>
        <p>Mr. L'ington said he had an application which had already been foreshadowed by the Superintendent's re- port. Mr. William Attfield wished to apply through him for a licence to sell wines and spirits to be consumed off the premises in respect of his house and shop in Queen- street, Earlestown. Mr. Attfield had recently removed to a Hew block of property in Queen-stnet and he now wis4el to have a licence t) sell wiaes and spirits off the premises.</p>
        <p>Thq Chairman : I understand your application is simply for the tr4o3ler of the licence which the applicant held for the old shop to toe new shop. Mr. Longton : That is so ; but it has to be male in the form of a new application. It was not Mr. Attfi2ld's intention to apply for the renewal of a licence he held for the old sh,p. He th ought he would b 3 able to satisfy the Bench that Mr. Attfi3A had all the qualifications necessary to hold a licence.</p>
        <p>William Attfi-ld was then called and statei that he at present held a licence for the sale of beer, wine and spirits off the premises. It was not his iutentinn to apply for a licence to sell beer at the new shop but only wines and spirits. Although he had held a licence to sell beer he had not sold any for 20 years. The Chairman: You simply want the licence trans- ferring to the new shop. The Clerk said the applicant was not applying on this occasion for a licence enabling him to sell beer, but only wine and spirits. ECICIOQC9 having been given that the shop was rated at £3O per annum, Mr. longton was aboul to call evi- dence as to character, when the C3airman said it was nnt necessary. Lie had known Mr. Attfield for a good many years.</p>
        <p>Stip;rintendent Higginb3tham said he had no objection to make to the application. Tao application wa3 granted. TEMPORARY Auraoarry TO SELL AT HEATEI-ROAD, PENKE</p>
        <p>Mr. Crampton (Mr. Arthur Browne) applied for tem- porary authority to be granted to Thomas Roller to sell beer at the off-licensed premiies in Heath-road, Penlath, in lieu of Thomas Philip Francis.—The application was granted.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN IRURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting ot the District Couna was held at the Workhouse' Dutton. on Tuesday, when there were present: Messrs Joseph Knowles (chairman) Admiral Jones Parry, .lessrs. S Woadward, T Wright, J B Chadwick, H A Talbott, C E Liaak3r, W Fletcher, T Lewis, J Tudor, J U Kydd, J Mclntyre, J Edwards, E Clarke, J Mounfield, T Antrobus, U Mawdsley, J Whit- low, G Pilling, with Mr. G3orge F Ashton (clerk), Mr W E Hough (assistant clerk), Messrs Diggle and Swinton (surveyors), Mr James Farrington (inspector), and Dr J Adams (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>er511.060.11.- ••••,...1107.0.0000111M- – – • . ,</head>
        <p>er511.060.11.- ••••,...1107.0.0000111M- - - • . ,</p>
        <p>TRANSFER OF LICENCES.</p>
        <p>Jane Jackson widow of the late James Jackson, of Pcnklth, applied for the transfer of the licence of the Crown Inn, Penketh, from the executors to her3eif.— rue p &gt;lie bad no objeetila and thi appliceion was granted. John Carlin applied for and was granted Cie transfe: of the R3yal Oak. Golborne.</p>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>THE STAG INN, WALION, TO BE RE \IOVED.</p>
        <p>The annual licening me:ting for Cie Direibury Petty Se3sional Divison Wai ti-1,1 in cti,3 Cur t H Dares• bary, on Tu3slay. M. J. C. Pm., J.P., D , we the chamuan of ail Bench ani the other magistrWs present were :—Colonel RiclAw.ty, Mr. Frlst and Mr J. W. Knowles, TEIE SUPERINTENDENT'S ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Superintmient Pearson read ilia annual report with reference to the conduct of licensed victuallers and beer retailers in the Daresbury division. He et-oxen tha: there were 23 licegised victuallers 1 beerseller and 2 others who sell off the premises, total 26; giving aa average of 311 of the population to each licence. N) licensed vie- tuallers or beersellera had been proceeded against, and none were proceeded against in the preceling year. Twenty-seven persons had been proceeded against for drunkennrss during the year, 25 of whom were convicted,as compared with 33 persortsproceelel against and 33 convicted during the preceding year, being ads- crease of Rix persons proceeded against and eight con- victed. No publicans or beerh ouse-keepers had been ra pored to the police during the year for serving intoxi- cating liq tot t 4 children apparently under the age of 13 years. Toe police did not object to the renewal of the lic'nn9q nor to the application of I:s. Maddocks far the erection of a new house at Lower Walton in place of the Stag Inn. The Chairman said he thought they might consider the report a satisfactory one. It did no: call for any special comment. A decrease in the number of charges for drunkenness always meant an improvNalent and the Bench was pleased to see a decrease ins'oad of an in- crease.</p>
        <p>All the existing licences were then renewed. _ THE STAG INN TO BE REBUILT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne applied on behalf of Mrs. Fanny Maddock for a provisional licence to rebuild the Stag Inn, on the Old Chester.road, Lower Walton. It was proposed in consequence of the structural condition of the house and other details to rebuild it at the corner of the road on the right hand side going to Warringten—a removal of about 20 yards. It was at present in a very deplorable conditionsthere being no place in which to serve cyclists. Within the last few years a publican in that district had been indicted for refusing to serve cyclists, and therr- fore on that account alone a much larger place was required. Mrs. Maddock received lumerous orders for retreshments for cyclists, and she was not able to accommodate them. It was not proposed to build a stylish house, but one suitable for the requirmments of the neighbourhood and outsiders. The plans had been prepared by Messrs. William and Segar 0 wen,and they were not in any way extravagant. The drinking area of the old house was 861 sgiare feet and the drinking area of the new house will he 912 squire feet, an increase of 43 square feet. In the old house there was no I avatory,and the sleeping accommo- dation was inadequate. It was proposed „to pull down two cottages which adjoined the Stlg Inn and the present house would be converted into two cottages,which would be entirely apart from the new house. Thera would be a bowling green added to the new house. The Chief Constable and Superintendent had no objection, but thought it would be a great convenience to travellers. Superintendent Pearson said that was so. James Sullen, clerk in the employ of Mr. A.rthus Browne, proved the serving of the necessary notices and produced the tenancy agreement between Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Company (who proposed to build the new house) and Mrs. Maddock showing that she had an interest in the premises. The application was eranted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>THE. ROBBERY FROM PARR'S BANK.</head>
        <p>THE. ROBBERY FROM PARR'S BANK.</p>
        <p>Confession of the Crime.</p>
        <p>At the Mansion House, London, on Thursday, Charles Eiward Goss, 27, of Stanlev-road. Woodford, formerly employed as a clerk at Parr's Bank, Bartholomew-lane, London, was charged on remand with stealing on January 23rd, 1899. from a drawer at the nank, £60,610 in Bank of England notes. Prisoner when arrested for having passed some of the stolen notes denied the allega- tion, bat subsequently said that about three weeks after the robbery he found some £5 notes in the pocket of his coat, which was hanging in the lavatory. It was 8) long after tho robbery that he did not like to take them to the manager. Detective-Inspector Davidson, who arrested prisoner, gave further evidence. He said since prisoner I had been in custody he had voluntarily made and signed a statement admitting his guilt. He stated in this that he pleaded guilty to taking the notes but without criminal intention. He bad no intention of stealing the notes until the moment it was done, and it was caused by his accidentally finding the note drawer open. He should not have done it only that he met with a disappointment which upset him, and he was only too ready to obtain the money wherewith to accomp• lish the object he had in view. Directly he took the money he repented, and sent hack at the first opportunity all he dared to. namely £40,000. In the letter accompanying it he intimated that all would be sent back in tim. He was frightened to touch the notes for same time, but afterwards, wanting money, he endeavoured to cash them. He was robbed of about £9OO. Throughout his action showed that he had bees a fool, and not a criminal. He hoped he would be dealt with leniently, and was anxious to restore to the bank the loss they had suffered, which amountad to £l,lOO. The assistant chief clerk at Parr's Bank produced an anonymoua letter which wa received with the returned £40,000. It said the writer had to cell to see a relative who who suffering from delirium trenzens and that in his pocket he tound the notes. There were som, missing which no doubt he had squandered at a gambling hell in the West End. He would endeavour to see that his relative returned all the money. Evidence was given of the return of other stolen notes and of musoner endeavouring to cash notes at various places. Prisoner, who reserved his defence, was committed for trial.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTII UTE. The following farther examination results have come to hand from the Board of &amp;location, South Kensing- ton.</p>
        <p>BUILDING CONSTRUCTION :—&amp;dvancecl stage, Ist class, T G Wallington ; 2ncl class, G Pennington, A T Midge, J Armstrong, C E Bradshaw, H W Booth, W J Harm. Elementary stage, Ist class, H E Mostin, R Bowden, W Jones. 2nd class, A Crepper, J W Sandbach. Practical Chemistry: Advanced stage, 2nd class. H Green. Elementary stage, Ist class, F H Lea, F Hallding, W Andrews, H B Rogers. Second class, J Tudor, H Waterwortb, A G Jeacock, R D Moore, W Rey- nolds, H Stanway. Thelretical Chemistry, Elemen • tary stage, Ist class, H Stanway, H B Rogers ; 2ad class, R D Moore, H Green, W Reynolds, F R Lea. APPLIED MECHANICS, Elementary Stage, 2nd class W Baguley, G H F R Griffiths. Drawing in Light and Shade, 2nd class : E Cameron, J T Beck, J H Bank's, 11 Stubbs, El Moores, J H Austin.</p>
        <p>TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable for tropical climates ; it is light, clean, cool, clmfortable, and durable ; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITE &amp; WEIGHT. Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, fienshaw-stre2t, opposite top of Bold-street, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WALTON ARCHES.</head>
        <p>THE WALTON ARCHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. F. Perkins, writing from the Engineers Depart- ment of the L. and N.W. Railway with reference to the public road at Walton Arches, Warrington,stated he had received a letter from Mr, Stevenson on the subject and he wished him te point out that he was willing to agree to the alteration to the public road as shown on accom- panying plan B the stream being diverted and the 2fl pipe laid alongside the intended diversion of the occupation road, instead of under it as last proposed; on the condition that Sir Gilbert Greenall gives the extra land for the diversion of the public road, and allows the pipe drain to be laid through the residue of his land without charge for ease- ment, as already offered : the Rural District Council,in- demnifying the Railway Company against all claims of the Ship Canal Company, who appeared to be interested in the stage, road, watercourse, and land adjoining; and also against any claim that might arise in consequence of the public road being metalled with granite for only a width of 12 feet, and the remainder with cinders. As these works and the grant of land and easement will have to be the subject of an agreement, the preparation of which will take some time, it is proposed to include those works in the future contract for widening the railway, and omit the proposed lengthening of culvert towards the river from the present contract. The public road in the meantimeonly being altered as shown coloured red on attached plan D if the District Council will agree to this being done as a temporary arrange- ment. Mr. Perkins also asked for the return of the plans with the Council's reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PARLI&amp;MENTLRY COMMITTEE REPORT PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>THE PARLI&amp;MENTLRY COMMITTEE REPORT PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J Mclntyre intimated that the Parliamentary Committee met that morning and considered first the lquastiea of the recommendation of that County Cmacil, that the District Council should apply to the Loaal Government Board to put in force in their district such sections of the Towns Police Clauses Act (1874) so as to enable the Council to make regulations for the control of hackney carriages &amp;. Well, the matter, continuel Mr. Mclntyre, had been deferrel whilst the Clerk gathered information from other district councils as to what steps they were tatting in the matter and when he would be more able to define the position than he had been that morning. The second question considered was respect' ing the anxiety of the Urban District Council of Wallasey to lay water mains through a c Ttain portion of the district. He believed the Clerk had written for the plans, but up to that day they had not b 3 forwarded. Thus it was that question tw 3 had been. deferred. (Liti,7,hter.) The third question, and last, was the applic atinn of the Chairman of the Norley Parish Council to make provisions for a water supply for the Parish of Norley. That too had been deferred o Ring to the fast that the Council had mat evidence before them of proof that the rPcoest was in achordanle with the desires of the majority of the ratepayers of Norley. They therefore decided to refer the matter back to the Parish Council, with the request !they should call a ratepayers' meeting and ascertain their views ou the question first. The reoort was taken as given, causing seine amuse- ment but no comment.</p>
        <p>A SATISFACTORY REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. Mahal (medical officer) in his report stated there were no cases of infectious disease notified in the district, and so far as he knew the district was practi- cally free. There were, however, three cases of erysipelas.—This was considered satisfactory.</p>
        <p>THE THELWA.LL TANNERY NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Farrington, inspector, in his report made reference to the fact that he had visited Mr Long's Tannery at Thelwall and had the nuisance there under observation. Four settling tanks had been erected—or were being erected—in an enclosed part of the Tannery, and means were being provided for the periodical fltrfh- ing of the whole of the brook. It had besn tested by him the previous day, and if the results in the future would bq as good he felt sure the nuisance would be satisfactorily disposed of. With regard to smell there was and always would be a smell—s tannery smell— whatever was done. He would report further on the matter as it develot,ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WAS IT HORSE GREASE,? --- Action -against a Runcorn Boat Owner.</head>
        <p>WAS IT HORSE GREASE,? --- Action -against a Runcorn Boat Owner.</p>
        <p>Before His Honour. Deputy-Judge L. Morton Brown, at the Warrington County Court, on Thurs- day, James Shepherd, general dealer, Bridgewater- street, Lymm, claimed £9 35 from Richard Abel, barge owner. Runcorn, for goods sold and delivered. —Mr. Browne represented the plaintiff, and Mr. Burton, of Runcorn, appeared for the defendant. The defendant filed a statutory defence and paid £sl4s with a denial of liability.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, in opening the case, said the claim wis for two casks of horse grease. When defendant ordered the grease he did not say what he wanted it for There was no complaint for something like five months after the delivery. The casks were ordered in November and the first complaint was made in May. Plaintiff deposed that he was a licensed horse slaughterer at one time, but had since disposed of the business. Up to the time he received the letter from defendant ordering the grease he had no transaction with the defendant. The letter, which he received on November 13, contained an order for two clacks of horse grease to be supplied as soon as possible. He had only one cask ready and he sent it, delivering the second one subsequently. It was ordinsry horse grease such as was usually sold. He had not authorised anyone to sell it at 19s per cwt. He had charged at the rate of 263 Iper cwt. Ihe price had never been disputed until now. to his knowledge. Some time after the account was sent in he saw defendant, and be said he thought he had charged him a little too much. Witness told him that it was the usual charge, and that if he would settle the account that day he would knock off 21 per cwt. They afterwards had a conversation about a horse and eventually defendant decided to see the horse and said he would settle the account on the same. day. He (plaintiff), had charged as much as 274 fid per cask and as low as 153. At the time he supplied plaintiff he was offered a contract for the wholeof his horse grease at 249 63 per cwt.—Cross-examined: He had included the weieht of the casks in the cbarge.—lt was usual to de- duct that weight when the casks were returned, but in this case they had not been returned. _ _</p>
        <p>Wiliam Walker, sailmaker in the employ of the de- fendant, said be had to go to plaintiff's with regard to a pony which was being delivered to him and he asked the man in charge what was the price of tallow and be said 19s a cwt. Witness told the man that he required tallow t) mix with Stockholm tar fer mak'ng sails waterproof. An order was given t) the plaintiff's nsan and two casks were delivered. They opened one in March and it was " pretty fair stuff "for a quarter of a ca.k down, but after chat they found horse hair and fl'shings in it and they had to throw a lot of it away. The refuse produced was a sample of what was left after the tallow had been put through a sieve. Witness went to the plaintiff and told him it was not fit to use. The weight of the casks witness had weighed was 74lbs. In the bottle produced of a sample taken, one third was tallow and two thirds rnbhish.—Cross-examined He- was aware that the casks were filled tram the bung hole. The weather affected it.</p>
        <p>Richard Abel, the defendant, said he was a boat owner and builder of Runvrn, and had been in the habit of getting; horse tallow for waterproofing tarpau line. He had been having horse tallow for many years from the plaintiff's predecessor. In the tallow supplied by the plaintiff he found hair, hoofs and parings. Only little of it was fit to Use and it was not proper horse grease. The right price for it was 193. a cask. He did not agree to take it for 243.—Cross examined : Mr. Shepherd told him that he had sold the stuff for 244 6d.</p>
        <p>By His Honour: Did you complain about it until you were asked to pay?—l offered him 19i a cask before I knew the stuff was not suitable.</p>
        <p>William Richard Earp, chemical manufacturer and analyst, said he reeeived two samples of horse grease from the defendant. It was the foots or dregs of th. bast iustcad of the real tallow. It was worth about £2 or £3 a ton for refining phrposes He could not under- stand the stuff being sent out for horse tallow. It was a totally unmarketable article.—Cross-examined : What he saw was not ordinary horse grease. George Hewitt, tallow chiondler, Runcorn, said he saw one cask opened which had been about half used. A grest deal of sediment was In as well as hair. Edward Pritchard, sail maker, said he had seen samples of horse tallow at Mr. Abel's. It contained hair and sediment. There was very little tallow in it.— Cross-examined ; He examined it on the previous day, and about six weeks previous to that. William Hopwood gave evidence as to delivering the , sample of the stuff to Mr. Earp. Thamas McCue, formerly in the employ of Mr. Shop- herd, said he did all the buying of the horses and also took orders for the plaintiq. Mr. Shepherd told him to charge Mr. Abel 19s a cwt. for horse grease and he wrote a letter to that effect to Mr Abel. When the order came, Mr Shepherd told him to forward it on. The hair found in the cask was from a pig, and there might be a little of the pig in it. (Laughter.) They could not help getting rubbish into it. Witness told Mr Shepherd that the second cask sent to Mr. Abel was not fit to send away. He was rather ashamed to go again for another crier because of the bad quality of the second cask. After' the wijournment, His Honour conferred with the solicitors and suggested terms of settlement which were accepted. The terms were not stated, but His Hononr said they were fair and reasonable, and both parties were wise in accepting them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Board of Guar- dians was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday. There were present :—Bilessrs:J Moseley Williams(chair- man), J Meadowcroft, A R Norman, T Reynolds, Taylor ; the members of the Rural District Council ; with George F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk) and Joseph Cunnah (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>STATE OF THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE STATE OF THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Master's returns for the past fortnight showed there were 130 inmates at the last Board day. Since then two were admitted, nine discharged, one died, leaving 122 inmates in the house at the end of the fort- night, against 170 in the corresponding period last year. The number of vagrants relieved during the same period was 20, against 38 relieved in the corre- sponding period last year. WHAT WILL TER FATHER no? "Mr. B. Wellesley :Bremner, superintendent of the Training Ship Indefatigable, wrote as follows under date 16% August with reference to Ernest Wakefield : "I am sure my committee will be willing to cancel thie boy's indentures, if you wish it,and I agree that it is the father's duty to relieve the ratepayers of the charge for his son. I enclose copy of indenture." _ _</p>
        <p>Mr S. Woodward proposed that the Board of Guardian( make application to the Training Saip Authorities to liberate the boy ; and any expenses be charged upon the father.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Will that form a block to the father as these exoenses are very heavy. Mr. Woodward: If his father is true to his relation- ship to the youth he will not object Co payiag the whole of the charges for his education already paid by the Guardians. I adhere to the proposals I have made and if it blocks the case then I say the father's not sincere. Mr. Linaker : What expen3es are you referring to. Mr. Wood ward: The expenses in regard to his apprenticeship. There is this in it you will see the spirit in which the father takes it.</p>
        <p>The proposition was then atzreei to unanimously. PAINTING THE OUTSIDE OF THE HOUSE. Tenders were received for toe decorating and painting of the outside of the House, and that of Mr. Billington, Wellington-street, Runcorn, for £9O was:accepted.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL STATEMENT. Mr. S. Woodward, who submitted the financial state.. meat, said that out-relief was granted as followe Runcorn, £80; Budworth, £25; and Frodsham, £2l. THE PURCHASE OF INVALID CHAIRS. The Master reported that Sine!, the last Board day there had been a meeting of the Lrdies' Csnimittee. They reported they visited the House and found all the wards very satisfactory. They were unanimous, how- ever, in the request that their appeal for invalid &amp;airs might soon be attended to.</p>
        <p>The Chairmtn state.] that a small committee had been appointed to make tne purchases of the chairs. be report of the Visiting Committee was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE DOCTOR'S SALARY.---,AN INCREASE GRANTED. Mr A R N)rrnan reponel that a special meeting of the House Committee WA9 held, and the question of Doctor application for an increase of salary was considered. Tune was a great diversity of opinion expressed, and some were for a large increase and some for hardly any at all. Ultimately, it was almost unanimously decidld—'ie thought there were two opposed to the increase—the following resolution was agreed to :—"That the salary of the Medical Officer be increased to £lO5, the thctor to provide the drugs as heretofore." IGwas practically an increase of .£l5 IThe Chairman seco.mied the proposition, which was formally muved by Mr. Norman.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Meadowcroft, having apologised to Mr. Nor- man for his absence at the court, added he could not but think that the resolution just put had justified the question being referred back to the House Committee. He thought that the court had taken a very generous view of the doctor's application, and he hoped that it would be accepted in that spirit. Further, he thought it was quite what the doctor expected. Mr Linaker remarked that the WNW Offlear hal never said so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Meadowcroft stated the Chairman of the Board had stated that possibly such a sum would be satisfac- tory to the doctor and he took that as sufficient warranty for wing the statement. Of course, a good deal had been said at the last meeting in opp3sition, but he felt that some little consideration should be given the doctor ; though, had he only been led by the returns submitted for their guidance, he could not have agreed to that but he had gone further, and was in favour of making some consideration for the doctor. He bad no objection to the increase proposed. Mr. Linaker said he would like to make the observa- tion that his motion to refer the matter back to the Committee was because the Committee he referred to had considered the question on two previous occasions. The question was put and carried. Mr. J. Edwards; Mr. Chairman, you never asked whether there was any amendmentk The Chairman : 0 yes, I did. Mr. E lwards : I call it drumming it through. (Laughter.) Upon a vote being taken there were three against. TEE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD'S NEW REGULATIONS. Mr. A. R. Norman further stated that the House Com- mittee had considered another very important question which was the long circular letter which had been re- ceived from the L3cal G -merriment Board with regard to the conduct of the house, the treatment of children; and also the question of imbeciles. As far they could they were going to carry out the "points" in the circular. One thing however, that the Board bad formed a special resolution upon was the question of the treatment of the imbeciles ,and they had resolved "that in the opinion of the Board County or District institutions should at once be provided throughout the country for the reception of the imbeciles ; and that copies of the resolution be forwarded to each Board of Guardians in the county, as well as to the Local Govern- ment Board. They had bad the question of imbeciles before them for some time ; they were no: chronic im- beciles and could not be treated at asylums, and they thought that some institution should be provided. The places in which to take charge of them would not be so costly as providing institutions for chronic lunatics, and it was thought the more they could push the Govern- ment on the better, which was the reason the resolution bad been come to</p>
        <p>-- The Chairman thought that Mr. Norman would allow him to add that 9-10:hs of the suggestions of the Local Government Board as embodied in the circular had already been anticipated by the House Committee some two years ago, especially so with regard to indulgencies and the removal of the boys to a day school and various other things. The question of the imbecilev, however, was a much larger question than they could deal with. Mt. Fletcher seconded the adoption of the resolutioa which was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DELICIOUS SUMMER DISHES.</head>
        <p>DELICIOUS SUMMER DISHES.</p>
        <p>Rizine is prepared by a special scientific! process. Rizine is a more easily digested food than rice, and at the same time contains a larger percentage of available nutriment.</p>
        <p>Rizine contains more flesh-forming substances than rice, tapioca, arrowroot, and cornflmr. Rice is the favourite food of. half the human race. Rizicie is a better food.</p>
        <p>Here are two more Rizine Recipss. Try them, and you will be delighted with the result. THE ICING OF PUDDINGS. Ingredients :—One and a half pints milk, 4 yolks of eggs, / pint cream, cz gelatine, 3 oz. Rizine, sugar, and flavouring to taste, 1 teacupful of dried fruit cut in small ~quares, such as dried cherries, ci-ron, sultanas, pistachios, kernels, &amp;a. Directions :—Cook Rizine in milk sto 7 nliautes, then add yolks of eggs beaten up with sugar flavouring and a little cold milk, let mixture thicken, then pour into a basin, stir occasionally, and when cold add the gelatine (dissolved), the fruit and cream whipped to a stiff froth, when on point of setting pour into a crown mould; wnen firm, turn out and garnish with chopued jelly.</p>
        <p>NURSERY PUDDING. Ingrelients :—Three slices of bread or cake, 4 sticks of rhubarb, *lb. sugar, 4oz Rizine, 1 pints milk. Directions.—Cut the bread and rhubarb in 3-inch strips, cook rhubarb until tender with sugar and a little water, put a layer of bread at bottom of glass dish then a laver of rhubarb, and so on until the dish is full. boil Rizine and milk until smooth, about 5 minu'es, sweeten and flavour to taste, then pour over the fruit and bread. Serve hot or cold.</p>
        <p>Rizine is the most convenient form of food, and it should enter into the most conapotent parts of all dishes, as it is plain and inexpensive, and it can be employed in a large number of ways. The" British Medical Journal "ways : " Rizine is a light and digestible Food." Sold everywhere in 31. and 6.1. packets only. A single trial wilt confirm our statement that "there is nothing so good as Rizine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall has purchased from Mr. Frank Usher, of liebblc3thorpe, South Cave, the yearling hackney filly Maid of Drewton, sister to the young champion Lord Drewton 11. Captain Reynolds 3rd Dragoon Guards, who SO distinguished himst:lf the other day by the defeat of a party of 80 Boers, while conducting a recon- naissance in charge of a small detachment of the sth Dragoon Guards, served with the latter regiment says "The World," throughout the seige of Lady- smith. In recognition of his services he was lately promoted to a troop in the 3rd Dragoon Guards, regiment which seems likely to benefit greatly by the tranhfer to it on promotion of young soldiers 'who have made their mark during the war. Pro- motion has been exceptionally rapid in the 3rd Drazoon Guards, so that advantage has been taken of the opportunity thus afThrded for bringing in &amp; contingent of tried officers of great merit. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDEX THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STMT.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
