      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>PLEASANT _PARAGRAPHS. SOME people don't count the cost until the bill In overdue.</p>
        <p>MISS ANTIQUE: "It is not a crime for a woman to remain single." Miss Pert: "Oh, no; merely a miss demeanour." TEACHER: "Now, Teddy, is Jerusalem a proper noun or common noun ." Teddy "Tain't neither; it's an ejaculation, mum 1" CUTTER: "There goes the light-weight chain. pion of England." Butter: "That so? Who Is he?" Cutter: "My grocer." SHE (reading) : " Mice are fond of music, and will get as close to it as they can." He : " Just cut that out, and I'll send it to the girl in the next flat."</p>
        <p>RECRUITING Sergeant: " Do you know anything about the drill ? " Recruit: "Ay coorse. Didn't Oijist tell ye Oi wurked in a quarry these foive year past?" Miss THIRTYSMITH : "Do you know Mr. Softly ?" Sally Gay : "No ; who is he ?" Miss Thirsty. smith : "Oh, he is the gentleman who—hee hee ! —raves about me." Sally Gay : "Indeed A lunatic—eh ?"</p>
        <p>"DID Miss Flavilla seen; pleased when you asked her to go to the theatie " " Pleased? She wanted to keep the tickets, for fear something might happen to me." "GOLF," remarked the young man who is weari- somely blase, 'is one of the very few games that do not fatigue." " What is the reason for that?" "I never learned to play it." "WHY does the Baron look so glum? I thought he had just married an heiress." So he has; but he speculated a few days after the marriage, and lost the better half of his wife." _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>- - ETHEL : "Have you noticed how Lord Slabsides drops his aspirates?" Penelope: "Oh, but that's nothing to the way he drops his vowels—papa says he has got more than a dozen of his "DARLING," he cried, "I cannot live without you." "But," she replied, "my father is bank- :rupt." In that case," he despondently replied, "I guess I'll go and shoot myself." "The sighs of an old schoolmate is—er-- well, it might be called both meat and drink.' She: " Yes ; that's what you men usually do under the circumstance." He: "Eh?" She: "Meet and drink." "I HAVE cast my bread upon the water," she sighed, "but it does not return." He strove to comfort her. "Perhaps," he suggested gently, "it has not yet been recognised as such." "If my employer does not retract what he said to me this morning I shall be compelled to resign." "Why, what did he say?" "lie told me I could look for another place." BRIDE (tearfully): "And the honeymoon scarcely over! To think that he should speak to me like that!" The Confidante: "What did he say, dear ?" Bride: "He said I was unreason. ah:le ! "</p>
        <p>COUNSEL : Why are you 80 sure it was just nine o'clock in tile morning when you heard the shot I Witness: The newsboys had just commenced cry- ing the evening papers. "11.4.uniu," said a father to his little daughter, "your school report ia not nearly so good this term as it was last." "Well, dad, last term you sent Mrs. Fizzeton a couple of ducks, and this term you Oln't," said Alaudie, in an aggrieved tone. A Bria ADVERTISEAIENT.—A countryman walked into a newspaper office to advertise the death of a relative. "What is your charge?" he asked of the clerk. "We charge Bs. per inch." "Oh !" said the countryman, "I cannot afford that. My friend was Oft. 3in.</p>
        <p>LI'TTLE BESSIE : Can't I have a birthday party next week, mamma? Mother: Why Bessie, your birthday is'nt till next gummer. Little Bessie : I know, but Uncle Jack says ladies don't have any birthdays after they are 25 years old, so I want to have lots of them before it is too late. "I WOULD go with you to the end of the earth," he asserted, passionately. "Not with me, you wouldn't," she replied, coldly. "Why not ? " he demanded. "For two reasons," she answered. "One is that I'm not going, and the other is that there isn't any." When one meets the prosaic new woman, one has to be careful what he says. SAVED HIMSELF.—" This pie," remarked Mr. Stoneharte, "is not like the pies that my mother used to make." "There you go again," exclaimed fiis wife, bursting into tears; you are never satis- fied with anything I cook for you." "I mean by that," said he, "that my mother, good cook that she was, never made a pie equal to this. This is the best that ever came out of the oven." WHAT HE WANTED TO KNOW. A Scotch farmer met his minister one day, and said: •` I see ye hae got back frae the Holy Land, sir." "Yes, John; back safe and sound." "Pe often thocht," said the farmer, "I wad like to hear o' that place frae ane that had seen the kintra." "Well," was the reply, "I saw Jericho, Jerusalem, and the Jordan, and was up Mount —" "Excuse me," said John; "but how were the neeps (turnips) lookite down about the Jordan and the Dead Sea?"</p>
        <p>OFF HIS GUARD.—A neighbour missed corn from his garner; and his suspicions rested upon a reck- less fellow called " Sam. ' The victim, determiend to satisfy. himself concerning the identity of the thief, made a temporary bed upon the kitchen floor and laid down to watch. He was aroused from a partial slumber by a noise overhead, when he suddenly called out at the top ot his voice: "Sam I" "Hallo !" responded the thief, en- tirely off his guard by this sudden call. "Don't take more thso a bushel." "Then I shall have to pour it out, for I've got two in the bag al- ready:" EGG-JOKES.--At a small social gathering the other,. nignt somebody started the egg-joke e- roding. , "Did you ever hear the story of the hard-boiled egg?" he solemnly inquired of some one across, the table. "No," was the innocent answer. "It's hard to hat," said the joker with mach gravity. You a help smiling at these things, nd after the laugh died down somebody else iprurig this: "Did anybody hear about the egg in the coffee?" "No," said an obliging some- body. "That settles it," remarked the funny man, blandly. Of course there was another laugh, and then aiurief silence. It looked as if the egg- jokes had been exhausted. But presently a little woman at one end of the table inquired in a high soprano voice if anybody present had heard the story of the three kggs. The guests shook, their heads, and one man said "No." The little woman smiled. "Two bad," she said. OLD Fattuaß X had been attending a political meeting. As he came up the path to the farm- house his wife came to the door and met him. " Pid ye have an excitire time ?" she asked. " Tremendous," he ens wered. "I s'pose ye've got everything settled." "No. To tell the truth, everytitine'i jtq about as much in doubt as ever." "Well, .1 s'pose iz.'s your own lookout. But 1 must say I hate to see ye wastin' so much good energy." "How d'ye mean?" "Pigs has to be fed an' wood has to be chopped, politics or no polities. I don't want you to neglect your country when. there's anything that really calls for you. Bo I none you will bear in mind that every time you wave your hat in the air because you get a little excited you're usin' up muscle that might have done good service choppin' wood, and that every time you yell hooray about nothin' in partie'lar, usin' up good lung power that would come in mighty handy to drive the plough with." "Mrs. TRELAWNEY," said Francis Wallingford, " there is something that I have for a long time wished. to say to you." The squ dcher of suitors looked over her glasses and frowned. She evidently knew what was corning, but after a moment's silence, she said, in hat most impressive tones : "Well, go on. What is it ?" "1---I love your daughter, Miss Gladys. I have reason to believe that she returns my passion, and I want to ask you to give her. into my keeping. Mrs. Trelawney's features hardened, and there was a cold, metal- lic ring in her voice as she answered : "What recommendation have you to offer for yourself? How can you convince me that you will always love her—that you will always think her beautiful ?" "She looks like her mother," said Francis 'Wallingford. "That is enough to convince me that her beauty will not diminish as her years increase. Of course I know that this can hardly be regarded as a final test. You have not reached the age at which women begin to lose their"— They were interrupted then, Mit he Lot the girl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.</head>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.</p>
        <p>Many ladies and gentlemen have by them old or disused 4;alse teeth, ,whilh might as well be turned into money. Messrs. R.D. and J.- B Fraser, 0! Princes Street. Ipswich (established since 183,3), buy old false teeth. If you send your teeth to them they will remit you by return post the utmost value ;or. If preferred, they will make you the beat offer, and hold the teeth over for your reply. If reference necessary, apply to Messrs. BACON &amp; CO., Bankers, Ipswich. ASK for. cuNNINOHABI'd HICIII.-CLAb'S IdINBRAL atlATARl3.—Advt</p>
