      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SHOCKING HOME AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A sad condition of affairs ex'sts in some Warrington homee,judging by the painful revelations which are made from time to time at the Borough Police Court in the cases brought before the :nagistrates by the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. A case heard on Monday may be taken as a sample.—John and Mary Hopkins, 11, Gaiden-row, were summoned for neglecting their five children,in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health. They pleaded not guilty.--Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.5.V.C.0., stated that the man was a bricksetter's labourer. In consequence of a letter re- ceivtal by Inspector Hackett regarding the whole of the property at this place, he and a constable visited the home of the prisoners, on August 24th. The allegation was that the children (Mary, aged nine,Margaret, eight, John, six, and Elizabeth, four) were continuatly running ala.mt the streets, half naked, and in a filthy condition. On his arrival at the house the Inspector found a num- ber of women there, who admitted that they did not know in what part of the house they slept the preceding night, for they had all been drinking. He ascerta;ned that ten persons had dept in one bed which was in an extremely bad condition. Dr. Bowden was called in,and on examining the children found that they were very dirty • They were also badly clothed. The house, he said, was unfit for human habitation. Both defendants were continually drinking.— Inspector Hackett gave evidence bearing out the above eatement. He said there was scarcely any furniture in the house, and the beds and chairs were mostly broken. The bedrooms were in a shocking condition, and the s'ench wes very had indeed. One of the women he saw told him that on the p-evious night they were all so drunk that they did net know where they slept.—P.C. Smith anii P.C. Clarke gave comborative evidence.—Dr Bowden statee that the coalition of th- house was not fit</p>
        <p>for anc human habitation. He described the condition of the children.—Both defendants promised to sign the poedge.—The Chief Constable said that was the male defendant's fifth appearance.—The Chairman (Mr. Birchal!), said it was a very bad case and it was surpris- ing that there was not more di,eace when people livid in Fu rh conditions. Defendants having promised to sign the pledge, and make an improvement, the magistrates decided to adjourn the case for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (tilderman Roberts) In the chair). Councillcr Smethurst and Mr. Hepher I. WIFE DESERTION.—Geerge Drinkwater. 70. Cartwright- street. failed to answer a summons charging him with desert- ing his wife Mary Drinkwater. of 122, Howley-street, on the 19th June.— Mary Drinkwater deposed that her husband was drinking heavily during May, and left her on the 19th June. She had received no money from him sines Kay last He was in receipt of 34s a week.—The magisteates decided to grant a separation order, and made an order of 15s a week. SATtruns.Y. -Before Messrs. Birchall (in the chair). R T Fairclough, and J W Hayward. IRREGULAR ATTENDLIME LT SCIIOOL.—The following parents were dealt with as under for fai!ing to send their children regularly to school :—James McKean, 11, Albert Street, fined 5s ; James King, b, I ythgoes Lane. 10s (two children) ; Henry Newton. 70, Lvthgoes Lane, ss, John Bran wood, 95. t;horley Street. 2s 6d ; John William Jackson 14, Manley-street. 5s ; James Tryner. 38, Catherine Street, 5s ; John Fiddler, 14. Ellen Street, 5s ; ueorg- Hill. 234, Winwick Road, 3s and William Worrall, 25, Wright Street, 6s. The cases were proved by Mr. William Jones, chief school attendance inei3ector.</p>
        <p>M3NDAY —Before Mr. Birchall (in the chat-), Councillor Smethurst anl Messrs. Hayward and Wright</p>
        <p>THEFT OF SUNDAY NEWSPAPEII9.—JOhIi Hayes, i 2, Morris- court, was charged with stealing from Bank Quay Station, at a quarter past six on Sunday morning, a qu mtity of news • papers of the value of 4s 4d. the property of the Dandon and North•Weatern Railway Company.—He pleaded guilty.— Detective Howells said that nine p,rcels of " Sunday Chronicles " were sent from London-red Station. Manches- ter, to Bank Quay on Funday morning, and on arrival were taken into the booking hall. About half past six a youth in the employ of the company was going to the parcels office and saw the prisoner taking some papers out of a parcel. ya hen he saw he was being watched he put the papers back. The youth then went into the office, and when he came out the prisoner had dis- appeared. He noticed that a quantity of papers wer3 missing from a parcel. he told a pointsraan named Kane what he had seen. Kane walked up Parker-street, and saw the prisoner carrying a parcel of papers. He called after him for one. and prisoner turned and drew one out Kane accused him of having stolen them, and he said he had not. Kane proceeded to walk back with him, when prisoner threw the papers down and tried to escape. A gentleman who was passing ou a bicycle went for a police offi ter, and prisoner was given into custody. When charged at the Police Station he admitted taking the papers He (Detective Howells) conld prove If necessary that prisoner had taken papers before. He formerly worked for the company in the goods warehouse.—Mr. Taylor, manager of the Warrington Branch of the" Chronicle," stated that papers had teen stolen from the parcels on Sunday mornings frequently uring the last six months —P:isouer was sent to gaol for a month. _ _</p>
        <p>THEFT Fnoat A DRUNKEN MAN —Bernard O'Rourke, 12, Dolman's-lane, was charged with stealing a pair of trousers, valued at 10s. 6i.. the property of Patrick Delaney, 25, Queen-street, Golborne, on Saturday night. —The Chief Con- stable said Delaney came to Warrington on Saturday at noon and purchased the trousers. He had some drink dur- ing the afternoon, and about nine o'clock he fell asleep on some steps in Bank-street. He then bad the parcel with him. When he awoke it was gene, and the next time ha saw it was in the police office. At the time the man was asleep two boys saw the prisoner lying near to him, and after wards saw him take the parcel and tilde it.—Delaney gave evidence in support of the Chief Constable's at ttement. He said that he had lost 32s as well as the trousers.—The Chief Constable said this was the first time he had mentioned the fact. He should say that the facts were gradually dawn- ing upon him.—Patrick Preston (15) and Joseph Mannion (12, stated that they saw the prisoner take and hide the parcel —D,teetive Meacock said he found the parcel carefully concealed under a large board in a disused entry • ehind the Crown and Sceptre public-house. Prisoner was arrested,and in answer to the charge said"! didn't."—Prisoner now denied that he had committed the theft.—He was sent to gaol for a month. ILLTREATING A HORSR —Thomas Turner, 62. Scott-street, was summ,med for illtreating a horse on August 21st by working it whilst in an unfit condition. He pleaded guilty. --Inspector Brown stated that at twelve noon on August 21st he saw the defendant in Queen-street driving a horse attached to a coal lurry, on which there WAS about a ton of coal. Seeing that the horse was lame, he examined it and found it to be in a very bad condition. It was suffering</p>
      </div>
