      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINEH, URIDA/ AUGUST 81, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEH, URIDA/ AUGUST 81, 1900.</p>
        <p>great pain, and was totally unfit for any kind of work. Defendant told him that he wanted to see him to ask him , whether it was fit for work.—Defendant, who said ha had sold the horse to a man who would have it 'destroyed was fined ss. NOT FIT TO BE AT LARGE —William Oldhira, of no settled abode. was charged that he being a person of suspicious character was found in th, early morning on the enclosed premises of Dallam Forge,being there for an unlawful purpose. Accorling to the evidence it appeared that he had been found 'behind a stack of iron. He said that the manager had sent for him, but that was found to be untrue.—Prisoner, who appears to be of a weak mind, said he hal permission to go into the forge:—The Chairman said the man was not fit to be at large.—The Chief Constable stated that when he was at the court on the last occasion the magistrates thought he was of unsound mind.and sent him to the Workhouse for fourteen days. The standard there, howevr, could no be a very high one, for the man was set at liberty and this WAS the way he made use of it,—The Chairman said he w wuld make further inquiries. As matters stood they could only send the man to gaol for a month. DRUNKENNESS.—The following were fined for this offence: William Lytligoe. Morecambe ; George Smit' ,: 47, Old-road ; William Stewart, Liverpool, and Jane Kelly, 345, Liverpool- road. _ _</p>
        <p>t REACH OF THE PEACE.—For being disorderly in Dallam- lane on August 18th, Elizabeth Lythgoe 26, Dallam•lane, was baund over to keep the peace for six months, and WAS ordered to pay the OOHS. CHIMNEY ON FIRE rinlOthV Monaghan. 12, Stanley-st., was fined is for allowing the chimney of his dwelling house to be on fire on August 21st. TUESDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair), and Mr.</p>
        <p>Birchall.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A STABLE. —Alfred Austen. 96, School brow, was charged with stealing from the stable of the Unicorn Inn In Gee-street, at eight o'clock on Monday morning, a ch.ck rein and body brasses, veined at 15s, the property of John Taylor.—The Chief Constable said that shortly after six on Monday eyeaing the prtsoner went to the shop of Mr. James Leslie, secondhand clothes dealer 21. Pinner's-brow, and offered a body brass for sale. While Mr. Leslie was looking at it, his wife came into the 'shop, and said the prisoner had sold her a chec,r rein and body brass in the morning. This excited Mr. Leslie's suspicion, and he communicated with P.C. Taylor, who was in the neighbourhood. The officer went in search of the prisoner and arrested him and brought him to the shop. There the prisoner was very restive and endeavowed to escape. He denied having stolen the articles. He was snbse- quently brought to the Police Station, and admitted to him (the Chief Const..ble) that he went to Mr. Taylor's stable yard for the purpose of obtaining employment, and seeime the articles there stole tlhem. This was prisoner's first offence.—He was sent to gaol for 14 days with hard labour. CHANGED HER MlND.—Patriek Walder, 14, Percival's--fold, was summoned on a warrant for assaulting his wife, Mary Walder,on Aug , 22nd He pleaded guilty.—Complainant said that her husband had taken the pledge,and said be would bo different. She could not pay her was if he were sent to gaol. —The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. G. F. F White) : Are you prepared to pay for the warrant ?—Complainant said she was —Mr. Birchall asked her why she took out a warrant and then came pleading for the case to be withdrawn P—Corn- plainant asked for a separation order and an allowance.—The Chairman said her own action had prevented them granting her that. The case would be withdrawn on payment for the warrant (6) 6i).</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Birch&amp;ll (in the chair), Mr.</p>
        <p>Geddes and Councillor Sniethur.t.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTING A DETECTIVE IN COURT.—Annie Smith. Manchester-lane, was charged with havinz. been disorderly in Peter-street on Tuesday night, and also with having assaulted Detective Delahay.—The police officer stated that he brought the prisoner to the police station for having been disorderly, and in the police offi3e she struck h.m across the face.—Prisoner who has a bad record, was sent to gaol for a month. She asked the magistrates why they did not give her six months and have done with it While being taken out of court she struck Detective Delahay. The ..:hairman ordered hur to be again placed in the dock, and sentenced her to an additional two months' imprisonment.—Prisoner informed the Bench that she could do that on lier head. A SHOP PFST.—Levi Redfern was sent to fa.ol for 14 days for begging in Sankey-street on Tuesday evening. It was stated that he begged from shop to shop, and when arrested had 6d in his possession. It was his 13th appearance.</p>
        <p>istening to considerable conflicting evidence the Bench dis- missed the case. No costs were allowed. MINOR OFFENCES. —John Cooke, of Princess-st., was fined lOs for using obscene language.—Priscilla Lange, Re- gent-street, pleackd guilty. and was fined 10s for a similar offence -Thomas W'orrall. ,or first offence drunkenness was fined 5s ; William Woodroe, Egerton-st., was fined ICs for being drunk and dis mderlv. A BOATWoMAN'S ABDUCTION. —Elizabeth Ann Nicholls,was summoned by her daughter Betsy Osborne. for ass iult. Com- plainant alleged that defandant and herself were cominz out of the yard of Simpson D ivies and Co,. coal merchants when her mother said something to her and hit her on the face with her ciosed fi,t.— Defendant stated she did strike her daughter but it was only a slap on the face with her open hand. Her " daughter's people " were always laughing and , jeering at her and her husband. What she had said to her daoghter was "you are all id, ht now yen are amongst the 40 thieves." and that was true for they hit taken her daughter and kept her for three days laefore her husband married her. —The Bench dismissed the case on payment of 4s 6d costs by the de'endant. LETTER WRITING EXI.RAoRDINARY —Patrick Wheelan,was charged with begging in Bridge-street, Runcorn on the 24th August, and pleaded guilty.—Police-Sergeant S. Gregory proved the case and said thet upon searching the prisoner he found the two following letters : —"Patrick Wheelan will be thankful if Lady Brooks can give him any assistance. He is unable to work at present and his been emp oyed on the estate some few months ." This WAS addressed to Lady Brooke.-- From Dr. McDougall to Lady Brooke" ran the next latter "Please Mr. Nixon lam sorry to trouble you. I have seen Mr. Evans and want to get to Manchester so could you help me to get there as I am out of work here. I want to go to Manchester to get work. Yen have helped me before so I trust you will help me once more. Patrick Wailing." This was addressed to Mr. Nixon.—Miss Florrie Gregory of Church-street came forward and stated she had written the last letter at the prisoner's dictation as a favour for him. —The Chairman Yes, that is all very well, but you have put *' From Dr. McDougall to Lady Brooke." Don't you know that you have no right to use someone else's name ? Witness : I have jut put it down as he told me. —The Chairman: But you have no right to do so. It is a very serious thing to write it down like that. —Superintendent Pearson: Do you know anything about the man P—Witness No.—The Chairman : You should not write anything like this again.—The Superintendent: I believe the letters have not beea used—Prisoner said that he was sorry for the occurrence.but would give no explanation for his oonduct.He bad been .in the !Workhouse about two weeks previously and before that he was looking for jobs where he could find hem. —The Bench decided to send prisoner to Knutiford for 14 days' hard labour. —Prisoner: What can you do in a town like I his, sir, when you can't get lodgings.—The police how- ever took the prisoner " down " arid probably he is com- fortably lodged by this.</p>
