      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. J. C. Parr (chairman), Colonel bidgway. Mr. Frost and Mr. J. W. Knowles. UNLIGHTED VEHICLES.—Richird Welling was fined 5s for not having a light attachel to his lorry at 9.40 on the 9th August at Newton-by-Daresbury.—Bernari Maginn, of White-street, Warrington, appeared in answer to a summons ch,rging him with not having a light at- tached to his furniture van at Walton at 10 15 p.m. on the 25th June. —P.C. Lee stated that he met the defendant In charge of the van at Walton superior. The defendant stated that be was returning from Helsbv. He thought hs would have been in Warrington before dusk. He tried to use a candle but it went out. As soon as ha got over the swing bridge into Warrington a borough policeman stopped him and he hAd to pay a fine for that. The magis- trates imposed a fine of ss.—Heary R. Rowlands was sum- moned for a similar offence at Preston Brook at 11.33 p.m. on the 19th July.— The defendant got into the box and said he was not the person in charge. It was his brother.—The case was dismissed. No NAME oN CsßT.—Edward Hanks of Thelwall, was ordered to pay the coats for not having his name painted on his cart at Stockton Heath on the 21st August.—George Hormbey of Barnton, for a similar offence at Dutton on the 10th August was similarly dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Newton Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TIINRSDAY.—Bbfore Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman). Mr. Collingwaod and Mr. Brawn. _ _ _ __</p>
        <p>TRAP WITROUT LIGII.T.—RiChAni Williams, was fine,' 2s 6d and costs for driving a trap at Newton on the 19th July with- out a lighted lamp. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—The following were fined for being drunk and dismderly ; riaomas Envlish on the 7th August, at Newton; John Hinds on the 27th August at Earlestown ; Samuel Hill on the 13th Au4ust at Golborne ; Emma Ackers on the 11th at Havdoek ; Patrick Hinds on the 26th at Havdock ; Patrick Havey and John Snee on the lltn at Haydock ; John Hazteden. Henry Jackson and James Ryder, at Haydock. on thel9th ; William Ward on the 19th at (solborne. _ _ _</p>
        <p>DRUNICENNESS.—For being drunk at Earlestown, Samuel Walton was fined 2s 6d and costs ; and Michael Frayne was similarly dealt with for a like offence at Haydeca on the 4th August. DAMAGE TO GROWING CMPS.--JOhil Entwistle. a lad, was charged with haying oh the 16th August, committed injury t,-) clover roots doing damage to the extent of twopence by going on certain land belonging to William Longton.—Mr Longton, solicitor, prosecuted, and stated that as the defend- ant had pleaded guilty. it was not nec •ssary to call evidence. Mr. Lonton only desired protection for his growing crops. He believed the defendant kept rabbits, but that was no reoson he should feed them" witk other peopleis food "—The magistrates ordered the lad to pay the costa and cautioned him. _ _</p>
        <p>WIFE DESERTIoN.—James Mason, of Burtonwood, was charged with having on the 17th June, deserted his wife, Minnie season.—Mr Crompton (Ur Arthur Browne) repre- sented the complainant and informed the Court that the parties h•id only been married two years, and their married life had been a very-anhappy one indeed The defendmt had already been charged before the magistrates at Bolton with Assaulting his wife ond he was bound over to keep the peace. They then removed from Bolton to the complainant's home at Burtonwood. Her father had to complain many times about the defendant's treatment of his (defendant's) wife, whom he constantly thrashed. The defendant ultimately left her and went to lodge with a Mrs Williams. His wife went to him and asked him to maintain her but he declined to do so. That was the deser— tion consolained of.—The complainant bore out her solicitor's statement, and stated that the defendant was able to earn 2?. a week as a spring maker.—The cb4fendant said he was unable to follow that employment now and his wages were only 3s 4d a day.—The magiatrates made an order of 103 a week. _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>• AN INDEPENDENT WIFE —Joseph Cartwright was charged with having on the 20th August at Newton used persistent cruelty to his wife whereby she had been compelled to live separately from him.—Blizabeth Cartwright,the complainant. stated that she had been married ten years Every time he got drunk he Mused her. His conduct became un- bearable and on th 3 20th August she was com- p9lled to leave him* She did not wish the magistrates to make an order against the defendant for a money payment to her but she desired a separators o.der.—The defendant said his wife had broken up seven homes in ten years.—A. separa- tion order was granted. . .</p>
        <p>CLANDESTINE REMOVAL OF GOODS —Robert Kenny of Newton Common Was summoned at the instance of James Proctor Jones of Warrington, for clamiestinely removing goods from his house at 55. Clarence street, Newton Corn* mon in order to prevent a distraint for rent.—The complain- ant stated that the goods were removed between Sunday night and Monday morning, The amount of rent owing was £3 6s. He estimated the value of the furniture at £5 or £6. The defendant told him that a sum of £10) was corning to him in respect of an accident. s' NAP" PLAYERS NAPPED.—Jostart Abbott, Henry Burrows, Thomas Burrows, John Berkett, James Berkett, Thomas Clark. John T Harrison, Henry Aston ; all of Hay- dock were summoned on a charge of gaming with cards on the 18th August.—Mr Crompton. W rrington, represented the defendants.—P.C. Thompson stated that he and P.C. Johnson secreted themselves near the Catholic Chapel in Mayciock on the date named and saw the defendants sitting in a circle playing " nap." He saw money pass between them. On going towards theirs, the defendants got up. Witness picked up the cards produced from the centre of the ring. Some of the defendants gave wrong names and addresses Cross-examined by Mr. Crompton, witness admitted that be had himself played nap, but not in a public plaoe.—He was certain that he saw a two shilling piece pass between the defendants.—P.C. Johnson gave corroborative evidence.—Mr Crocapton said the defendants advised him that they were not playing cards at all. The cards fell from one of the defendants :pockets and the officer picked them up.—The defendant's gave evidence and stated that they were reading newspaper.—The magistrates fined Abbott 2s 6d and costs ann the other defendants Is and costs each. A WARRANT ISSUED.—BiIis Jones and Margaret Jones, of Edge-lane, Golborne, were summoned at the instance of Inspector Hackett, of the N.S.P.C.O , on a charge of cruelty to their children.—The female defendant did not appear and Mr Knowles, solicitor, who represented the society, askei that a warrant might be issued for her arrest.—This was granted and the case was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessicrs.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessicrs.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. A R Norman and W Handley. ANOTHER MAN WITH A CONSCIENCE.—WiIIiam A Stephen- son, booth-road, Weston. applied for an exemption from vaccination on behalf of his littie child. The grounds stated were •• conscientious," but witness did not know why he had the objection, and was still sure it was there.—The appli‘a- tion was granted on payment of the usual fee. FOWLS CAUSE " FOUL" LANGUAGE.—This was a case in which Mr. H N Linaker solicitor, Runcorn,appeareci to defend John and Ann Roberts, for charges of using threatening language towards Mrs. Clarke. a widow, and neighbour of th, irs residing on Cheater-road, Sutton.—From the evidence it appPars that Roberts's fowls, three in number trespassed upon complainant's garden to her great annoyance. This. complainant resented and showed her displeasure by chas- ing the fowls off with a brush, whereupon the defendants had come out and used the language alleged.—The defence was that Mrs. Clarke was a disagreeable neikhbour and had injured one of the fowls with a fire shovel; and that she and not the defendants had used abusive language.—After</p>
      </div>
