      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Inquest and Verdict.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Friday afternoon Mr. R. Dobson, coroner, held an inquiry relative to the death of Thomas Fox,who resided at 17 Fryer-street, and who expired on the previous day as the result of serious in- juries to the throat. —lllr. John Yates was the foreman of the jury, and Acting-Sergeant Gregory conducted the case on behalf of the police. Michael Mulhairn, living at 17, Fryer-street, said he was a dock labourer. The deceased lodged with him, and was also a dock labourer. He was about 40 years of age. He had been married, bur his wife was dead. Witness had known him for about 25 years. Last Sunday witness noticed that the deceased only took a small portion of his breakfast, which be thought was du,) to drink, the deceased having loNan imbibing very heavily. Some time ago he was strange in his mind. Deceased worked up to a week last Saturday, and witness was not aware he was short of money. a)ceased retired about 10 20 on Wednesday night. Prior to this Um-) he had talkad with witness but he did not then think he was strange. About 440 the next morning his dauglit'r Mary Ann knocked wit- ness up. When he wok) he rose up on his elbow ac,d SAW Fox lying on the broad of his back on the fl or. He went over to him and noticed his right arm was covered with bloo.l. There was a wound in his neck, and the blood was flowing from it. Upon looking round he espied a small pocket knife lying bed&amp; deceased's left arm on the floor. He could not say whether there was any blood on the instrument. Witness ran for Dr. Delaney,and he thought when he went that the man was dead.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : Has hs ever attempted to take his life before ? Wltness : According to information I received, he was found on the railway metals some years ago, and I believe be was brought Co the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>P.C. Nicholl stated that about 5 a.m on the previous day he was called to 15, Fryer-street. He followed Dr. Delaney into the house, and he found the deceased lying on his back on the floor. Close to ffis left hand was the knife produced. It was stained with blood, Witness knew Jeceased very well, aid had noticed him walking up and down the bottom of the street and rubbing his hands. His conversation, however, was rati•Jnal.</p>
        <p>Dr. Delaney stated that he wai called to the house on the previous morning. He got there about the same tima as last witness. He found deceased lying op the floor in a pool of blood, portions of which were clotted. He was quite motionless, blanched, and appeared on a cursory glance to ne dead. Witness had him transferred to the bed, and found that life was not extinct. lie ex- amined the wousd, which was partially filled with clotted blood. He sewed the wound tip. It was about an inch ia depth and about 1-1 inches in length. It did not interfere with the cartilage of the throat,as deceased took without difficulty some hold. He saw him later in the afternoon when he seemed to be better, although there were signs of inflammation and swelling. Ho called in the evening, when the man had passel away. Death was due to exhaustion following loss of blood.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: From the direction of the wound are you of opinion that it was self-iofilctcd ? •IWitne3s: Yes. There was a smillor wound about an inch further up the neck, as though there had been a second attempt. Mr. Yates: Have you any idea as to his state of mind?</p>
        <p>Witness: I have been informed that he was strange in his mind. Drink would affect it. Sergeant Gregory statPi that he had known deceased for the last nine years. Three years ago be was brought up for having attempted to commit suicide by cutting his throat. He was let off on promising not to repeat the offence. He was a very vspectable, hardworking man, except when he took drink- He had broken off the drink, and that he believed had affected him.</p>
        <p>The Coroner thought there was no doubt that the man did cut his throat. It seemed that he had previously attempted buici In, but there was no direct evidence as to the condition of his mind on the present occasion. The jury returned a verdict that the man committed suicide whilst temp Jrarily insane.</p>
        <p>RE-THE HUBBARD DOG.</p>
        <p>BY F. KLICKMA.NN. Old mother Hubbard She went to the cupboard To get her poor dog some bread. When she got there The cupboard was bare So the quadruped ate her instead. To this she objected As might tP expected— Bat he, with a shrug of his face, Said, " War Mrs. Hubbard, The state of your cupboard Has long been a national disgrace .</p>
        <p>" It's always the same— No poultry, no gamo, Not a vestige of knuckle of pheasant, Not a loin of roast ham, Not a wing of cold lamb. Not even a saustv of apricot jam— And I find it diy,inctly unpleasant ! "That greedy young Horner Sits smug in his corner. And gorges at pie all the day ; While horrid Miss Muffet Just lives on her tuffet, And gobbles her cords and whey. "But when I have bones They're lika underdone stones ! Though you know my digestion is shady ; And as for your biscuit, My t,.eth wouldn't ri,k it ! Such fossils don't tempt them, old lady ! "1 have made up my mini That whmever I find Na menu afliaml to the larder, I always shall eat you It's hard thus to treat you, But when I am hungry—it's harder ! "So now we'll adjourn All remarks, and return Our attention to lunch for a minute But wise mother Hubbard Reached down from her c ,pboard muzz.e, a,nd popped his head in it! WINDSOR MAGAZINE.</p>
        <p>Scholars' Treat at Daresbury, . The scholars attending the Board ..Schools of Daresbury, Moore, Hatton, and Preston Brook were on Friday given their annual tea party in the Dares- bury school. The children assembled about 230, those from Moore being conveyed on a lurry by Mr. Bradbury, those from Hatton by Mr. R. L. Warbur- ton, and those from Preston Brook by Mr. Henry Whitlow. In a field adjoining the Eichool, kindly lent by Mr. Billington, games of all kinds were in- dulged in until four,when an excellent tea was par- taken of by about 300 juveniles. After tea sports again occupied the time until dusk,when the enjoy- able treat was brought ta a close by the usual dis- tribution of sweets and buns. CADBUP.Y'S CO(01 is;iabsolutely pure, being entirely free from kok, malt, holm, alkali, or tiny foreign admixture Caution 1 The public should insist on having CADBURY'S sold only in Packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are oftene übstituted for the sake of extra profit.—Advt.--(4).</p>
        <p>RESULT OF • SPRING CLEANING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>We have received such a number of really good parodies on Kipling's celebrated verses that we should like v,ry mac to give the prize to several dozens of them. As that, how- ever, is impossible. we h tve hal to make a choice and have decided upon which we think is really the most appropriate. We publish this one, and also a selection of the others, ri!:4 adjudging them the best of those which have been sent )11 but as a representative selection. We desire to thank all those who have taken the trouble to send us contributiong towards this competition.</p>
        <p>X3was AWARDED to EDWARD H. DAVIgs /forborne, for the following verses :—BOOT</p>
        <p>ADVICE to HOUSE WII'ES.</p>
        <p>When you're started with Spring Cleaning—and you Want to do it clean</p>
        <p>(And you know you cannot do it with your mouth)— Will you kindly buy a bottle of our lurnitewrer Crean', Which is used in Haat and West and North and South. It's a powerful kind of compound, and it's very good to And you and Jane can take it as you find it. When it's used in active service, cleaning stains from " sticks,'' You will find it wont leave many marks behind it.</p>
        <p>Boots shop, Boots shop, shop of a hundred things An enterprising Company, it's all the rage to-dai Everything sold for the lowest cash (as you can see for yourselves), Then step In ladies, take your choice, and pAy I pay pay 1 There are wives who do it secret—asking no permission tO--• For their husbands wouldn't give it if they did; But suppose you use oar polish"—it will make things looit like new— And you need not stop the work " cos " there's a kid. You could do your cleaning casual—without worry—when he's gone, For if the beggar's absent never mind him. You might use our "Wills' Ammonia," which would take away the smell Of tobacco—which he always leaves behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc. You can manage so as later, when you look him in the facer You can tell him what he'll think is only fair, How that while he earned the wages you had tidied up the place And had polished up his special old arm chair. He's an absent-minded begg,r, and he may forget it all, But you do not want his kiddies to remind him That you locked them in the coal house while you sleanei his house and hall, And were bright'aing up the home he'd left behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>When you've started on your cleaning, when you're scour- ing all your rooms, Use " Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing," made by Wills, You can buy it for a shilling, and you'll find its pleasant fumes All the dirt and noxious germs quite quickly kills, Boots' an advertising beggar, but his stores are very great. And I and you must take him as we find him, He'd sooner have your cash thAn put it on a slate. And he's leaving all the other shops behind him. Cooks Boots, dukes Boots, Boots of a hundred kings. Boots of a market public sn,he'll fit them alLwe say; With Varnish, or Cr,ans. and Paint, and thousands Of other things, Ask for them all for your cleaning's sake and pay, pay. pay. There are girls who make no secret,'cos they've:no occasion to Boots Cream to polish furniture is good, That Gold Paint as made by Morgan is the finest thing that you Can gild upon old ormolu or wood. There are girls you talk to casual, will assure you that they love To polish up their brasses, and their tins, With Paste th it's sold at Boots, so much better, and above All others, covering multitudes of sins, Cooks' sins, dukes' sins, sins of a belted earl, Fifty thousand soldiers' situ, gone from theta astray, Buttoas and buckles and gun, will shine our khaki girl, Buy it quick for your polishing's sake, to-daY, day. day. There are families by thousands using Bootees Oaken Stain To furbish up bare spots around their floors, It saves them yards 01 carpet, for it will wash again When varished well to close the open pores. You Can polish up the chairs with tne Cream they sell Boots— You need not go to Africa to find him. He has so many branches, from scores of .good old roots, He is mostly at the front, nor looks behind him Dukes' Cream, cooks' dtain, Palish for metals. Gel Paint, These are the things you will ask for going to Boots to-day, With Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing, to save you frogs a faint, Take your cash in your pocket and pay, paysPaY* Nom de plume JACIOB WILSON, Nottinglinne•</p>
        <p>If your slavey goes and leaves you when spring cleisning'a coming on, And you cannot get another maid that suits. I will tell you how to manage with little extra toil, Just walk off to the neare.r, shop of !sOOTS. They are civil and obliging, you will very quickly find' Their goods are cheap and fresh and up to date. $ And you'll bay for say one shilling what most firm would sell for three, As they trade for cash and do not keep a slate. Pure Drugs, Gold Paint, Furniture Cream and Pills, Stuff tor saving elbow grease, powders and v 521010; roots, Stains and Polish of every kina, things that will Ogre all ills, So cease to fret, but put on your shoes aud jart - - try BOOrS. If you think your chairs and tables get a little Worse for wear, And the sideboard is not what it used to seem. Don't break your cups and saucers and say you WiSli you're dead, For 4d. only need be spent on Cream. With a rag apply it gently, any child can put it on, It's as easy if you want to stain the floor, Spend another 4id. on a pot of Varnish Stain, Then your home will look just as it did of yore. Soft Soap. Brunswick Black, Paste for a hunired tins. Oils and Paints of every kind, Cloudy Amtnellill too, ld EaC co ia of them certain todo its work, just as you're It will, So keep horns clean for your credit's sake spa you'll not rue. When you've finished in the bedroom, and the parlour andl the ball. Although you're well repaid doal think you're done, There's the pits and pans and kettles want a little clean' lug out, So again to BOOTS you'll have to run; Get a tin of lAetal Polish, just one penny does the trick, No stains or scratches you will ever trace, Then you'll sing about your housework and be glad Y are alive, And in everything you'll see a smiling face. Cash Chemists, Good Chemists, things at the lowest rate, Everything superiorlor a price thit always suits, 50,000 housewives sing praise of one accord, And never deal with another firm but 8000. BOOTS. BOOTS. F, JOHNSON, 4, Victoria-rd., Penketh, near Warrington'</p>
        <p>When the Springtime surreptitiously steals on us unawares With a sudden light of pale and sickly sun, Then the prudent British matron takes to polishing the stafth And there's lots of other house-work to be done. So in order that the mansion may be thoroughly made bright° BOOTS VARNISH STAINS and other things are bought. And the hubby's consolation is in thoughts of speedy flight. For he doesn't understand things as he ought. Hub, rub, scrub, scrub, ain't it awful work. So the hubby thinketh, though he really does know One little bit about it when he fancies terrors lurk. and the best thing he can do is to go, go, go Oh. the saving in the coal bills with thecoming of the SO"' When the black bacteria wobble in their flight, And the income tax collectors and the birds begin to sing. And the airty smoky ceilings turn to white. If you view it as you ought to it's a most congenial time. With UNRIVALLED METAL POLISH to the fore. In burnishing door handles and removing all the grime From the metals while tho women scrub the floor. Rub, rub, scrub, s rub, making things look like new FURNiTURE CREAM and BRUNSWICK BLAcx are articles that pay, Buy all you want at Boots, yoa'll keep that Wsllla view? And do your work when hubby's out of the WO' way, way. When the work is nearly over there's a transformation seehe' And you don't regret you've tackled it in time, n. As you realize the fact that now your house is nice and des To have left it dirty would have been a crime. Oh I the glory of the carpets which now look as goDI as ilevh Oh the brightness of the fenders and the chairs, surprising to the hubby as he takes a startled view. And the wife enjoys his wonder as he stares. What, what! what, what Ate done the whole lot in a - (Heaven seems to open to the guilty man YIP scoots) aeret Wonderful 1 how did you do it? Tell me your S— pray, " I did it all with things I bought from 110°'" Boots, Boots." C. P. JONRS, Solicitor, 6, St. Andrews-street, Cambridge'</p>
