        <p>(Near Lowton St. Mary's Station) Bolton Road. Ashton Vulcan Village Burtonwood</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The increasing popularity of the annual athletic festival promoted by the Warrington Police Force was fully evidenced last Thursday, by the large at- tendance of spectators and the increased number of competitors for the valuable prizes offered. When a policeman takes anything in hand, thoroughness generally characterises his treatment of it,and this was certainly the case with regard to the sportr, on Thursday. The arrangements were complete in every respect, and an enjoyable afternoon's pro- ceedings were conducted with marked promptitude and order. Beautifully fine weather favoured the event and about four thousand spectators gathered on the enclosure Au excellent programme of events was submitted and it wasgenerally conceded that the festival was the best which the police have arranged. The B)rough Band was in attendance and enlivened the proceedings with a cho'ce selection of music. In addi• tion to the nu-nerous events, a grand gymnastic display was given by members of the Wartingtoa Gymnasium, under the direction of dlr.Kinsley,the popular conductor. The display included performances with dumbells, Parallel bars, horiz3ntal bars, singlesticks, barbells, quaTterstaff, Indian clubs and vaulting horse. The pro- ceeds are to be divided between the Warrington Infirm- ary and the District Nursing Association. The officials were the following:—President,Mr L Tal bot, Chief Constable ; vice-president. D Aective Superintendent Monaghan; judges,Councillor D Tinnion, Mr Stanley Wilson. and Mr Harvey; stewards of the course, Chief Inspector Phillips, Inspectors Taylor and Rowbotharn,Sergeant Cheeney andiSergeant Williamson; marksmen. Messrs A Booth, Wylie and J Babbitt, starter, Mr T Wainwr,ght, Salford Harriers : secretary, Sergeant William Evans, Mr E Pickavan ce, N.C.U., official timekeeper, Mr. Walter Platt, N.C.C.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>POLICEMAN'S ONE HUNDRED YARDI HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>POLICEMAN'S ONE HUNDRED YARDI HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Those who witnessed this, the first item on the pro. gramme, must now be convinced that we have in the Borough force, a numty r of first-class sprinters,and that be who runs away with a policeman at his heels will have but a meagre chance of getting very far away. In the first heat, P.C. Robinson, whose name has figured in quite a number of athletic pro- grammes recently, ran strongly and easily out-distanced his opponents. P.C. Pennington gained second place. Starting scratch man in the second heat P C. Whittaker gained first place, and Detective Barlow was a good second. The third heat was won by P.C. Newey and P.C. Wood respectively. The final was an exciting event. The handicapping had been very carefully attended to and a keen struggle for supremacy was witnessed. Detective Barlow succeeded in getting to the tape first, but owing to some mistake in the firing of the pistol by the starter it was decided to run the final a second time. Detective Barlow outdis- tanced lois opponent and carried off the first prize. P.C. Pennington was second, P.C. Whittaker third, and P.C. Wood fourth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>100 YARDS OPEN HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>100 YARDS OPEN HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>There were 36 entries in this event which was raced in nine heats. After a close contest R,Mcro'racken, Fair- field Harriers (6) ran in first, the second place being secured by N W Wilson,Salford (8), who was only a foot behind. C W Roberts, of Liverpool (10) caine in third. The final was run in lOisecs.</p>
        <p>440 YARDS FLAT RACE.</p>
        <p>The 440 yards flat race was productive of some capital racing. Tnirty-five competitors had entered, most of whom took part! The final was most exciting. William Growcott, Crewe Tally Ho (24), breasting the tape first, being three yards in advance of J Inglesfield. of Runcorn Harriers (32). Half a yard separated Inglesfield from J Lovelady, Waterloo Ear- Harriers (32). Growcott, on finishing the race, fell down in a state of exhaustion and took some time to revive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ONE MILE NOVICE BICYCLE RACE.</head>
        <p>ONE MILE NOVICE BICYCLE RACE.</p>
        <p>There was a stiff fight for suoretnacv in the one mile novice bicycle race. In the last lap the issue lay between B Hughes, Sale Wheelers (105), and C Evans of Chester (65). Both riders rode in splendid style, Hughes coming in winner by ten lengths. The third place was obtained by S B Watm, of Manchester (70), E Clayton. of War- rington (100), came in second in the third heat, but he fell bohind in the final.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>220 YARDS OPEN HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>220 YARDS OPEN HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>P.C. Robinson of Warrington competed in the 220 yards handicap, which was open to all polies of the United Kingdom. lie was scratch man but succeeded in ob"aining third position in the final. P.C. J Scholes of Manchester (13) came in first beim?: followed two yards behind by P.C. El Stephens, of Liverpool (12). Five yards separated Stephens from Robinson. The time recorded was 24 4-sth seconds. CATCH-THE-THIEF RACE.</p>
        <p>A novel and amusing feature was the catch•the-thief race. P.C. Carter took the part of the thief and attired in plain clothes and carrying a bundle weighing Th. he was pursued by P.C's, Pennington, Newey, Phillips, and Deteclive Barlow who wore their uniform. The "thief" dodged about briskly but was eventually run to earth by P.C. Phillips. ONE MILE BICYCLE HANDICAP (MEMBERS ONLY).</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting events was the one mile I oicycle handicap which was confined to members of the Rorou,,h Police Force, the first MiZA b-ing the Royal Court Cup presented by Mr F. Crabtree. P.C's. Whittaker and Richardson, and P.S. Smith were the competitors: Whittaker WPS seratch man.whilst Richard- son and Smith had starts of 200 and 180 yards respectively Smith was overtaken after the first few laps, but Richardson made a plucky fight for the Cup. Whittaker forged ahead in the last lap,and just managed to win by two lengths. Whittaker having won the Con twice in succession it now bnomes his property. Had Richardson ridden a higher geared machine he would have stood a be'tn chance of wresting the cup from its present owner. The time recorded was 3 mins. 24/ secs.</p>
        <p>ll!treating a Lunatic at Rainhill.</p>
        <p>At St.. Helens, on Monday, George Waddington, an attendant in the employ of thelßainhill Asylum, was summoied for illtreating Henry Wrigley. Mr. Garner, clerk to the visiting justices, said that on Friday, July 20, Dr. Simpson, on going into the ward, saw Waddington atrike Wrigley three times. Wrigley was a noisy and degraded oatient, and had spat into Waddington's face, but Waddington had been told that while in charge of such men he was to use every forbearance, and on no account to strike them. Mr Riley, who defended, said the man Wad- dington was married, and had one child.He had lost his work at the asylum. He was very sorry he had committed this act. The consequences having been so serious, he asked the Bench to consent to inflict the minimum penalty of £2 and costs. This the Bench agreed to.</p>
      </div>
