      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SWEEPSTAKES.</head>
        <p>SWEEPSTAKES.</p>
        <p>Sn,geant W Crawford, of the Hartford Company, Northwich, kindly presented a silver teapot for c )mpeti- don. There was a good entrance, the feu; forming a sweepitake. Five sho:s were fired kneeling at 200 yards. The following is the result : —l, Lance-Sergeant J F Elwards, teapot, 25 poi4ts ; 2. Corporal J Mort, 24 ;3, Sergeant J Rushton, 24; 4, Sergeant D Edward, 24 ; 5, Colour-Sergeant W H Harvey, 24 ; 6, Private W Gregory. 24; 7, Lance-Corporal Sadler, 23 ; C, C)rporal W Griffiths, 23.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SHOCKING FATALITY AT WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING FATALITY AT WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dat;son, Coroner, held an inquest at the Weaver Hotel, Weston Puint, on Friday, on the body of Thomas Shaw, aged three years, son of William Shaw, boatman, who met his death under very sad circumstances that 111 0 Vain.. The rather of &amp;ceased Said he resided at Malkin's- bank, Sandbaelo, and was captain of th-3 narrow boats Wilfred and Four Brothers. About one o'clock that day he was having his dinner i the cabin. Two children were paying with the deceased in the boat. Witness heard the exclamation " Come to Tommy " ! and he rah to declase 1, who was lying in the bottom of the beat. I,Vitness picked him up, and found he was bleeding from the ears. The mast was lying in the bottom of the boat. It was not on the child when he got there. He thought it had probably struck deceased and bounced away. After witness had piclred him up deceased gave three "snatches" and died. The mast was fastened to a beam with a chain. It fitted into the beam and the chain was passed round to secure it. It was possible the children had been playing with th 3 chain. He could not see anything else to account for it, as he made it perfectly secure when they left Sandbach. The boat was being towed when the accident happened. By Inspector Hicks : The boats were occupied. His wife was at the helm steering, and she could see the children playing from where she stood. Martha Ann Shaw. mother of deceased, stated that she was steering the boat that day about o:::e o'clock. Her husband was finishing his dinner. There were three children playing in the boat : Mary, aged eight,Xlartha Inn flee, and the deceased. Witness could see along the craft. She did not:see the mast fall. Deceased was playing round about the mast by himself, and his sisters were in the forepart of the boat. After the mast fell witness shouted. She could not herself stir out of the hatches, being dazed. The mast was a very heavy one. The rope by which they were towed was fastened to the other boat's mast. William Charlesworth, labourer at the Sand Quarry, and residing at Weston, said he was turnint, out to work at one o'clock when he saw the father of turning run down the planks and call for assistance. Witness jumped aboard. The father had deceased in his arms, and be asl ea witness to take him. The mast was lying in the 1&amp;t. He never saw whether the mast had been upon Lim or not. The child breathed twice and then died. Tea Coroner explained that the deceased had died from a fracture of the roue of the skull. He then briefly reviewed the evidence.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death." Whilst the evidence was being taken both the fatly.r and motner sobbed b:tterly.</p>
        <p>Two Conscientious Objectors to Vaccination. At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday before Mr. J. C. Parr and other magistrates, Ernest Smith Garland, of Lower Walton, made an applica- tion for an Exenagtion Order under the Vaccination Act, for his child. He stated that he had a con- scientiou3 objection to vaccination, believing that it would be prejudicial to the health of the child. —The exemption order was granted, the Chairman remarking that he thought it was a great mistake to make the application Mr. A Booth, Butter- market-street, applied to Alderman Sutton and Mr. Birchall, at the Police Court on Tuesday, for a cer- tificate exempting his child from vaccination. This was the second application of the kind made by Mr. Boath. He stated that be had.a conscientious objection to vaccination. Alderman Sutton said it was remarkable that with all the experience people had of .the evils of non-vaccination they still be- lieved in it. The application was granted.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES —Greatly reduced. —3. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Advt, SEGONDHAND CYCLES to be cleared chean, from 3e.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warriugton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
