      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>CHINA.</p>
        <p>Reported Great Defeat of the</p>
        <p>Boxers.</p>
        <p>A " Standard " telegram from Shanghai, of August 29, says :—According to reports current along the river, the Reform party, under Kang-Yu- Wei, are actively preparing for a rebellion. It is rumoured that Yuan-Shi-Kai has gained a great victory over the Boxers and defeated the Imperial troops from Pei Tsang and Hosidu, killing 1,500 men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Pekin's Terrible Plight.</head>
        <p>Pekin's Terrible Plight.</p>
        <p>A "Daily Mill "telegram " from Chefu, dated Wednesday, says :—The first Europeans to arrive from Pekin say that the condition of the capital is indescribable. Wreckage and deaecration are every- where. The cemeteries have been destroyed, and the graves of foreigners subjected to terrible treat- ment. Hundreds of bodies lie in the streets.</p>
        <p>It is suspected for the most part they are of those supposed traitors to the Boxers' cause who have been mutilated and murdered.</p>
        <p>Large numbers of foreign troops, including 2,000 Italians, are arriving at Taku and are being promptly pushed up to Pekin, as it appears rein- forcements, are still needed there.</p>
        <p>The Railway War in Wales.</p>
        <p>Prospects of a Settlement.</p>
        <p>A Cardiff cprrespondept,, telegraphing yesterday, says :—The prospects of settlement of the Taff Vale strike to-day are reported as hopeful.—Tbe men's delegates have approved of Sir W. Lewis's sugges- tion to allow the company one month to dispense with imported labour.</p>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S CRICKET. Close of Play Scores. LANCASHIRE v. LEICESTERSHIRE :—Lanca- shire, 317 all out. MIDDLESEX v WORCESTERSHIRE:—MiddIesex 193 ; Worcestershire, 76 for 0. SURREY v. DERBYSHIRE :—Derbyslair3 325 all out. SUSSEX v. KENI' :—Sussex, 365 for 6 ; 220.</p>
        <p>GENTLEMEN v. PLlYERS:—Gentletn:n, 259; Players'9l for 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Botha' Plight to the Hills.</p>
        <p>Boers Demoralised.</p>
        <p>LOURENCO MARQUES, WEDNESDAY, AUG. 29.</p>
        <p>President Kruger, who is still at Waterval °Eider, is reported to have said that he will remain there till the end.</p>
        <p>The Boers are very demoralised, and their horses are all played out. Five thousand women and children are in Bar- berton, and a train load of the less seriously wounded burghers have arrived there.</p>
        <p>Boer reports appear to confirm the news of their utter rout at Machadodorp.</p>
        <p>Natives report that the British have occupied</p>
        <p>Lydenburg. A telegram, dated Pretoria, Tuesday, says : General Botha has f alien back to the formidable hills north-east of Machadodorp. French is still trying to outflank him. De Wet's followers are said to be tired of the war and disgusted with their ..•,emmander's tactics. Thirty eight of them sur- rendered the other day, preferring to go to Ceylon rather than to keep the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>MISHAP TO THE LIVERPOOL</head>
        <p>THE MISHAP TO THE LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>A Well-known Warrington Captain Wounded and Captured. The report of the mishap to the Liverpool Regiment and the wounding of Captain Plomer, who was also taken prisoner, has occasioned considerable regret in Warrington, where he was well-known and highly respected. Captain Plomer was well-known in zricketing circles in Lancshire, J foT many years _was attached to VII WArr;?lgton Club, where ne first tattle hie appen,raiice in 1894. When stationed at Orford BarraCks he was a reeular playing member of the War- rington first team. Last season he played for Warring, ton in several matches, but latterly he cast in his lot with the Western Club, whose ground is situate at Eccles. In 1895 he was drafted to the depot at Cork, and when he returned to Orford Barracks he was appointed adjutant. On the outbreak of the war he volunteered for active service, but his request was not granted until February 25, on which day he left Warrington with a detachment of the Ist King's Liverpool Regiment. when he and the men were given a most enthusiastic send-off. Welcome Home to Ambulance Men. After the cycle parade and carnival on Saturday, the members of the corps assembled at the Midland Hotel to welcome home two ambulance volunteers, Messrs. H. Holloway, and J. Newns.and also to entertain and thank the Walton Band for the valuabl6 services rendered to the corps on several occasions. Mr. S Wakefield catered in an admirable manner for the party, and everyone did justice to the good things provided. A " smoker" was afterwards held, during which the volunteers gave a little of their South African experiences. Songs were rendered in a very creditable manner by several mem hers of the party, accompanied by Mr. W. Roberts Superintendent Baldry presided. The Cheshire Regiment at the Front Boole Lodge, Chester, 27th Auzust, 1900.—Dear sir,— Yesterday I received a letter from Colonel Curzeis, com- manding 2nd Battalion, Cheshire Regiment, dated Johannesburg, Ist August, 1900, in which he informs me that a large quantity of the " comforts " sent out to the battalion were received on the 31st July ; and he desired me to express the gratitude and appreciation of the officers and men to all those who have so kindly re- membered them, by either working or presentinz consignments of warm clothing, cigars, cigarettes, and other things, which will add so much to their comfort.— I should be greatly obliged if you would insert this in your valuable paper, and oblige, yours faithfully,— J. H. HAMERSLEY (Chief Constable of Cheshire).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Captain Tupper, 6th battalion Kings Liverpool Regiment with headquarters at Warrington has been formally appointed a Staff Captain on the lines of communication in South Africa. Second Lieutenants Browne,Wheen,Rad ford,Wood, Hall and Grounds, of the Liverpool Regiment, have been promoted to the rank of Lieutanant in the corps The first named joined the regiment last December, but the others were appointed to their first com- missions only in the spring. Captain Ford Mellows has been appointed adju- tant of the 2nd Battalion Royal Lancashire Reserve Regiment which comprises ex-soldiers of the South Lancashire Regiment and the Liverpool Regiment. Second-Lieutenants Campbell, Jones,Shaw,Robin- eon, Fox, and Oppenheim,2nd Lancashire Volunteer Engineer Corps at St. Helens.have been promoted to the rank of lieutenant in the corps. Lieutenant Campbell joined the regiment in March 1899; Lieu- tenant Jones in December and the other officers named were appointed last March. Lieutenant W. E. Burton has been promoted Cap- tain in the 2nd Volunteer Battalion Cheshire Regi- ment. He was appointed Second-Lieutenant in February 1895, and promoted Lieutenant in July 1896.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Sir O. P. C. Walker, Bart.,has been appointed to command the new sth Volunteer Battalion Liverpool Regiment. He served several years in the Lancashire Yeomanry Be became Lieutenant in the Duke of Lancaster's Yeomanry</p>
        <p>Cavalry in 1877, and in 1885 was transferred to the Derbyshire Yeomanry in which he reached the rank</p>
        <p>of Major in July 1898. Lieutenant Earle, late of the old 2ad Derbyshire militia has been appointed captain in the 3rd batta- lion Cheshire Regiment and has taken over com- mand of a:company in that corps. Major Tripp, 2nd battalion Liverpool Regiment, will, it is stated, very likely be selected for the ap- pointment of Inspector of musketry in this district in succession to the late Major Wyld. Major Tripp was at one time serving at Warrington as adjutant of the sth battalion Liverpool Regiment. He has been 18 years in the army having been appointed to his first commission in the Liverpool Regiment in August 1880. He was promoted to field rank in the corps in February of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 31, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, AUGUST 31, 1900.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN 8c NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Ou Sunday the Rev D. Jones (superintendent minister) and the Rev P. R. Passmore (re-ident minister), bade farewell to the congregation ''at Earle,town and preached morning and evening re- spectively. At the evening servicri special music was rendered by the choir. 1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE, No. 1644.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting was held in Wargrave Hall on Monday, August 27. Bro G Clarke, .E S presid- ing. One brother was restored to membership. Bro Woods,L.D. read the annual report of Good Templar Orphanage. Sunbury-on-Thames. Bro Carke gave the report of district lodge held at Liverpool, the report after discussion being adopted. Resolved that a donation be given to the Lifeboat Saturday fund. It was decided to invite the No. 1 Convention to hold their next meeting in Earlestown. The lodge was closed by Bro Clarke in the usual manner fruit being afterwards handed ronnd, Greetings received from :-evchelle Lodge, India, and Brother Sergeant Major Devlin and Benjamin Tripp, South 'Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CHILD FOUND DROWNED AT HAYDOCK,</head>
        <p>A CHILD FOUND DROWNED AT HAYDOCK,</p>
        <p>At the Waggon and Horses Hotel, Haydock, on Monday evening, Mr S Brighouse, county coroner held an inquest on the body of Frederick Lawton, aged four years, son of James Lawton, labourer, Haydock Lock House, who was found drowned in the canal at Haydock on Saturday.—Mary Lawton mother of the deceased, stated that she last saw her son alive at half past two o'clock on Friday after- noon, and she brought him into the house from the canal bank, where he was playing with some more of her children. Sl'e placed them in the back yard. She did not miss the deceased until four o'clock when she called him to come to his tea. She gave information to the police, but she got no tidings of him until his body was found in the Sankey canal at eleven o'clock on Saturday morning. She thought he must have been playing on the canal bank and had fallen into the water. Ralph Lloyd, check weighman, stated that about a quarter past eleven o'clock on Saturday morning he was coming home from his work and saw the body of the deceased floating in the water, close to the lock gate, and opposite the deceased's parents' home. He took the body out of the water and carried it into the house. A verdict of" Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>A First class Life Company, offering special advantages in tbe way of investments and insurances, is prepared to receive applications for the pm s t of Resident Agent at St. Helens. A fixed stipend and commi.sion Would be offered to a suitable applicant who would be prepared to devote a fair portion of his time to the introduction of a high class business.— Adiress •• ARSURA.XCE," 28 and 29, S. S within's-lane, London, E. C .—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON FOOTBALL CLUBS. Prospects fQr the Y9r111:1: Season</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON FOOTBALL CLUBS. Prospects fQr the Y9r111:1: Season</p>
        <p>The four months that have elapsed since the close of last season appear to have been profitably spent by the management of the Earlestown Football Club. At lIIR time since Vie formation or the club have the prospects been so bright and encouraging as they appear at the present juncture. The composinon pf the team in the earlier matches will m*t probably differ but slightly from that which did duty when the curtain was rung down last season. Several new players have been signed ontand there will' therefore be plenty of capable reserves to call upon. The new men who have signed on are : Ashton (back), late of Handley; Bell (half-back), late of Parr; Morgan, the speedy outside right who last season played for South Liverpool. Bartley a forward from Flint and Sumner late of Haydock. Negotiations are also proceeding with Lowe,who last season kept goal for Haydock,and should terms be Fatisfactorily arranged he should be a decided acquisition to the club. A practice match'was played on toe Moaned on Saturday, the first team defence being pitted against the first team for- wards.</p>
        <p>To-morrow will witaess the opeoing of the season proper, when Stalybridge Rovers visit the Mesnes. Last season the Rovers were second in the Lancashire League Table and they should therefore m, ke the locals go all the way. The following Saturday Darwen are the visitors and this should again prove a bard nut to crack. Besides the Lancashire League Competition Earlestown have entered for the English and Wigan Cups,but in the case of the former, there are so many qualifying rounds to contest that they will have to fight hard if they wish to get into the Competition proper. In the first round of the English Cup they play Haydock at Earlestown. The team for to-morrow against Stalybridge Rovers is as follows: Chew, goal; Hayes and Cottam, back,; , Pennington. Green and Clibborn, half-backs; Morgan, Linton, Bartley, Leach and Settle, forwards. Newton had such a successful season in the Liverpool and District League that this season they decided to ' join the cowbination with the idea of introducing a superior clan of football to the patrons in the Newton district. Th 6 combination contains most of the best Welsh Clubs., four of its members have won the Welsh Cup, as weld as other strong _clubs in Lancashire and Cheshire and Newton will have all their work cut out to attain a respectable position in the League. They have however got together a splendid lot of Wayne, most of whom did duty in the .Liverpool and District League matches. In addition to all last season's players they have signed Dalton, a half back late of Burtonwood ; Lee late of Doocaster Rovers and rßrogan and Worrall, two speedy forwards who last Beason played for Gol- borne, They open the season to-morrow with Buck ey Vies. at Buckley, whilst on the following Saturday Tran- , mere Rovers visit Nursery Park. A reserve string has also been formed and has entered the Warrington and 1 District League. Earleatown Rovers have won the championship of the Warrington and District League for the last two seasons so easily that this season they decided to try their luck in better company. They accordingly joined the Liver- pool and District Amateur League which contains several of the premier amateur organisations of the Liverpool district. The committee are relying on precisely the same team that perform3d so well throughout the whole of last season, the only new man who has been signed being Houghton a full back, late of Golborne. In addition to the: Amateur League they have entered for the Warrington Cup and the Wigan Shield and with a little luck they ought to run well up in both these com- petitions. Their first fixture is on September Ist when they play Whiston at Whiston.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SPORTS AT ASTON-BY-SUTTON.</head>
        <p>SPORTS AT ASTON-BY-SUTTON.</p>
        <p>Local Winners.</p>
        <p>The annual military and athletic sports were held in the Higher Park, Aston,on Saturday, At the conclusion of the proceedings Mrs. Algernon Talbot distributed the prizss to the succmsful cowetitors. During the after- noon the Astcn and District Band played seleztions. Appended are the results :- 120 Yards Flat Race Handicap.—Heat w nn2rs F Andrews Frodsharn, 8 ; T smith, Runcorn, 6 ; G Beetham, Acton Bridge, 4 ; G A Linaker, Frodshana. 12 ; 8 T Whitlow Preston Brook. 12 : A Sinclair, Warrington, 7. Final : 1, Andrews ;2, Beetham ; 3, Whitlow. Won by two yards. inches. 440 Yards Flat Race Handicap.—Heat winners J Du-) bavin, Weston Point, 22 ;S T Whitlow, Preston ook, 45 G Beetham. Acton Brio ge, 9; T Craddock, Warrington, 14 ; F StrinJellow, Preston Broo k. 4) ;G Williams, Roaksavage 10. Final . 1, Dunbavin ; 2 Whitlow ; 3, Stringtellov,— Won by inahes : a yard. Mile Bicycic Race.--Heat Winners : W Walker, Norley.Bs J J Barker. Tarvin, scratch ; W Scott, Oakmere, 100; H Meers. Davenham. 10 ;T C Sutton, Wrexham, scratch ; J Plinston, Warrington, 15 ; T J Ridgway, Wrexham. scratch ; A Jackson, Helsby. 50; and R Braban, Warrington, 70. Final : 1, Walker ;2, Barker ;3. Ridgway. Won easily. Mile Flat Race.—Result 1. J Goodall. Frodsham, 65 ; 2. J Mather, Warrington, scratch ; 3, H Heward, Halton, 130. Inches ; yard. _</p>
        <p>, Horse Race (Fiat), half-breeds —Hight horses were entered for this event. The course ran as far as the river and back, being about two miles in length, The result was as follows : —l, T Shalicross's Primrose ;2: At Walker's Ladysmith, It Jones's horse Tommy came in first, but upon being weighed Out it was discovered that he had lost some things on the way, relueing his weight by about 61b., and he was disquali- fied. 11.1 A Cowap's animal was also put out of the race for a similar offence.</p>
        <p>Horse RAU over hurdles (open)--1, W Shepherd ;2, Trooper Kitchen, Cheshire Yeomanry. Shepherd's animal came in almost half-a-mile ahead of the winner ot the second place. Tent-pegging.-1. Sergeant Jones, Cheshire Yeomanry ; 2. it Sheaherd, ex-Yeoman.</p>
        <p>Lemon-cutting.-1, bereeant Lawton ;2, a Kitchen. Sword v. Sword —l, Sergeant-Major Campbell, Duke of Lancaster's Yeomanry ; 2. IL .) Shepherd. A most stubborn contest, Horse Race over hurdles (half-breeds).-1. W Shepherd's Dorothy ; 2. R Jones's Merry Gal ; 3, la Kitchen. Victoria Cross Race•-1, Sergeant E Jones ; 2, W Shep herd 3, U Kitchen. Horse Race (open).-1. Allan Vaughan's Ida ; 2, T Shallcrose's Primrose ; 3, R Jones.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Man's Substitute fora " Lighted Lamp."</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday before Mr Gillespie (chairman) and other magistrates,William Longshaw, a hawker, of Warrington, was sum- moned for having on the 11th August driven a horse and cart at Haydock without having a lighted lamp attached.—The defendant pleaded not guilty and stated that he had a lighted candle in a glass bottle at the time.—P.C. Thompson gave evidence as to meeting the defen- dant at Haydock. He was carrying a bottle which contained a lighted candle! He had cautioned the defendant previously and told him that the by- laws distinctly stated " a lighted lamp " and that he could not accept the defendant's light as such.— The Chairman said a candle in a bottle was not sufficient. The defendant must pay a line of 1.03 including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football. The Warrington Committee have chosen the following team to play against Whines on Saturday: Hallam, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Lawless, and Harris, three- quartets; Burgess and Duckworth, halves; Taylor, Bpardman, Antrobui, Eden, Mereweater, Scholtze, Swift, Fairhurst, forwards.</p>
        <p>The folhwinz is the tearn t 3 do duty for Widnes : Baty, back ; Huf4hes, Ripan, Nevitt, and Peacock, three qu4rters ; Bown and Thuglag, halvea ; and Harnp,on, A3pey, Power, Dprinelley, Leyshon, Purkis, A.llen and Riley, forwards.</p>
        <p>The following Senior Competition matches will be played to-day (Saturday) : Runcorn v Barrow. Salford v Millom St. Helens v Broughton Rangers. Swinton v Leigh. Widnes v Warrington. (To be played on the gr ,und of the first named in each case).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cricket team is to be congratulated upon its highly creditable victory at B)sedon on Satur- day. Thewlis, the local pro, was in fine form and was very successful with the bat and ball. It is a matter for regret that he failed by a single run to complete his fifty runs. The match was the only one which had been arranged this season.</p>
        <p>Rain Threatened from the commencement, but for- tunately held aloof and the match was brought to a satisfact)ry conclusion so far as Warrington were con- cerm-d. On a slow wicket the home captain batted first T A Bothwell and J A MacLarea being opposed by J W Bibby and Thewlis. Bota batsmen forced the game,but the fielding was of such a keen nature that runs were hard to dotain. With 15 runs on the board, Bothwell failed to negotiate a fine ball from Thewlis whilst in the next over Bibby sent the Bowdon captain back before he had opened his account. At the same total MacLaren jumped out to Tnewlis, missed, and had his stump knocked out of the vound. The bowlers continued to hold toe upper hand, Woolf! and Ambery being speedily dismissed by Thewlis, who had up to the dismissal of Ambery taken four wickets for eleven',runs.</p>
        <p>A useful stand was made by R H Voss and Brelsferd, the professional playing the bowling with confidence whilst the amateur made several lucky snicks which just escaped the fielders. With the total at 42 the Warrington captain banded the ball t W H Podmore, and success immediately met the change, for Voss tried to play one of the new bowler's slow ones to leg, missed, and was out. With the arrival of Church, Brelsford forced the pace, and Gibson was at once tried in place of "'limbs. Runs came at a fair rate until at 65, Church was caught at short leg. Brelsford was next out to a brilliant catch at mid-off, the retiring batsman having made 32 and with 86 runs on the board the innings closed. The fielding of the Warrington team was very close, 4rd iJa pri !c) allow a single eXtra,</p>
        <p>3 Callon and B Si!cock were the initial batsmEn for Warrington and they were opposed by E Wainwright , and Brelsford. Both batsmen, especially Callon, played 'the bowling with ease, until at 13 Callon was bowled in attempting to drive. E Cannor came next and quickly addeu coven, when he got his leg in front, making way for W H Podmore. The new comer speedily got the measure of the bowling, and a fine square leg hit off Wainwright, recording six, was deservedly applauded. Silcock continued his plodding career, but Podmore hit hard, and aster placing Wainwright again out oF the ground he was adjudged lbw at 47, his share being 20.</p>
        <p>Thewlis came next, and the:towling was freely hit. Boundary strokes were the order from the profeessional's bat, and his hitting all round the wicket was a treat to witness. The Rev. A. E. Marriott was tried in place of Brelsford, but this did not check the fast rate at which runs were being recorded, and the Bowdon aggregate was passed by Thewlis with a fine drive between cover and mid-off. In the following over Silcock returned one to Church,his 24 having been made in his usually careful style. Some vigorous hitting was now witnessed between Thewlis and G. Clarke, and a fine stroke by Thewlis off Marriott recorded the third six, and also sent up the century. Runs continued to come freely and Thewlis again hit a six, following which he was bowled when only one short of fifty. His runs were made in about forty minutes,and included in his faulth S 3 display were two sixes, four fours, and four threes. Briggs came next, but failed to score, and the stumps were then drawn, Clark'S;being not out 16. Warrington thus won easily.</p>
        <p>Ia striking contrast with the victory of the first team, the second team fared rather badly at home. For the Icss of only four wickets. the Bowdon second team scored 167 and then declared their innings closed. Bes- wick was the chief contributor, his score of 67 not out being a capital performance. The Warrington men batted indifferently and were only able to score 47 for the loss of five wickets. Time was then called and certainly saved Warrington from a heavy defeat.</p>
        <p>The Grappenhall team gave a capital performance at Arley. G3ing to the wickets first they compiled 119 runs. Arley on going to bat could make but little pro- gress against the bowling of Carter and Fenton and were all dismissed for 19 runs. Carter's bowling analysis was five wickets for two runs.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's journeyed to Appleton Ball on Saturday and returned yictcricus. The scorers were S. Paul's 26 Appleton, 17.</p>
        <p>Arpley Ist added another victory to their list of RUC- cesses on Saturday, this time at the expense of Walker's Brewery second. The representatives of King-Grog were dismissed for 46 runs. Arpley ran up this score for the loss of only one wicket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>BRAVE ACT REWARDED. A Latchford Bridgeman Receives a</head>
        <p>A BRAVE ACT REWARDED. A Latchford Bridgeman Receives a</p>
        <p>Certificate.</p>
        <p>An interesting event markel the conclusion of the business at Daresbury Pe:Ay Sessions on Tues- day, when the Chairman of the Bench (Mr. J. C. Parr), presented a Royal Humane Society's certifi- cate to Robert Thomas, a bridgaman in the employ of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, for res- cuing a drowning man from the Ship Canal on June 18th hit. The incident was recorded at the time and will be still fresh in the memory of some readers. On the date mentioned a man named John Moody, of Latchford, who has been pronounced since to be inane threw himself into the Ship Canal at Latchford. An alarm was raised,and Robert Thomas by means of a rope suspended from the swing bridge descended into the canal and suc- ceeded in throwing a life buoy to the drowning man. With assistance he was got out and after- wards removed to linutsford Asylum. The bravery of Thomas was brought to the notice of the Royal Humane Society and that society de- ci:Jel to present its certificate to him. Mr. T. C: Parr, in making the presentation, said he had a very pleasing duty to perform and that was to hand to Robert Thomas a certificate of the Royal Humane Society for saving life by his prompt and energetic action. Such deeds of prompt courage and action were very much to be commended, and won the admiration of everybody. The certificate he (the Chairman) had now the honour to present to him, would, he was sure,be a valuable possession, and would stamp him as a hero as long as he lived. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Thomas, in accepting the certificate, thanked the Chairman for the interest and kindness he had taken in the matter, and said he hoped he would always be a worthy possessor of the certificate. The Chairman: lam very pleased to see you none the worse for the act you performed.</p>
        <p>Plucky Conduct of a Police Constable, An exciting scene was witnessed in Napier-st. on !tionday evening shortly after six o'clock, when a horse attached to a lurry belonging to Messrs. Buckley, of Church-street, took fright and bolted down the street at a furious pace. Police Constable Taylor was at the bottom of the street going on duty. HJ ran out and sprang at the horse,eatehing it by the head, but was flung violently on the foot- path. He got up, however, and giving chase again caught the horse as it was turning into Ellesmere- street, and with great difficulty brought it to a standstill. Just prior to that the vehicle had been dashed violently against the wall and had knocked a quantity of bricks out. It seems that the horse had managed to get one of its legs over the shaft, and took fright before the driver could get it under control.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>UNCONQUERABLE.</head>
        <p>UNCONQUERABLE.</p>
        <p>A Plain Statement of Facts.</p>
        <p>Veno'a Seaweed Tonic is used in hospitals and by doc- tors themselves at home and abroad, because they recognise in it a scientific remedy possessing the greatest therapeutic value, and a quick cure for all ailments arising from a diseased condition of the stomach, liver. kidneys and blood. It possesses marvellous tonic 7nd strengthening power, is. icl. and 23. 9d. Sold by Bort's Limited,Knowles Warrington, Peake, Earlestown and Newton, Weston and Co., Runcorn, Foggitt Wi dnes and all Chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NEWTON AND DISTRICT AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>NEWTON AND DISTRICT AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>There was only a moderate attendance at the 15:h annual exhibltion of fruit, flowers vegetables, Szo., held on Saturday in a field adjoining the Newton Nurseries. There was an increase in both the number of entries and the number of indiOidual competitors. Aauual, Mr A R Theme, Mrs Smith (Bror kside), Messrs W Sephton, and J Randall and Sergeant-Major Strange (Ashton), carried off the bulk of the priws. The groups of plants this year were a decided improvement, the staging being effectively carried out. There were some remark- ably fine flowering and foliage plants exhibited. Mr. A. C. Leslie was a very successful ex- hibitor for the first time and Mr. G. Moon showed a number of good floral specimens. Mr. W. Sephton, who usually can more than hold his own in the vegetable department, was this year in the onion classes beaten by NT.r It Cowley, of Sutton. For an exhibition conducted on such modest lines the appear- ance was decidedly encouraging. The judges were— Mr J Leary. Aigburth ; Mr J Eaton,the Lawn, Aigbarth ; Mr G Clarke, the Nurseries, Lymm. and Mr H Jackson, the Gardens, Barkbill, Aigburtb. The Earlestown Prize Band played selections of music during the day, and also for dancing in the evening. The whole of the arrange- ments were satisfactorily carried out by an energetic committee, with Mr T Williams as secretary. The following is the prize list :</p>
      </div>
