      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLE PARADE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLE PARADE.</p>
        <p>Continued from page 3.</p>
        <p>THE CONCERT IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. J. FOIVIRAW presided over the proc,T3iogs in Parr Hall, and remarked that it was not his intention to say much. They were all exceedingly pleased that the weather had been so favourable for the procession, which had been a treat to the inhabitants of the town. It was certainly the finest procession of the kind which had ever been seen in Warrington, and he hopes the promoters would make arrangements for one to be held annually. (Applause). The object was a very worthy one, namely, to assist the fun ds of the Infirmary, which was a most useful local institution. In a tawn like Warrington, with such a large industrial population, it was a most valuable institution, and In earnestly hoped that the clmmittee would be able to band over to the treasurer a very handsome sum. (Hear, hear). An interesting programme of vocal and instrumental music was provided. Solos were well sung by Mr W H Ireland, Mr E Whitehouse, Miss Lena Dobson and Miss Suttpn. A.L.C.M., and they were deservedly applauded. A feature of the evening's proceedings was the humorous element. This was capably sustained by Mr Harry Gill, of Liverplol, and Mr J Emaon, n Warring'on, both of whern were encored. The Orpheus Glee Party, consisting of four local singers named Messrs J Davenport, Whitehouse, Scattergood, and Sherlock, mi de a very favourable impression. Responding to an encore they gave " Simple Simon" in such good style as to still further delight the audience. The instrumental portion of the programme was well maintained by Mr. R. H. Pearson (violin l and Miss M. Sutton, L.L.C.M. (Pianoforte). The violin solos of Mr. Pearson were given with his usual skill and were greatly appreciated. Mr. W. H. Pavt-m. ;Tue. Ric., was the aclompanist. THE JUDGES AND THE PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>Daring an interval Mr. W. H. STONIER (of Huyton), Van has had considerable experience in arranging cycle parade. raid he mart congratulete the people of War- rington un,n the great sulcoss of the afternoon's pro- Nrdings, He understood that it was one of the first cYcle paradea that had over been seriously attempted in War- rington, and certainly for a first parade it had g(ane off exceedingly well. There were very few gapa,and that spoke well for the work the marshals must have undertaken. It was very *diffieult to marshal a great crowd, and great credit was therefore due to the marshals for the manner in which the procession had been managed. (Hear, hear. and applause.) There was a " little" suggestion he should like to make. Ho had had great sxperience in parades all over the district, and he hopcd that in future the promoters would see their way to give a few more prizes for the collectors. P: would also he batter if the priz's were separated from each other—he meant that they should have pi izes for the lady collectors and prizes for the gentlemen collec- tors. Some little trouble had been experienced owing to the prizes not having been arranged as he had indicated. For instance, he noticed from the list that a lady was given a walking stink, or a pip'.—(laughter)—which was certainly not a very sc.itable present to make. (Renewed laughter). The CHAIRMAN. at a lat.r.r stage of the proceedings, announced that the prizes jast mentioned by Mr. Stonier would be changei.</p>
        <p>Desertion of Shop Premises.—Action in the County Court</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before His Honour Deputy-Judge L Morton-Brown, an action was heard in which Christian Bower, butcher, of Appleton sued Edwin Mounfield, of the Globe Inn, Peckford-street, Macclesfield for £36 19s 41 for goods sold, arrears of rent and damages for breach of covenant.—Mr.Forshaw,who appeared for the plaintiff, said the claim was made up by a quarter's rent for a shop, 5. Market-street. Warring- ton, £l3 ; goods sold and delivered, £lO 17.3 4d; and damages for desertion of the premises of which de- fendant was lessee,f.,l3 The defendant was the lessee of the shop of which the plaintiff was the owner. He b.came lessee under an indenture of lease dated June 30, 1899, with a yearly rent of £52 payable Quarterly. The lease was for a term of seven years Defendant deserted the premises on November 28th 1899, and plaintiff took possession but did not get a tenant before July of this year. Plaintiff, how- ever, only claimed a quarter's rent.—Defend ant said he disputed the £l3 rent and £4 of the bills for goods sold ani delivered.—His Honour said de- fendant had made himself liable for five years rent. Plaintiff was letting him off very reasonably.—John William Bower, son of the plaintiff, produced the books, showing that defendant owed £lO 19s 41 for goods.—His Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff with costs.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS 70 ALL, UNDER THE CLOCK.- rt</p>
