      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-08-31_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey School Board.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the ~chool Board was held at the school on Tuesday evening. There were present, Messrs. T Halton (chairman),Fulton Smith, WHOA, Perks, and Kilshaw; W D Jeans,jun.(clerk , and Mr U Clarke (headmaster). Mr Clarke reported upon the month as follows:—Week ending Aug 4th, on books 300,present 283,average 232 6,fees,los 10d; August 11th, on books 296, present 267, average 230.8, fees 93 2d; August 18-h, on books 295, present 270, average 236.2, fees 93 2d; Auguct 25th on books 293, present 273, average 2381, fees lOs 4d; total average 232, total fees £1 193 61. Two children were absent through fever. The attendance was at present very poor, being 78 per cent. After some discussion it was unanimously decided to have a night school during the coming winter. Mr Clarke said the school last winter was very successful. Last Week-Day Performance in the Park.</p>
        <p>About six or seven thousand people assembled in I the park on Wednesday evening to listen to the I Volunteer Band give the last week-day perfor- mance of the season. The excellent programme gone through was as follows :—March, "Esprit de Corps" (F Haines) : clarionet solo," tin Rove " (J Delposse), soloist, Lance-Corporal Hampson ; selec- tion from A Sullivan's Opera " Haddon Hall ;" selection, " lolanthe " (A Sullivan) ; and con- cluded with the descriptive war piece "The Relief" (Mason). To wards the middle of the piece the signal was given in the band stand by coloured fire, aud Mr William Parkin- son, who had charge of the fireworks, sent off a number of rockets. A number of men in the ser- vice of the Corparation,who had been posted in the adjoining bushes, also ignited a large quantity of coloured magnesium, Twenty volunteers, under the command of Colour-sergeants Spencer and Cliff and Sergeant Ryder, were also in attendance, and fired blank cartridges, each having 30 rounds. Mr Bonehill sent up two balloons, much to the delight of the youngsters. There was scarcely any wind at the time, with the result that the balloons went up in a perpendicular direction until lost to view. The proceedings concluded with the playing of the National Anthem. Mr J Richardson conducted the band.</p>
